// Generated by scripts/gen-l10n.mjs (gdetail n2, en) — 不要手改;改翻譯請改 scripts/out/l10n/en/ 再重 build。
window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N=window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N||{};window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]=Object.assign(window.GRAMMAR_DETAIL_L10N["en"]||{},{"n2-37":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜かねる」 is like what customer service says: <b>“I'm afraid we're unable to help with that.”</b> It's also like the official-letter phrase <b>“We regret that we cannot comply.”</b> Literally it means “I'd like to, but it's awkward to,” and in practice it's a <b>very polite refusal</b>, or a way of saying you're in a tough spot.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Polite refusal</span>Staff, customer service and companies use this most when speaking to the public. It softens a flat “no.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>返品はお受けいたしかねます。</span>(We're unable to accept returns.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その件についてはお答えしかねます。</span>(I'm afraid I can't answer that.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>申し訳ございませんが、私ではわかりかねます。</span>(I'm sorry, but I'm not able to say on this one.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't accept it inwardly</span>Your position or feelings won't let you nod along.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その意見には賛成しかねます。</span>(I can't bring myself to agree with that opinion.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't make up your mind</span><span class='gd-jp'>決めかねる</span> is a very common set phrase meaning “to hesitate, to be unable to decide.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>どちらにするか決めかねている。</span>(I still can't decide which one to pick.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in its ます form, drop 「ます」, then add 「かねる」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>答えます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>答えかねる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>わかります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>わかりかねる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しかねる</span></span><br>For extra politeness, swap する for the humble いたす: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>いたします</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>いたしかねます</span></span><br>In real life you'll hear the polite form <b>「〜かねます」</b> most.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't use it for physical inability.</b> “Too heavy to lift” is <span class='gd-jp'>重くて持てない</span>, not 重くて持ちかねる. かねる is about rules, position or feelings: “it's awkward” or “I'm unwilling.”<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>わかりかねます</span> has no negative in it, but it means “I don't know.” Don't hear it as “I understand.”<br>・<b>かねます and かねません mean opposite things.</b> <span class='gd-jp'>お答えしかねます</span> means “I can't answer,” while <span class='gd-jp'>起こりかねません</span> means “it could very well happen.” Only one ん separates them, so don't mix them up.<br>・It's very formal, so it sounds odd with friends. Between friends, <span class='gd-jp'>ちょっと無理</span> is enough.<br>・Learn these two fixed expressions separately: <span class='gd-jp'>見るに見かねて手伝った</span> (I couldn't just watch, so I helped) and <span class='gd-jp'>お待ちかねの新商品</span> (the new product everyone's been waiting for). Neither one is a refusal.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Say a customer asks for a return:<br><b>できない</b> = a direct “can't,” fairly blunt: <span class='gd-jp'>返品はできません。</span> (Returns aren't possible.)<br><b>かねる</b> = a polite, indirect refusal: <span class='gd-jp'>返品はお受けいたしかねます。</span> (We're unable to accept returns.)<br><b>がたい</b> isn't for refusing people. It describes something as “hard to ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>信じがたい話だ。</span> (That's a hard-to-believe story.)<br><b>かねない</b> = worry that something bad might happen: <span class='gd-jp'>このままでは事故になりかねない。</span> (If this goes on, it could well end in an accident.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: politely turning down someone's request → かねる. Describing something as hard to believe or accept → がたい. Worrying “this might happen” → かねない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The next lesson is 「〜かねない」. It looks almost identical but means the opposite, so it's a good one to learn together.</div>","n2-54":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜につれて」 means <b>“as ~, gradually ~.”</b> The first thing changes slowly, and the second thing changes slowly along with it. It's like “As the weather gets colder, more and more people order hot drinks”: two things changing together.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verb</span>Add につれて directly to the dictionary form (the base form you'd look up).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>時間が経つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>時間が経つにつれて</span></span> (as time passes)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>慣れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>慣れるにつれて</span></span> (as you get used to it)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun</span>Add につれて directly, but the noun itself must carry a sense of “change,” like <span class='gd-jp'>成長</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>進化</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>変化</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>高齢化</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>成長</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>成長につれて</span></span> (as someone grows)</div><div class='gd-sec'>In writing you'll often see the shorter 「<span class='gd-jp'>につれ</span>」. The meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Use it when both things change <b>little by little</b>. The second half often pairs with 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜てきた</span>」, 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ていく</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜くなる</span>」 to bring out the “gradually” feeling.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本の生活に慣れるにつれて、毎日が楽しくなってきた。</span>(As I got used to life in Japan, every day became more enjoyable.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年を取るにつれて、体力が落ちてきた。</span>(As I've gotten older, my stamina has declined.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもの成長につれて、家が狭く感じるようになった。</span>(As the kids grew, the house started to feel small.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't use it for things that happen all at once.</b> “Graduating,” “moving” and “getting married” are one-time events, so you can't say <span class='gd-jp'>卒業するにつれて</span>. The first part must be a change that unfolds gradually.<br>・<b>The second half can't be “I will ~” or “Please ~.”</b> It has to describe a change that follows naturally, not your own plans or a request. A sentence like “As your Japanese improves, let's chat more” can't use につれて.<br>・<b>You can't put just any noun in front.</b> <span class='gd-jp'>雨につれて</span> is wrong because “rain” itself isn't a change. Say <span class='gd-jp'>雨が強くなるにつれて</span> (as the rain gets heavier).<br>・English “as” and “along with” cover a lot of ground, but につれて only handles “both sides change gradually in step.” Don't use it every time you'd say “as.”</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に伴って</b>: very close in meaning, but more formal and common in news and announcements. The first part can also be a <b>one-time event</b>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>人口が増えるにつれて、ゴミの問題が深刻になった。</span>(As the population grew, the garbage problem became serious.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>人口が増えるに伴って、ゴミの問題が深刻になった。</span>(Same meaning, more formal.)<br>Both work. But with a one-time event like moving:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>引っ越しに伴って、住所が変わります。</span>(Your address will change due to the move.)<br>につれて can't be used here.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に応じて</b>: “depending on ~.” The focus is a correspondence, not change over time.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>年齢に応じて、料金が違います。</span>(The fee differs depending on age.)<br>Here, people of different ages pay different prices. It's not one person growing older and the price shifting along with them, so につれて doesn't fit.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can say “as ~, gradually ~,” use につれて. If it's more formal, or the first part is a one-time event, use に伴って. If it means “depending on ~,” use に応じて.</div>","n2-2":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Dictionary form + ばかりだ means <b>“nothing but more and more ~,” “only getting worse,” “going steadily downhill.”</b> Things keep moving in one direction with no sign of improvement or turning back, and the speaker usually sounds resigned or worried.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Getting worse and worse</span>The most common use. You'll hear it all the time when people complain about prices, the economy, health or workload.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>物価は上がるばかりだ。</span>(Prices only go up, never down.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父の病気は悪くなるばかりだった。</span>(My father's illness just got worse day by day.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Mid-sentence</span>Change it to ばかりで, then add another thing that makes the situation even more frustrating.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>仕事は増えるばかりで、給料は全然上がらない。</span>(The work only keeps piling up, and my pay doesn't go up at all.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Occasionally for good things</span>It's possible, but less common, and mostly describes an objective trend.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店の人気は高まるばかりだ。</span>(This shop just keeps getting more popular.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb dictionary form (the base form you'd look up in a dictionary) + ばかりだ. The verb in front must be a <b>verb of change</b>, like <span class='gd-jp'>増える</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>減る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>上がる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>広がる</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>悪化する</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>増える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>増えるばかりだ</span></span><br>Adjectives first take なる to mean “become ~”, and then you add ばかりだ:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>悪い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>悪くなる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>悪くなるばかりだ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>不便</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>不便になる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>不便になるばかりだ</span></span><br>For things in the past, use <b>ばかりだった</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・If the verb in front isn't a verb of change, the meaning shifts to “does nothing but ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>彼女はただ泣くばかりだった。</span>(She did nothing but cry.) This is not “more and more.”<br>・「<span class='gd-jp'>あとは</span>～ばかりだ」means “all that's left is ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>準備はできた。あとは出発するばかりだ。</span>(Everything's ready. All that's left is to leave.)<br>・Noun + ばかり means “nothing but ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>甘い物ばかり食べている。</span>(I eat nothing but sweets.) That's a different thing from the “more and more” meaning here.<br>・It's not usually used for your own progress. To say “my Japanese is getting better and better,” <span class='gd-jp'>日本語がどんどん上手になっている</span> sounds more natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>The same ばかり means completely different things depending on the form before it:</b></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に来たばかりだ。</span>(I just arrived in Japan: た-form + ばかり = “just did ~”)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日ゲームをしてばかりいる。</span>(He does nothing but play video games every day: て-form + ばかりいる = “keeps doing ~ all the time”)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>帰国してから、日本語は忘れるばかりだ。</span>(Since going back home, I just keep forgetting more and more Japanese: dictionary form + ばかりだ = “more and more ~”)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Compared with 「～つつある」:</b> Both describe change, but the tone differs.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>景気は回復しつつある。</span>(The economy is gradually recovering.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>景気は悪くなるばかりだ。</span>(The economy can only get worse and worse.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>つつある simply states, calmly, that “a change is in progress.” It's more formal and works for good or bad. ばかりだ stresses “it can't be stopped, it only goes one way,” with a sense of resignation.<br>There's also <span class='gd-jp'>～一方だ</span>, which means almost the same as ばかりだ and can often replace it: <span class='gd-jp'>人口は減る一方だ。</span>(The population only keeps shrinking.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: look at what comes before ばかり. た-form means “just did ~,” て-form means “keeps doing ~,” and dictionary form with a verb of change means “more and more ~.”</div>","n2-5":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>〜たとたん（に）means <b>“the moment I ~, suddenly ~.”</b> Think of a cat bolting out the instant you open the door, or getting dizzy the second you stand up. The first action has only just finished, and then <b>something unexpected pops up</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in the た-form (the same form as the past tense 「～た」) and add とたん. You can add or drop に, with no change in meaning.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>開ける</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>開けたとたん</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>出る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>出たとたん</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>飲む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>飲んだとたんに</span></span><br>The action in front is usually something that finishes in an instant, like opening a door, going out, standing up or seeing something.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Something suddenly happens outside</span>Other people, animals or the weather suddenly do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家を出たとたん、雨が降り出した。</span>(The moment I left the house, it started to rain.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>窓を開けたとたん、虫が入ってきた。</span>(The moment I opened the window, a bug flew in.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Body and feelings out of your control</span>It also works for reactions in your own body, as long as you didn't do them on purpose.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>立ち上がったとたん、めまいがした。</span>(The moment I stood up, I felt dizzy.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>母の声を聞いたとたん、涙が出てきた。</span>(The moment I heard my mother's voice, tears came to my eyes.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The form in front must be the た-form: say <span class='gd-jp'>開けたとたん</span>, never 「開けるとたん」.<br>・What follows is something that <b>has already happened and was unexpected</b>, so the sentence usually ends in the past tense.<br>・You can't follow it with will, invitations or commands, such as “I'll ~,” “let's ~” or “please ~.” 「家に着いたとたん、電話してください」is wrong.<br>・You also can't follow it with <b>an action you do on purpose</b>. 「家に帰ったとたん、シャワーを浴びた」sounds unnatural, because taking a shower is something you decided to do. In that case use <span class='gd-jp'>家に帰ってすぐ、シャワーを浴びた。</span>(As soon as I got home, I took a shower right away.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜次第</b> is also often translated as “as soon as ~,” but it works the opposite way. Take the same situation:<br><span class='gd-jp'>駅に着いたとたん、電車が行ってしまった。</span>(The moment I got to the station, the train left.)<br>→ It already happened and was unexpected, so use とたん.<br><span class='gd-jp'>駅に着き次第、連絡します。</span>(I'll contact you as soon as I get to the station.)<br>→ It hasn't happened yet and it's something <b>I intend to do</b>, so use 次第.<br>次第 attaches to the ます-form with ます removed (<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>着きます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>着き次第</span></span>), and it's often used in work and formal situations.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if what follows is something that pops up suddenly and you can't control it, use とたん. If it's something you'll go and do next, use 次第.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>とたん can also start a sentence in the form 「その途端」, meaning “at that very moment”:<br><span class='gd-jp'>ドアを開けた。その途端、猫が飛び出した。</span>(I opened the door. At that very moment, the cat dashed out.)</div>","n2-8":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜からには」means <b>\"since you've already ~, then you must ~\"</b> or <b>\"now that ~, you'd better ~\"</b>. First you state something that's already decided, already happened, or can't be undone. Then you say \"so I'm definitely going to…\". In English you'd say \"If you're going to do it, do it right.\" In Japanese that's: <span class='gd-jp'>やるからには、一番を目指す。</span> (Since I'm doing it, I'm aiming to be number one.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's most often used with verbs. What matters is whether you use the た-form or the dictionary form, because the meaning changes:<br><b>Something already done</b>: た-form + からには　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>引き受けた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>引き受けたからには</span></span> (since I've already taken it on)<br><b>Something you've decided to do but haven't done yet</b>: dictionary form + からには　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>留学する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>留学するからには</span></span> (since I'm going to study abroad)<br><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: add 「である」 in between　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>プロ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>プロであるからには</span></span> (since you're a professional)</div><div class='gd-h'>What comes after it</div><div class='gd-sec'>After からには, the sentence is always about \"so this is the attitude I should have.\" It's never just a statement that something happened. The four most common patterns are:</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Determination</span>I will definitely follow through.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>約束したからには、必ず守る。</span> (Since I made a promise, I'll definitely keep it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Obligation</span>Often paired with 「なければならない」 or 「べきだ」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試合に出るからには、毎日練習しなければならない。</span> (If you're going to compete, you have to practice every day.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking the other person</span>Often paired with 「てください」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に住むからには、ゴミの出し方はちゃんと覚えてください。</span> (If you're going to live in Japan, please learn properly how to take out the trash.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A natural expectation</span>Often paired with 「はずだ」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼がやると言ったからには、ちゃんとやるはずだ。</span> (Since he said he'd do it, he should do it properly.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'>These lines are almost set phrases, so memorize them whole:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>やるからには、本気でやろう。</span> (If we're doing it, let's do it seriously.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こうなったからには、もうやるしかない。</span> (Now that it's come to this, we have no choice but to do it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>自分で決めたからには、最後までがんばる。</span> (Since I decided this myself, I'll keep at it to the end.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What follows can't be just a fact about something that already happened.<br>×<span class='gd-jp'>日本に来たからには、寿司を食べた。</span><br>○<span class='gd-jp'>日本に来たからには、本場の寿司を食べたい。</span> (Since I've come to Japan, I want to eat authentic sushi.)<br>・Nouns can't attach directly: not プロからには, but <span class='gd-jp'>プロであるからには</span>.<br>・Don't mix up the た-form and the dictionary form: <span class='gd-jp'>結婚したからには</span> means \"now that we're married\", while <span class='gd-jp'>結婚するからには</span> means \"since we've decided to marry\".<br>・It carries a sense of \"I'm taking this on,\" so the tone is strong. If you're just giving a plain reason, like \"it rained, so I took an umbrella,\" use 「から」 instead of からには.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>からこそ</b> (n2-9) = <b>\"precisely because ~\"</b>. It stresses that this is the reason.<br><b>からには</b> = <b>\"since ~, then ~\"</b>. It focuses on what you should do next.<br>Both about a job you love:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>好きな仕事を選んだからには、簡単にはやめない。</span> (Since I chose a job I love, I won't quit easily.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>好きな仕事だからこそ、毎日がんばれる。</span> (It's precisely because it's a job I love that I can give it my best every day.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>上は</b> (n2-32) = almost the same meaning as からには, but stiffer and more formal, mostly used in writing or formal settings. The commonly heard 「以上（は）」 is also very close in meaning.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>引き受けたからには、最後までやります。</span> (Since I've taken it on, I'll see it through to the end: fine in everyday speech.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>引き受けた上は、最後までやり遂げる所存です。</span> (Having taken it on, I intend to carry it through to the end: formal settings.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use からには for \"since ~, then ~\"; use からこそ for \"precisely because ~\"; and for the same \"since\" in a more formal register, switch to 上は.</div>","n2-25":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜どころか」means <b>\"far from ~, it's actually…\"</b> or <b>\"never mind ~, not even…\"</b>. First you mention an A that everyone assumes, then you say the real situation is very far from A, often <b>the complete opposite</b>. For example, you expect a day off to be restful, but it turns out to be \"not restful at all, more tiring than work.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs: dictionary form + どころか　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>やせる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>やせるどころか</span></span><br>い-adjectives: attach directly　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安いどころか</span></span><br>な-adjectives and nouns: attach directly, <b>don't add だ</b>　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>簡単</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>簡単どころか</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>旅行</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>旅行どころか</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>The opposite instead</span>Not only did A not happen, things went the other way. Often followed by <span class='gd-jp'>逆に</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>かえって</span>, or <span class='gd-jp'>もっと</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ダイエットしているのに、やせるどころか太ってしまった。</span> (I'm on a diet, but far from losing weight, I actually gained weight.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>薬を飲んだのに、よくなるどころか、もっと悪くなった。</span> (I took medicine, but instead of getting better, I got worse.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Not even B, let alone A</span>A is the harder thing and B is the more basic thing. If you can't even do B, A is out of the question. Often followed by 「も」 or 「さえ」 + a negative.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しくて、旅行どころか、週末に休むこともできない。</span> (I'm so busy that, never mind traveling, I can't even rest on weekends.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は漢字どころか、ひらがなも読めない。</span> (He can't even read hiragana, let alone kanji.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Far more than A</span>The reality is much more extreme than A, in the same direction but going far beyond it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>1万円どころか、5万円もかかった。</span> (Far from 10,000 yen, it cost a whole 50,000.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>それどころか</span>Placed at the start of a sentence to respond to the other person: \"Not at all, in fact…\". Very common in conversation.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「迷惑だった？」「それどころか、すごく助かったよ。」</span> (\"Was I a bother?\" \"Not at all, you were a huge help.\")</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't add だ after nouns or な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>簡単どころか</span>, not 簡単だどころか.<br>・Don't flip the order in \"not even B, let alone A\": put the <b>harder</b> thing as A and the <b>more basic</b> thing as B. 「漢字どころか、ひらがなも読めない」 works, but reversing it as 「ひらがなどころか、漢字も読めない」 makes the meaning odd.<br>・When A and B are both good things and you just want to \"add one more,\" you can't use どころか. To say \"this restaurant is tasty and also cheap,\" you can't say 美味しいどころか安い; use <span class='gd-jp'>ばかりか</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>だけでなく</span> instead.<br>・どころか carries a feeling of surprise or being taken aback, so it suits unexpected results. Don't force it onto plain, matter-of-fact statements.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>All about a rainy day:<br><b>ばかりか</b> = A <b>holds true</b>, and B is added on top: <span class='gd-jp'>雨ばかりか、雷まで鳴りだした。</span> (Not only rain, but thunder started rumbling too.)<br><b>どころか</b> = A is <b>overturned</b> and the reality is the opposite: <span class='gd-jp'>雨がやむどころか、どんどん強くなっている。</span> (Far from stopping, the rain is getting heavier and heavier.)<br><b>どころじゃない</b> = right now you <b>have no room at all</b> for A: <span class='gd-jp'>雨がひどくて、花見どころじゃない。</span> (The rain is so bad that flower viewing is out of the question.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: \"not only A, but also B\" is ばかりか; \"far from A, it's the opposite\" is どころか; \"no time or mood for A right now\" is どころじゃない.</div>","n2-26":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ばかりか」 and 「〜ばかりでなく」 mean <b>\"not only ~, but also ~\"</b> or <b>\"not just ~, even ~\"</b>. You state one thing, then <b>add</b> a bigger one on top. In everyday speech people usually say 「〜だけじゃなくて」. ばかりか and ばかりでなく sound more formal and show up often in writing, news and speeches.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Piling on good things</span>One advantage after another.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は安いばかりでなく、味もいい。</span>(This shop is not only cheap, the food is good too.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その歌は子どもばかりか、大人にも人気がある。</span>(That song is popular not only with children but with adults too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Piling on bad things</span>Things were already bad, and then something even worse happened. ばかりか is especially common in this \"and on top of that...\" situation, with a hint of surprise or annoyance.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>遅刻したばかりか、資料も忘れてきた。</span>(He was not only late, he even forgot the documents.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は謝らないばかりか、文句まで言ってきた。</span>(He not only didn't apologize, he even complained to me.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half often pairs with <b>「も」「まで」「さえ」</b>, meaning \"even ~\". That makes the \"on top of that\" feeling clearer.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遅刻した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遅刻したばかりか</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安いばかりか</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add 「な」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利なばかりか</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly, no だ　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子ども</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子どもばかりか</span></span><br>(In more formal writing, な-adjectives and nouns can also connect with 「である」, for example <span class='gd-jp'>学生であるばかりでなく</span>.)<br>ばかりでなく connects in exactly the same way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この町は交通が便利なばかりか、家賃も安い。</span>(This town is not only convenient for transport, the rent is cheap too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't put だ after a noun: say <span class='gd-jp'>日本語ばかりか</span>, not 「日本語だばかりか」. Remember to add な after な-adjectives: <span class='gd-jp'>きれいなばかりか</span>.<br>・<b>Don't follow ばかりか with a request, command or decision</b>, like \"please do ~\", \"you must ~\" or \"I will ~\". In those cases use ばかりでなく (or だけでなく): <span class='gd-jp'>肉ばかりでなく、野菜も食べなさい。</span> (Don't eat only meat, eat vegetables too.) Saying 「肉ばかりか、野菜も食べなさい」 sounds unnatural.<br>・Don't confuse it with plain 「ばかり」: <span class='gd-jp'>日本語ばかり勉強している</span> means \"I study <b>only</b> Japanese\", while <span class='gd-jp'>日本語ばかりか英語も勉強している</span> means \"I study <b>not only</b> Japanese, but English too.\" One extra か changes the meaning a lot.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>どころか</b> is also often translated as \"not only ~\", but it works differently. The key is <b>whether the first thing is actually true</b>.<br><b>ばかりか</b>: the first part is true, and the second part adds more.<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼は英語ばかりか、フランス語も話せる。</span> (He speaks not only English but French too. He does speak English.)<br><b>どころか</b>: the first part is out of the question, and reality is even further off.<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼はフランス語どころか、英語も話せない。</span> (Never mind French, he can't even speak English. He can't speak French.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>やせるどころか、太ってしまった。</span> (Far from losing weight, I actually gained weight.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>のみならず</b> means about the same as ばかりか and ばかりでなく, but it is more bookish and stiff, and mostly appears in writing or formal settings.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if the first thing is true and you're adding another, use ばかりか or ばかりでなく. If the first thing is not true at all, or the result is the complete opposite, use どころか. If the second half asks someone to do something, choose ばかりでなく.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>These all belong to the same \"not only ~\" family. Look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜のみならず</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜はもちろん</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜上に</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜に限らず</span>, and compare how each one feels.</div>","n2-35":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ざるを得ない」 means <b>\"have no choice but to ~\"</b> or <b>\"can't help but ~\"</b>. You don't really want to, but given the situation there is <b>no other option</b>, so you have to do it. It's like saying in English, \"I had no choice, so I just gritted my teeth and went.\"</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's easier to remember if you break it apart: <span class='gd-jp'>ざる</span> is the classical Japanese \"not\", and <span class='gd-jp'>得ない</span> means \"can't\". Together that's \"<b>can't not do it</b>\", which equals \"have no choice but to\". <span class='gd-jp'>得ない</span> is read <b>えない</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Forced by circumstances</span>For some reason, you're forced to do something you'd rather not do.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台風が来るので、旅行を中止せざるを得ない。</span>(A typhoon is coming, so we have no choice but to cancel the trip.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人が足りないので、休みの日も出勤せざるを得なかった。</span>(We were short-staffed, so I had no choice but to go to work on my day off.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Have to admit / have to say</span>Often used with <span class='gd-jp'>認める</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>言う</span>, meaning \"I don't like it, but that's the fact.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の実力は認めざるを得ない。</span>(I have to admit his ability.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>残念ながら、この計画は失敗だったと言わざるを得ない。</span>(Unfortunately, I have to say this plan was a failure.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first check the verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its ない-form, <b>drop the ない</b>, then add ざるを得ない.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → drop ない → <span class='gd-jp'>行かざるを得ない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言わざるを得ない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>従う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>従わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>従わざるを得ない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>認める</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>認めない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>認めざるを得ない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>諦める</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>諦めざるを得ない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (both are exceptions, just memorize them)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>せざるを得ない</span></span> (not しざる)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ざるを得ない</span> (こざるをえない)</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>The past tense is <span class='gd-jp'>得なかった</span>, and the polite form is <span class='gd-jp'>得ません</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>する</span> becomes <b>せざる</b>. 「しざるを得ない」 is wrong. \"Noun + する\" verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>中止する</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>出勤する</span> work the same way: <span class='gd-jp'>中止せざるを得ない</span>.<br>・For verbs ending in う, like <span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>従う</span>, the ない-form uses <b>わ</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>言わない</span>. So it's <span class='gd-jp'>言わざる</span>, not 「言あざる」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>得ない</span> is read <b>えない</b>. Don't read it as とくない or うない.<br>・This is a fairly formal, written-style expression, common in news, reports and formal situations. When chatting with friends, a phrase like <span class='gd-jp'>行くしかない</span> sounds more natural for \"have no choice but to\".<br>・It carries the flavor of \"I don't actually want to\", so don't use it for things you really want to do.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>なければならない</b> = <b>should/must do</b> because of rules or reason, not necessarily with any emotion:<br><span class='gd-jp'>明日は会議だから、早く行かなければならない。</span> (There's a meeting tomorrow, so I have to go early.)<br><b>ざるを得ない</b> = you didn't want to, but <b>the situation leaves you no choice</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>熱があるが、代わりの人がいないので、行かざるを得ない。</span> (I have a fever, but there's nobody to cover for me, so I have no choice but to go.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ずにはいられない</b> (it looks similar, with ず/ざる in both) = you <b>can't hold back, can't help it</b>. The emotion just bursts out by itself:<br><span class='gd-jp'>部長の冗談がおもしろくて、笑わずにはいられなかった。</span> (The manager's joke was so funny I couldn't help laughing.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>部長の冗談はつまらなかったが、笑わざるを得なかった。</span> (The manager's joke was boring, but I had no choice but to laugh along.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for a plain \"should do\", use なければならない. For \"don't want to, but no choice\", use ざるを得ない. For \"can't help it\", use ずにはいられない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>It often appears with \"since ~\" clauses: <span class='gd-jp'>上司の判断である以上、従わざるを得ない。</span> (Since it's the boss's decision, we have no choice but to follow.) Next, try <span class='gd-jp'>〜上は</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜からには</span>, which also express \"since ~, then ~\".</div>","n2-6":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜次第」means <b>\"as soon as ~, right away ~\"</b>. Think of what customer service says: \"We'll let you know as soon as your order arrives\" or \"We'll tell you the moment the results are out.\" It's the <b>formal, slightly businesslike</b> \"as soon as ~\".</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: change to the ます form, drop ます, and attach 次第 directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>届きます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>届き次第</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>終わります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>終わり次第</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>わかります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>わかり次第</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見つけます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見つけ次第</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Action nouns that can take する</b> (like <span class='gd-jp'>到着する</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>決定する</span>): usually you drop する and attach 次第 straight to the noun.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>到着する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>到着次第</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>決定する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>決定次第</span></span><br>(You'll also see <span class='gd-jp'>到着し次第</span>. It means the same thing.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>I'll do it right away</span>The first part is something that hasn't happened yet but you're waiting for. The second part is what the speaker plans to do.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>荷物が届き次第、ご連絡いたします。</span>(We'll contact you as soon as the package arrives.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>詳しいことがわかり次第、お知らせします。</span>(We'll let you know as soon as the details are confirmed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Asking the other person to act right away</span>The second part can also be a request or an instruction.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>到着次第、お電話ください。</span>(Please call me as soon as you arrive.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>準備ができ次第、始めてください。</span>(Please start as soon as you're ready.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>You can't use it for things that already happened</b>: \"It started raining as soon as I got to the station\" can't use 次第. What follows 次第 is something you're going to do next, never a past-tense event.<br>・What follows has to be something <b>a person can choose to do</b> (contact, leave, ask someone to do something). It can't be an uncontrollable result like \"it started raining\" or \"I was startled.\"<br>・It doesn't attach to the dictionary form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>届き次第</span>, not 届く次第.<br>・The tone is very formal and sounds stiff with friends. Among friends, just say <span class='gd-jp'>着いたらすぐ連絡するね</span> (I'll message you as soon as I get there).</div><div class='gd-h'>Another meaning: depends on ~</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun + 次第だ／次第で</span>「次第」has another common use meaning \"it depends on ~\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>成功するかどうかは、君の努力次第だ。</span>(Whether you succeed depends on your effort.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天気次第で、予定が変わるかもしれません。</span>(Depending on the weather, the plans might change.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>This is a different thing from the \"as soon as ~\" use above. After an <b>ordinary noun</b> (effort, weather, money, etc.), you'll usually see <b>次第だ／次第で</b>, meaning \"depends on.\" After an <b>action noun that can take する</b>, like <span class='gd-jp'>到着</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>決定</span>, followed by what you'll do, it means \"as soon as ~.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from a similar-looking pattern</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both can mean \"as soon as I get home ~\":<br><b>次第</b> = hasn't happened yet, and <b>I'll act right away when it does</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>家に着き次第、電話します。</span> (I'll call you as soon as I get home.)<br><b>たとたん</b> = already happened, and <b>something unexpected pops up at that very moment</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>家に着いたとたん、雨が降り出した。</span> (The moment I got home, it suddenly started to rain.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if it hasn't happened yet and you'll act right once it's done, use 次第. If it already happened and something unexpected occurred in that instant, use たとたん.</div>","n2-11":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ものの」means <b>\"I did ~, but...\"</b> or <b>\"although ~, ...\"</b>. For example: \"I bought the ticket, but in the end I couldn't go.\" The first part is true and you admit it, but what follows doesn't go the way you'd expect. It often carries a feeling of \"ah, it didn't work out.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Did it, but nothing came of it</span>This is the most common use. You did something, but things didn't continue as expected.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>チケットを買ったものの、結局行けなかった。</span>(I bought the ticket, but in the end I couldn't go.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ジムに入会したものの、忙しくて一度しか行っていない。</span>(I joined a gym, but I've been too busy and have only gone once.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>やると言ったものの、正直自信がない。</span>(I said I'd do it, but honestly I'm not confident.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A gap between the two parts</span>The first part makes you expect a certain result, but the second part isn't that. The second part doesn't have to be something bad.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このアパートは駅から遠いものの、家賃が安い。</span>(This apartment is far from the station, but the rent is cheap.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>買った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>買ったものの</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遠い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遠いものの</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: change 「だ」 to 「な」, or to 「である」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>簡単だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>簡単なものの</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>簡単だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>簡単であるものの</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: change 「だ」 to 「である」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生であるものの</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>The form you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>とはいうものの</span>\"That said, but...\" It can come at the end of a clause, or stand alone at the start of a sentence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に十年住んでいるとはいうものの、敬語はまだ苦手だ。</span>(Even though I've lived in Japan for ten years, I'm still not good at honorific speech.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ダイエット中だ。とはいうものの、甘いものはやめられない。</span>(I'm on a diet. That said, I can't give up sweets.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns and な-adjectives can't take 「だ」 directly: it's not <span class='gd-jp'>静かだものの</span> but <span class='gd-jp'>静かなものの</span>, and nouns use <span class='gd-jp'>学生であるものの</span>.<br>・The second half <b>can't contain your own intention, a command, or a request</b>. You can't say <span class='gd-jp'>高いものの、買おう</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>高いものの、買ってください</span>. Use 「けど」 there: <span class='gd-jp'>高いけど、買おう</span>.<br>・The first half has to be something that's <b>already a fact</b>. You can't use it for things that haven't happened or are just hypothetical.<br>・The tone is fairly formal and shows up in writing, news, and formal settings. When chatting with friends, <span class='gd-jp'>買ったけど、行けなかった</span> sounds more natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar-looking patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ものの</b> = \"I did ~, but...\" The focus is <b>acknowledging the first part, but the result falls short</b>, often with a note of regret:<br><span class='gd-jp'>チケットを買ったものの、行けなかった。</span> (I bought the ticket, but couldn't go.)<br><b>にもかかわらず</b> = \"despite ~, ...\" The focus is <b>a sharp contrast between the two parts</b>, often with surprise or dissatisfaction, and it's often used about other people:<br><span class='gd-jp'>チケットを買ったにもかかわらず、彼は来なかった。</span> (Even though he'd bought a ticket, he didn't come.)<br><b>ものなら</b> only looks similar and means something totally different: \"if it were possible to ~\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>行けるものなら、行きたかった。</span> (If I could have gone, I would have wanted to.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for \"I did ~, but...\" use ものの. For \"despite ~\" use にもかかわらず. If you see \"if it were possible to ~,\" that's ものなら, not a contrast.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Other patterns with a similar \"although\" meaning are <span class='gd-jp'>とはいえ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>つつも</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>ながらも</span>. It helps to compare them side by side.</div>","n2-15":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にしても」is the English <b>\"even if ~, still ~\"</b> or <b>\"even granting that ~\"</b>. You give way first and accept the other person's reason (fine, you're busy), but your conclusion doesn't change (you could at least have been in touch). It often carries a complaining or critical tone: \"that still doesn't excuse it.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly to the plain form.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忙しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忙しいにしても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知らなかった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知らなかったにしても</span></span><br><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: in the present tense, <b>don't add だ</b>; attach directly. In the past tense, use だった.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>冗談</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>冗談にしても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大変</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大変にしても</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>冗談だった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>冗談だったにしても</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Patterns you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Even if ~</span>You concede a point, then give your own opinion or complaint. It's often preceded by 「いくら」(no matter how much).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いくら忙しいにしても、返事ぐらいはしてほしい。</span>(No matter how busy you are, I'd like at least a reply.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冗談にしても、言っていいことと悪いことがある。</span>(Even if it's a joke, there are things you can say and things you can't.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No matter ~</span>Put a question word like 「何、どこ、誰」in front.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何をするにしても、健康が一番だ。</span>(Whatever you do, health comes first.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>AにしてもBにしても</span>\"Whether A or B,\" the result is the same. A and B are usually things of the same kind, or two opposite choices.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>行くにしても行かないにしても、早めに連絡してください。</span>(Whether you go or not, please let me know early.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車にしてもバスにしても、朝は混む。</span>(Whether it's the train or the bus, it's crowded in the morning.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>それにしても</span>This has become a fixed phrase meaning \"even so\" or \"still, though.\" It's super common in conversation.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「バスが遅れたんだって。」「それにしても遅すぎるよ。」</span>(\"I heard the bus was late.\" \"Even so, that's way too late.\")</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't add だ after a noun: say <span class='gd-jp'>冗談にしても</span>, not 「冗談だにしても」.<br>・The second half is usually <b>your own view</b>: criticism, dissatisfaction, advice or a judgment. It pairs often with 「はずだ」「べきだ」「〜てほしい」「〜ない」.<br>・English \"even if\" can be quite neutral, but 「にしても」often has a flavor of \"I can understand that, but it's still not OK.\" Said straight to a superior, it can sound like you're blaming them.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にしては</b> = \"for ~, surprisingly ~.\" The result <b>differs from what you'd expect</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>初めてにしては上手だね。</span>(For a first time, that's really good!)<br><b>にしても</b> = \"even if it's ~, still ~.\" The reason <b>can't be used as an excuse</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>初めてにしても、これはひどすぎる。</span>(Even if it's your first time, this is way too bad.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にしろ・にせよ</b>: nearly identical in meaning and usage to にしても, just more formal. <b>にしても</b> is the most colloquial; <b>にせよ</b> leans toward writing and formal settings.<br><span class='gd-jp'>いずれにしても、明日また連絡します。</span>／<span class='gd-jp'>いずれにせよ、明日また連絡します。</span>(In any case, I'll contact you again tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: to say \"even if ~,\" use にしても; to say \"for ~, surprisingly,\" use にしては. One syllable apart, very different meanings.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>More formal synonyms: <span class='gd-jp'>〜にしろ・〜にせよ</span>.</div>","n2-18":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ものなら」has two meanings, and you can tell them apart by <b>the form of the verb in front</b>:<br>・After the <b>potential form</b> (<span class='gd-jp'>行ける</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>戻れる</span>): <b>\"if only I could ~\"</b> (but it's hard). It's like sighing, \"If only I could do it over.\"<br>・After the <b>volitional form</b> (〜よう, 〜おう): <b>\"if you should ~, you're in trouble.\"</b> It's like warning someone, \"Don't you dare be even a minute late.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Usage ①: potential form + ものなら</b> (the potential form is the \"can do ~\" form)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>戻る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>戻れるものなら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べられるものなら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>できるものなら</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Usage ②: volitional form + ものなら</b> (the volitional form is the 〜よう／〜おう form, so you often see 「〜ようものなら」)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言おうものなら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遅れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遅れようものなら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>失敗する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>失敗しようものなら</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>In speech, ものなら often becomes <b>もんなら</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>戻れるもんなら戻りたいよ。</span>(If I could go back, I would, believe me.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A wistful wish</span>Usage ①. You know it's hard, almost impossible, but you can't help saying \"if only it were possible...\" It's usually followed by a wish like 「〜たい」「〜てあげたい」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>戻れるものなら、高校時代に戻りたい。</span>(If I could, I'd go back to my high school days.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>代われるものなら、代わってあげたい。</span>(If only I could, I'd take your place.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>休めるものなら、一週間ぐらい休みたい。</span>(If I could take time off, I'd love a week.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A challenge</span>Also usage ①, but followed by the imperative 「〜てみろ」. It means \"go ahead and try, if you can,\" assuming the other person can't, and deliberately provoking them.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>やれるものなら、やってみろ。</span>(If you think you can, go ahead and try.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A warning</span>Usage ②. It means \"if you do ~, you'll be in big trouble.\" It's often used with 「<span class='gd-jp'>少しでも</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>一度でも</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>一分でも</span>」, exaggerating how terrible the consequences would be.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>うちの父は、門限を一分でも過ぎようものなら、すごく怒る。</span>(My dad gets furious if I'm even one minute past curfew.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長の前でそんなことを言おうものなら、大変なことになるよ。</span>(If you said something like that in front of the department head, it would turn into a huge mess.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Usage ① must use the <b>potential form</b>: say <span class='gd-jp'>行けるものなら</span>, not 行くものなら.<br>・Usage ② uses the <b>volitional form</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>言おうものなら</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>遅れようものなら</span>. Don't mix the two patterns.<br>・Usage ① carries a sense of \"hard to do.\" If something is actually easy, it sounds odd, and a plain 「〜たら」 is enough.<br>・Usage ① is usually followed by a wish (〜たい) or a command (〜てみろ), rarely by a plain statement.<br>・「<span class='gd-jp'>やれるものならやってみろ</span>」 is a provocation. Say it to a senior or a boss and you're guaranteed a fight.<br>・Both usages can be translated as \"if ~\" in English, but the meanings are very different: the potential form is \"I'd love to, but I can't,\" and the volitional form is \"don't you dare.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Even for the same \"I want to go,\" 〜たら and 〜ものなら show different levels of confidence on your part:<br><b>〜たら</b> = a simple hypothetical that could really happen: <span class='gd-jp'>時間があったら、行きたい。</span>(If I have time, I'd like to go.)<br><b>〜ものなら</b> = you feel it's nearly impossible and are just saying it: <span class='gd-jp'>行けるものなら、行きたい。</span>(If only I could go... but I probably can't.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if you feel there's a chance, use たら. If you feel there's little hope and you're just saying it, use potential form + ものなら. To warn someone \"if you dare, you're done,\" use 〜ようものなら.</div>","n2-19":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ないことには」 means <b>“unless you ~ first, there's no way to ~”</b>. The first thing is a <b>threshold</b>: if you don't cross it, the rest is off the table. Think of “How would you know if it's good if you've never tried it?” or “Without the boss's approval, nothing can happen.”</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>You can't know until you try</span>The most common use. The first part is almost always 「〜てみない」 (“don't try”).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>食べてみないことには、おいしいかどうかわからない。</span>(Unless you try it, you can't tell if it's good.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>やってみないことには、何とも言えません。</span>(Until I actually try it, I really can't say.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Prerequisite</span>If one thing isn't done first, the next step can't go ahead.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>部長がOKしないことには、この企画は進められない。</span>(Unless the department head signs off, we can't move this project forward.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the verb's ない form and add 「ことには」 directly. First, a quick review of how to make the ない form:<br><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: change the last sound to the matching 「あ」-row sound, then add ない.<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → change く to か → <span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会わない</span></span> (う becomes わ)<br><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: drop 「る」 and add ない.<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見ない</span></span><br><b>Group 3</b>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span> (こない)</span><br>Put together: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>確認しない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>確認しないことには</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>These are the endings that most often follow in the second half. Learn them as set phrases: <span class='gd-jp'>わからない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>何とも言えない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>始まらない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>決められない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>話にならない</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The second half must be a result like <b>“can't / don't know / doesn't work out”</b>. It can't be a positive outcome: <span class='gd-jp'>勉強しないことには、合格できる</span> is wrong.<br>・The second half also can't be a command, request, or invitation: <span class='gd-jp'>確認しないことには、電話してください</span> sounds unnatural.<br>・Don't mix it up with 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ないことはない</span>」, which means “it's not that I don't ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>行けないことはない</span> (it's not that I can't go). One small difference in wording, a completely different meaning.<br>・The tone is fairly formal and common at work and in writing. With friends, people more often say 「<span class='gd-jp'>やってみないとわからない</span>」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ないことには</b> = the thing hasn't happened yet, and you stress <b>“nothing works until you do it first.”</b> The second half is always negative:<br><span class='gd-jp'>住んでみないことには、その町のよさはわからない。</span> (Unless you actually live there, you won't know how good the town is.)<br><b>てはじめて</b> = the thing has already been done, and <b>“only after doing it did I realize for the first time.”</b> The second half is what you found out or understood (good or bad):<br><span class='gd-jp'>住んでみてはじめて、その町のよさがわかった。</span> (Only after living there did I understand how good the town is.)<br><b>ないと／なければ</b> = the most general “if you don't ~.” The second half is less restricted and can be a warning, a result, a judgment, and so on (but after 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜ないと</span>」 you can't use sentences of will or request, like 「～ましょう」 or 「～てください」):<br><span class='gd-jp'>予約しないと、入れないよ。</span> (If you don't book, you won't get in.)<br>You can also say 「<span class='gd-jp'>予約しないことには、入れない</span>」, but it sounds harder, as if stressing that “booking is an absolute requirement.”</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to say “nothing can happen before you do it,” use ないことには. For “you only know once you've done it,” use てはじめて. For an ordinary “if you don't ~,” ないと／なければ is enough.</div>","n2-29":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にすぎない」 means <b>“it's merely ~” or “it's nothing more than ~”</b>. The speaker deliberately <b>plays something down</b>, as if to say “that's all it is, no big deal.”</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly, with no だ in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>噂</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>噂にすぎない</span></span> (it's just a rumor)<br><b>Verbs</b>: attach to the plain form (dictionary form or た form).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>従った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>従ったにすぎない</span></span> (he was merely following orders)<br>For the past tense, use <b>にすぎなかった</b>; for a polite style, use <b>にすぎません</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Downplaying something</span>Stresses “that's all it is, don't read too much into it.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>それはただの噂にすぎない。</span>(That's nothing more than a rumor.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は命令に従ったにすぎない。</span>(He was merely following orders.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Small numbers</span>Often appears with 「わずか」 (“a mere”) to emphasize how small the number is.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>参加者はわずか5人にすぎなかった。</span>(There were only five participants, a mere five.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Modesty when praised</span>Said about yourself, it becomes “I didn't do anything special.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>私は当たり前のことをしたにすぎません。</span>(I only did what anyone should do.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's often preceded by <span class='gd-jp'>ただの</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>単なる</span> (“mere”), <span class='gd-jp'>ほんの</span> (“just a little”), or <span class='gd-jp'>わずか</span>, which strengthen the “only” feeling.<br>When the sentence isn't finished and continues, it becomes <b>にすぎず</b>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは一つの例にすぎず、問題はほかにもたくさんある。</span>(This is only one example, and there are plenty of other problems.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't add だ</b> after a noun: write <span class='gd-jp'>噂にすぎない</span>, not 「噂だにすぎない」.<br>・It's a different thing from 「〜すぎる」 (“too ~”). <span class='gd-jp'>高すぎない</span> means “not too expensive,” while <span class='gd-jp'>にすぎない</span> always has 「に」 in front and means “nothing more than.”<br>・The tone is fairly formal and very common in news, reports, and speeches. With friends, <span class='gd-jp'>ただの噂だよ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>〜だけだよ</span> sounds more natural.<br>・It carries a judgment of “not worth mentioning.” Said about someone else, as in <span class='gd-jp'>彼はまだ新入社員にすぎない</span>, it can sound a bit dismissive. Said about yourself, it comes across as modest.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にすぎない</b> = <b>plays something down</b>: “it's merely ~”:<br><span class='gd-jp'>今回の成功は偶然にすぎない。</span> (This success is nothing more than luck.)<br><b>にほかならない</b> = <b>states something flatly</b>: “it is precisely ~, nothing else”:<br><span class='gd-jp'>今回の成功はみんなの努力の結果にほかならない。</span> (This success is nothing other than the result of everyone's hard work.)<br>Both look like 「〜にＸない」, and both can end up translated with something like “nothing but ~” in English, so they're easy to mix up. But the tone is actually the opposite: the first belittles, the second affirms strongly.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to say “it's nothing special,” use にすぎない. To say “it's exactly that and nothing else,” use にほかならない.</div>","n2-41":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に違いない」 means <b>\"must be ~\"</b> or <b>\"has to be ~\"</b>. You see a clue and draw a confident conclusion, like a detective: \"The light is on, so someone <b>must</b> be inside.\" It expresses your <b>inference</b>, not a confirmed fact.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Concluding from a clue</span>There is evidence or a situation in front of you that makes you very sure.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電気がついている。誰かいるに違いない。</span> (The light is on. Someone must be there.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>顔がそっくりだ。あの二人は兄弟に違いない。</span> (Their faces look exactly alike. Those two must be brothers.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Inferring about the past</span>Put the verb in the past form to guess \"it must have been ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女はもう知っていたに違いない。</span> (She must have known already.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It often appears together with <span class='gd-jp'>きっと</span>, which makes the tone even more certain: <span class='gd-jp'>きっと疲れているに違いない。</span> (He must be exhausted.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly. Present, past and negative all work.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来るに違いない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来た</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来たに違いない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来ない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ないに違いない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いに違いない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>な-adjectives and nouns</b>: drop 「だ」 first, then attach.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大変だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大変に違いない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>犯人だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>犯人に違いない</span></span><br>In more formal writing you will also see <span class='gd-jp'>犯人であるに違いない</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't keep 「だ」 after a な-adjective or noun: <span class='gd-jp'>大変だに違いない</span> is wrong.<br>・English \"must\" can also express obligation (\"You must come!\"), but 「に違いない」 only means \"I'm confident this is the case.\" You can't use it to tell someone to do something.<br>・Don't use it for things you know for certain yourself. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>私は学生に違いない</span> about your own status or feelings sounds like you're guessing about yourself, which is strange.<br>・It has a slightly written, novel-like feel. When chatting with friends, Japanese people more often say <span class='gd-jp'>きっと〜だよ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>〜に決まってる</span>.<br>・The polite form is <span class='gd-jp'>〜に違いありません</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>はず</b> = based on known facts or arrangements, \"it should be ~\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>昨日送ったから、今日届くはずだ。</span> (I sent it yesterday, so it should arrive today.)<br><b>に違いない</b> = you see a clue and reason your way to a conclusion:<br><span class='gd-jp'>窓が割れている。泥棒が入ったに違いない。</span> (The window is broken. A burglar must have broken in.)<br>Swapping in はず here would sound unnatural, because you aren't calculating from a known arrangement. You're making a judgment from what you see in front of you. Also, はず can be followed by \"but it turned out otherwise\": <span class='gd-jp'>来るはずなのに来ない。</span> (He's supposed to come, but he hasn't.) に違いない has no such use.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に決まっている</b> = \"of course it is ~!\" It's more subjective and colloquial, and often carries emotion:<br><span class='gd-jp'>そんな話、嘘に決まってるよ。</span> (A story like that? Of course it's a lie!)<br><b>に相違ない</b> = same meaning as に違いない, but stiffer and more formal, mostly found in writing.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use 「はず」 when you reason from logic or arrangements; use 「に違いない」 when you reach a conclusion from a clue; use 「に決まってる」 for an emotional \"of course it is!\" in conversation.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜に決まっている</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜に相違ない</span>, which also express confidence, and sort out the differences in tone and situation.</div>","n2-43":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に相違ない」 is the <b>formal-suit version</b> of 「〜に違いない」. It means \"must be ~\" or \"surely is ~.\" You feel very sure, but it is <b>still an inference</b>. The tone is like \"undoubtedly\" or \"it must be the case that…\" in a report or newspaper commentary. You wouldn't say it in everyday chat.</div><div class='gd-sec'>First, the word itself: <span class='gd-jp'>相違</span> is read <b>そうい</b> and means \"difference, discrepancy.\" So <span class='gd-jp'>相違ない</span> literally means \"there is no discrepancy,\" that is, \"it can't be otherwise.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs and い-adjectives: attach directly to the plain form (the form without ます or です).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知っている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知っているに相違ない</span></span> (must know)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高かった</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高かったに相違ない</span></span> (must have been expensive)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Nouns and な-adjectives: <b>don't add だ</b>, just attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>彼</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>彼に相違ない</span></span> (must be him)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>有名</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>有名に相違ない</span></span> (must be famous)<br>For the past, use <span class='gd-jp'>だった</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>彼だったに相違ない</span> (it must have been him)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Written inference</span>In reports, papers, newspaper commentary and detective novels, to draw a conclusion from clues.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>犯人はこの近くに住んでいるに相違ない。</span> (The culprit must live near here.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この結果は全員の努力の賜物に相違ない。</span> (This result must be the fruit of everyone's effort.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Polite version</span>Change it to <span class='gd-jp'>〜に相違ありません</span> to state your inference in a formal setting.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>犯人はあの男に相違ありません。</span> (The culprit must be that man.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Confirming facts: testimony and documents</span>This type is <b>not an inference</b>. When giving testimony, answering the police or filling out documents, <span class='gd-jp'>相違ありません</span> is a set phrase meaning \"that is correct, there is no mistake,\" about something you already know yourself.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これは私の財布に相違ありません。</span> (This is indeed my wallet.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>上記のとおり相違ありません。</span> (The above is true and correct.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second sentence is often printed at the bottom of application forms and written affirmations. There is no に before 相違 because it means \"there is no discrepancy with what is written above.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After nouns and な-adjectives, <b>don't add だ</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>彼に相違ない</span>, not 彼だに相違ない.<br>・The 「相」 in <span class='gd-jp'>相違</span> is read <b>そう</b>, and the whole word is read そうい.<br>・Saying <span class='gd-jp'>あの店、おいしいに相違ない</span> to a friend would sound odd, like you're acting in a detective drama. In casual speech, use <span class='gd-jp'>おいしいに違いない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>きっとおいしいよ</span>.<br>・When used as an inference, the core is a confident guess. You wouldn't use 「〜に相違ない」 to guess at something you confirmed with your own eyes or already knew. The exception is <span class='gd-jp'>相違ありません</span> as a set phrase for confirming or certifying (the testimony and documents type above).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: he succeeded. Why?<br><b>に違いない</b> = the general \"it must be ~,\" usable in both speech and writing:<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼が成功したのは、努力したからに違いない。</span> (The reason he succeeded must be that he worked hard.)<br><b>に相違ない</b> = same meaning, but stiffer and more formal, found almost only in writing:<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼が成功したのは、努力したからに相違ない。</span> (The reason he succeeded is undoubtedly that he worked hard.)<br><b>に決まっている</b> = colloquial and subjective, \"of course that's why,\" often with a tone that brooks no argument:<br><span class='gd-jp'>努力したからに決まってるよ。</span> (Of course it's because he worked hard!)<br><b>にほかならない</b> = not an inference but a <b>firm assertion</b> by the speaker, \"nothing other than ~,\" used to explain a cause or essence:<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼の成功は、努力の結果にほかならない。</span> (His success is nothing other than the result of hard work.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use に違いない for a general inference; use に相違ない for the same inference when writing a report or essay; use に決まっている for \"of course!\" with friends; use にほかならない to stress \"it's precisely this, nothing else.\"</div>","n2-50":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に伴って」 is the formal <b>“along with ~” / “as ~ (changes)”</b> you see in news reports and official notices. One thing happens or changes, and something else happens or changes with it. A notice like “Due to the office relocation, our phone number will change” is exactly where Japanese reaches for it.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Changing together</span>When the first thing changes, the second changes with it. It is often used for gradual changes such as population, the economy, or temperature.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年を取るに伴って、体力が落ちてきた。</span>(As I've gotten older, my stamina has declined.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>気温の上昇に伴い、電気の使用量が増えている。</span>(As temperatures rise, electricity use keeps increasing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Bringing another event with it</span>The first part is a one-time event, and something else happens as a result or side effect. This is extremely common in notices and news.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>地震に伴い、津波が発生した。</span>(The earthquake was accompanied by a tsunami.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>事務所の移転に伴い、電話番号が変わります。</span>(Due to the office relocation, our phone number will change.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>工事に伴って、この道は来週から通れなくなります。</span>(Because of construction, this road will be closed from next week.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Noun + に伴って</b>: The most common pattern. The noun is usually one that expresses change or an event, like “increase, development, spread, relocation”.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>人口の増加</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>人口の増加に伴って</span></span><br><b>Verb dictionary form + に伴って</b>, or with の added: <b>のに伴って</b>. You'll see both.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>人口が増える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>人口が増えるに伴って</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>年を取る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>年を取るのに伴って</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に伴って</b> and <b>に伴い</b> mean the same thing, but <span class='gd-jp'>に伴い</span> is more formal and is the favorite in news headlines and notices.<br>To connect directly to a noun, change it to <b>に伴う + noun</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>台風に伴う大雨</span> (heavy rain accompanying a typhoon)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The kanji 伴 is read <b>とも</b> here, as in <b>ともなって</b>. Don't read it as ばん.<br>・It's very formal, the language of news, reports and notices. If you tell a friend “it got cold, so everyone started wearing coats”, using it would sound like reading a press release. In casual speech, <span class='gd-jp'>〜につれて</span> or even <span class='gd-jp'>〜と</span> sounds more natural.<br>・With nouns, the noun before it usually takes の: it's <span class='gd-jp'>経済の発展に伴って</span>, not 経済が発展に伴って.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>につれて</b> also means “as ~”, but it only covers <b>“one side changes little by little, and the other side changes little by little along with it”</b>.<br>Both work with the same topic, just with a different tone:<br><span class='gd-jp'>人口が増えるにつれて、町がにぎやかになった。</span>(As the population grew, the town became livelier. Explanation or conversation both work.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>人口の増加に伴って、住宅不足が深刻化した。</span>(With the population increase, the housing shortage became serious. News or report tone.)<br>But if the first part is a <b>one-time event</b>, only に伴って works:<br>○<span class='gd-jp'>地震に伴い、津波が発生した。</span><br>×地震につれて、津波が発生した。(An earthquake isn't a “gradual change”, so につれて can't be used.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for things that happen once, like an earthquake, a move or construction, use only に伴って. When both sides are changing gradually, either works. Casual talk leans toward につれて; news and notices lean toward に伴って.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another pattern about things linking together is <span class='gd-jp'>〜に応じて</span> (adjusting according to ~), as in <span class='gd-jp'>人数に応じて、部屋を選べます。</span> (You can choose a room according to the number of people.) Its focus is “responding to different situations”, not “changing along with something”, so it's a good one to memorize as a contrast with に伴って.</div>","n2-57":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>Think of 「〜まい」 as the <b>negative counterpart</b> of the volitional form 「〜よう」, and a stiff, written-style one at that. <span class='gd-jp'>行こう</span> means “I'll go”, so <span class='gd-jp'>行くまい</span> means “I will never go (again)”. It has a second meaning, “probably won't ~”, much like the literary English “I shall never…” and “surely it won't…”.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Resolving not to</span>You make up your mind: “I will never ~ again”. It often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>二度と</span> (never again), and is often followed by <span class='gd-jp'>と思う</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>と心に誓う</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>と決める</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの店には二度と行くまい。</span>(I will never go to that shop again.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>二度とあのようなミスは犯すまいと心に誓った。</span>(I vowed to myself never to make a mistake like that again.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Guessing it won't</span>It means the same as <span class='gd-jp'>ないだろう</span>, used to guess that someone or something “probably won't ~”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は今さら戻ってくるまい。</span>(At this point, he probably won't come back.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんなことはあるまい。</span>(Surely nothing like that could happen.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>How to tell which one it is: if the subject is “I” and it's about resolve, it's the first type. If the subject is someone else or something else, it's the second.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>: Add まい directly to the dictionary form.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くまい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>乗る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>乗るまい</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>あるまい</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>: Drop 「る」 and add まい, or keep it and add まい to the dictionary form. Both are correct.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べまい</span></span>／<span class='gd-jp'>食べるまい</span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (する・来る)</b>: There are several forms, and you should recognize them all.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>するまい</span></span>／<span class='gd-jp'>すまい</span> (some people also say <span class='gd-jp'>しまい</span>)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来るまい</span></span>／<span class='gd-jp'>来まい</span><br>「来まい」 is traditionally read こまい, but きまい is also common today. You'll see both readings.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・With Group 1 verbs, まい attaches to the <b>dictionary form</b>, not to something that looks like the ない form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>行くまい</span>, not 「行かまい」, and certainly not 「行かないまい」.<br>・まい is itself negative, and there is no affirmative version. To say “I'll do it”, use the volitional form <span class='gd-jp'>行こう</span>.<br>・It <b>can't be used to tell someone else not to do something</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>あなたは行くまい</span> isn't “don't go”; it sounds like “you probably won't go”. To tell someone not to go, say <span class='gd-jp'>行かないで</span>.<br>・When 「〜まい」 ends a sentence to express resolve, the person resolving is the speaker.<br>・It's very written and stiff. Saying <span class='gd-jp'>もう行くまい</span> to a friend would sound like novel narration. In speech, <span class='gd-jp'>もう二度と行かない</span> is enough.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: guessing whether he'll come tomorrow.<br><b>ないだろう</b>: The general form, fine in both speech and writing.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は明日来ないだろう。</span>(He probably won't come tomorrow.)<br><b>まい</b>: The same meaning, but written and stiff, and the tone stays reserved: “probably”.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は明日来るまい。</span>(He surely won't come tomorrow.)<br><b>っこない</b>: Colloquial and very firm: “no way”.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>あんなに怒っていたんだから、来っこないよ。</span>(He was that angry, so there's no way he'll come.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: in everyday speech use ないだろう; for a reserved “probably not” in writing use まい; for a flat “no way” in casual talk use っこない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>まい often shows up in two set phrases:<br>「〜うが〜まいが」 means “whether or not you do ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>行こうが行くまいが、私の自由だ。</span> (Whether I go or not is my choice.)<br>「〜ではあるまいし」 means “it's not like ~”: <span class='gd-jp'>子どもじゃあるまいし、一人で行けるよ。</span> (It's not like I'm a child; I can go by myself.)</div>","n2-62":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に越したことはない」 means <b>\"it's best if you can ~\"</b> or <b>\"nothing beats ~\"</b>. It states something everyone agrees with, like saying \"well, obviously.\" The tone is mild, not a command.</div><div class='gd-sec'>Literally, <span class='gd-jp'>越す</span> means \"to exceed,\" so the whole phrase says \"there's nothing better than this.\" That's how it comes to mean \"this is best.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs: dictionary form or ない form + に越したことはない<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>確認する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>確認するに越したことはない</span></span> (it's best to double-check)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>使わない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>使わないに越したことはない</span></span> (it's best not to use it if you can)<br>い-adjectives: attach directly<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安いに越したことはない</span></span><br>な-adjectives and nouns: attach directly, or add である (more formal)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安全</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安全に越したことはない</span></span>／<span class='gd-jp'>安全であるに越したことはない</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Stating common sense</span>Used on its own, it means \"you can't go wrong doing this.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>準備は早いに越したことはない。</span> (Of course, the earlier you prepare, the better.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>薬は飲まないに越したことはない。</span> (It's best not to take medicine if you can avoid it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Followed by けど・が</span>This is the most common use: first you admit \"it's best if you can,\" then you say \"but in reality…\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>経験はあるに越したことはないが、なくても大丈夫です。</span> (Experience is ideal, but it's fine if you don't have any.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>値段は安いに越したことはないけど、品質も大事だよ。</span> (Cheaper is always better, but quality matters too, you know.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Job ads often use the first kind of phrasing. It means \"not required, but a plus if you have it.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Write <b>越した</b>, not 越す: <span class='gd-jp'>越すことはない</span> is wrong.<br>・Use the <b>dictionary form</b> before it, not the た form: <span class='gd-jp'>早く行ったに越したことはない</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>早く行くに越したことはない</span>.<br>・Don't see only the 「ことはない」 at the end and assume it means \"no need.\" The meaning is quite different (see the comparison below).<br>・It means \"great if you can,\" <b>not \"you must.\"</b> You wouldn't use it to firmly demand something from someone.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: \"Is there a hurry?\"<br><b>ことはない</b> = no need to: <span class='gd-jp'>そんなに急ぐことはない。</span> (There's no need to rush that much.)<br><b>に越したことはない</b> = it's best if you can: <span class='gd-jp'>急ぐに越したことはない。</span> (Of course, the faster the better.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: \"Leaving early.\"<br><b>ほうがいい</b> = gives the other person direct advice: <span class='gd-jp'>早く出たほうがいいよ。</span> (You'd better leave early.)<br><b>に越したことはない</b> = states a general truth, softer and leaves room: <span class='gd-jp'>早く出るに越したことはない。</span> (Leaving early is always best.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another one with a similar feel: <b>に限る</b> = \"~ is the best, go for it,\" which is your own strong recommendation: <span class='gd-jp'>暑い日は冷たいビールに限る。</span> (On a hot day, nothing beats a cold beer.) に越したことはない is common sense everyone agrees with, and the tone is calmer.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: when you see 「に越した」, think \"it doesn't get better than this.\" Only 「ことはない」 on its own means \"no need.\" To advise someone directly, use 「ほうがいい」.</div>","n2-1":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜つつある」 means <b>\"is gradually ~ing\"</b> or <b>\"is increasingly ~\"</b>. It describes a <b>change that is happening and hasn't finished yet</b>, like the news saying \"temperatures keep rising\" or \"the economy is gradually recovering.\" The tone is fairly formal, and you'll mostly see it in news, reports, and articles.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Change in progress</span>Something is slowly shifting in one direction, and it's still shifting now.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>景気は少しずつ回復しつつある。</span> (The economy is slowly recovering.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この町の人口は減りつつある。</span> (This town's population is gradually shrinking.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本でもキャッシュレス決済が広がりつつある。</span> (In Japan too, cashless payment is becoming more widespread.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Heading step by step toward a result</span>With verbs like \"disappear\" or \"approach,\" which describe changes that complete in a moment, it means \"is moving step by step toward that result, but hasn't reached it yet.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台風が日本に近づきつつある。</span> (The typhoon is gradually approaching Japan.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>伝統的な祭りが消えつつある。</span> (Traditional festivals are gradually disappearing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Past tense</span>Change it to 「つつあった」 to say something was in the middle of changing at that time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>当時、この町は大きく変わりつつあった。</span> (At that time, this town was undergoing big changes.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the verb in ます form, then <b>drop ます</b> and add 「つつある」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>増えます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>増えつつある</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>進みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>進みつつある</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>回復します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>回復しつつある</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>失われます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>失われつつある</span></span><br>To be more polite, say 「つつあります」: <span class='gd-jp'>景気は回復しつつあります。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches to the ます stem, not the dictionary form: say <span class='gd-jp'>増えつつある</span>, not 「増えるつつある」; say <span class='gd-jp'>回復しつつある</span>, not 「回復するつつある」.<br>・The ending is <b>ある</b>, not いる.<br>・It only works with verbs that describe change, such as <span class='gd-jp'>増える</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>減る</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>変わる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>広がる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>進む</span>, and 「〜化する」. Ordinary actions can't use it: \"I'm eating\" is <span class='gd-jp'>ご飯を食べている</span>, not 「ご飯を食べつつある」.<br>・It's very bookish. If you tell a friend \"it's been getting colder lately,\" the natural way is <span class='gd-jp'>最近、だんだん寒くなってきたね。</span> Saying 「寒くなりつつある」 would sound like a news broadcast.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜ている</b>: with verbs like \"disappear,\" it means the change is <b>already complete</b>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>その技術はもう失われている。</span> (That technology has already been lost.)<br><b>〜つつある</b>: means <b>still changing, not finished yet</b>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>その技術は失われつつある。</span> (That technology is gradually being lost.)<br>With verbs like \"increase\" and \"decrease,\" <span class='gd-jp'>人口が減っている</span> can also mean \"is decreasing,\" and the meaning is very close. The difference is that つつある puts more emphasis on the ongoing process of change, and it's more formal.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜ばかりだ</b> (after the dictionary form): things only move in one direction, almost always getting worse, with a tone of worry or complaint.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>景気は悪くなるばかりだ。</span> (The economy just keeps getting worse.)<br><b>〜つつある</b>: simply describes a change, either for the better or for the worse.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>景気は回復しつつある。</span> (The economy is gradually recovering.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜つつ（も）</b>: with no ある after it, the meaning is completely different: \"while ~ing\" or \"even though ~.\"<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>体に悪いと知りつつ、タバコがやめられない。</span> (Even though I know it's bad for my health, I can't quit smoking.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: when you see 「つつ＋ある」, think \"is gradually ~ing.\" For \"has already become ~,\" use ている. For \"only getting worse and worse,\" use ばかりだ. If there's only 「つつ」 with no ある, it means \"while ~ing / even though ~.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Since this is also about change, a good next step is 「〜につれて」 (as ~), for example <span class='gd-jp'>年をとるにつれて、体力が落ちてきた。</span> (As I've gotten older, my stamina has declined.)</div>","n2-3":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AからBにかけて」 means <b>\"from A through B, roughly\"</b> or <b>\"across this area / this stretch of time\"</b>. The key word is <b>\"roughly\"</b>: neither the start nor the end is a precise line. It's a fuzzy range. A weather forecast says \"rain tonight into tomorrow.\" It doesn't say what minute it starts or stops, and Japanese uses にかけて for exactly that.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Time range</span>Something happens within a rough period of time. It might continue the whole time, or it might come and go.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今夜から明日の朝にかけて、強い雨が降るでしょう。</span> (Heavy rain is expected from tonight into tomorrow morning.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この辺りは、年末から正月にかけてとても混む。</span> (This area gets very crowded from the end of the year through New Year's.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Place range</span>A whole area stretching from one place to another. Very common in weather reports and news.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>九州から四国にかけて、大雨に注意してください。</span> (From Kyushu to Shikoku, please watch out for heavy rain.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Body part</span>You'll also hear this in daily life, and it's especially handy at the doctor's when describing where it hurts.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>首から肩にかけてが痛いんです。</span> (It hurts from my neck down to my shoulders.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Basic pattern: noun + から + noun + にかけて<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>3月から4月</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>3月から4月にかけて</span></span><br><br>You can sometimes drop the 「から」 part and just say \"toward ~ / around ~\":</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>週末にかけて、天気が崩れるでしょう。</span> (The weather will turn bad toward the weekend.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>When it comes before a noun, it becomes <b>にかけての</b>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>春から夏にかけての季節が一番好きだ。</span> (My favorite time of year is from spring into summer.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・English \"from A to B\" covers everything, but Japanese splits it up. When the time is <b>precise</b>, like working hours or business hours, use <b>から～まで</b>.<br>　×<span class='gd-jp'>9時から6時にかけて働きます。</span><br>　○<span class='gd-jp'>9時から6時まで働きます。</span><br>・Only a <b>noun</b> can go before it, and it has to be something you can draw a range around: time, place, body part. Verbs don't work, and neither do people: for \"from Mr. A to Mr. B\" you don't use にかけて.<br>・Don't forget 「の」 before a following noun: it's <span class='gd-jp'>夏にかけての旅行</span>, not 夏にかけて旅行.<br>・The tone is written and formal, most common in news, weather reports and explanatory writing. You'll hear it in everyday talk too, but for times, spoken Japanese more often just uses から～まで.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>から～まで</b> = the start and end are <b>clear and exact</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>会議は1時から3時までです。</span> (The meeting is from one to three.)<br><b>にかけて</b> = <b>roughly</b> within this range: <span class='gd-jp'>午後から夕方にかけて雨が降る。</span> (It will rain from the afternoon into the evening.)<br><b>にわたって</b> = stresses that <b>the whole span is covered, on a large scale</b>. It often follows words of length or size like 「3日間」「10年」「全国」: <span class='gd-jp'>雨が3日間にわたって降り続いた。</span> (The rain kept falling for three full days.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>One more: <b>にかけては</b> has an extra 「は」 and means something completely different: \"when it comes to ~\". <span class='gd-jp'>料理の腕にかけては、彼に勝てる人はいない。</span> (When it comes to cooking skill, nobody can beat him.) If you see the 「は」 and the sentence goes on to praise someone's ability, that's this one.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: exact times use まで, rough ranges use にかけて, and \"the whole long stretch\" uses にわたって. If there's an extra 「は」 and the sentence is about ability, it's にかけては.</div>","n2-4":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜末（に）」 means <b>\"after going through a lot of ~, in the end…\"</b>. Before it comes a process that took time and effort (long discussions, long agonizing, long hard work), and after it comes <b>the result you finally arrived at</b>. It's like saying, \"We argued all night and in the end decided to go to Japan.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun</span>noun + の + 末（に）<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>議論</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>議論の末に</span></span> (after discussion)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>苦労</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>苦労の末に</span></span> (after a lot of hardship)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Verb</span>verb た-form + 末（に）<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>悩む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>悩んだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>悩んだ末に</span></span> (after agonizing for a long time)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>考える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>考えた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>考えた末に</span></span> (after thinking it over for a long time)</div><div class='gd-sec'>You can drop 「に」 with no change in meaning. Before a noun, use 「末の」: <span class='gd-jp'>悩んだ末の決断</span> (a decision made after long agonizing).</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's often followed by 「<span class='gd-jp'>ようやく</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>やっと</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>ついに</span>」, stressing \"finally.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>長い話し合いの末、やっと契約が決まった。</span> (After long negotiations, the contract was finally settled.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何度も迷った末に、この部屋に決めた。</span> (After going back and forth many times, I finally chose this room.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The result doesn't have to be good. <b>Bad outcomes work too</b>, since 「末」 just states objectively how things ended up.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>長い闘病の末に亡くなった。</span> (After a long battle with illness, he passed away.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>悩んだ末、進学を諦めた。</span> (After agonizing for a long time, I gave up on further study.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Verbs must be in the <b>た-form</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>悩んだ末</span>, not 「悩む末」.<br>・Here 末 is read <b>すえ</b>, not まつ.<br>・What comes before it has to be \"a process,\" something that takes time like discussing, striving, agonizing or negotiating. A bare length of time doesn't work: 「3時間の末」 sounds unnatural, so say <span class='gd-jp'>3時間の議論の末</span>.<br>・「time noun + の末」 is a different meaning, \"the end of ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>来月の末に引っ越す</span> means \"I'm moving at the end of next month,\" not \"after going through next month.\"<br>・What follows is <b>a result that has already come out</b>, usually in the past tense (決めた、決まった、出た). You can't follow it with \"let's ~\" or \"please ~\".<br>・The tone is fairly formal and common in news, reports and formal explanations. With friends, people more often say 「<span class='gd-jp'>結局</span>」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>The same-level 「<b>〜あげく（に）</b>」 attaches the same way (た-form / noun + の) and also means \"after going through a lot of ~, in the end…\". The difference is <b>the speaker's feeling</b>.<br><b>末</b> = objectively states the final result. Good, bad or neutral all work:<br><span class='gd-jp'>さんざん迷った末、何も買わなかった。</span> (After a lot of hesitating, I ended up buying nothing.)<br><b>あげく</b> = carries dissatisfaction or resignation, with the feeling of \"after all that, you won't believe what happened…\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>さんざん迷ったあげく、何も買わなかった。</span> (After all that hesitating, I ended up buying nothing!)<br>So when the result is good, as in <span class='gd-jp'>努力の末に合格した</span> (after hard work, I passed), you normally wouldn't swap in あげく.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to simply report the result, use 末. If you're complaining or feeling resigned, as in \"after all that, it came to this,\" use あげく.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, try <span class='gd-jp'>〜あげく（に）</span>. Practicing the two together makes the difference in tone easier to catch.</div>","n2-7":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜て以来」 means <b>\"ever since ~, and it has stayed that way\"</b>. Put a past event in front as the <b>starting point</b>, and the second half describes <b>the state that has continued from then until now</b>. Think of sentences like \"Ever since I moved, I've been running every morning.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verb て-form + <span class='gd-jp'>以来</span> (read いらい). You already know the て-form, so just attach it:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来て以来</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会って以来</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>卒業する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>卒業して以来</span></span><br>The passive works too: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>導入される</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>導入されて以来</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A state that continues up to now</span>It often pairs with 「ている」 and 「ずっと」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に来て以来、ずっとこのアパートに住んでいる。</span> (Ever since I came to Japan, I've lived in this apartment the whole time.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもが生まれて以来、ゆっくり映画を見る時間がない。</span> (Ever since our child was born, I haven't had time to watch a movie in peace.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Not once since then…</span>It often pairs with 「一度も」 and 「〜ていない」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼とけんかして以来、一度も話していない。</span> (Ever since I had a fight with him, we haven't spoken even once.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Became something since then</span>With 「〜ようになった」, it means a change happened and has lasted until now.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>留学して以来、字幕なしでドラマが見られるようになった。</span> (Ever since I studied abroad, I've become able to watch dramas without subtitles.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches to the <b>て-form</b>, not the た-form: say <span class='gd-jp'>卒業して以来</span>, not 「卒業した以来」.<br>・The second half <b>can't be a one-time event</b>. \"Since coming to Japan, I went to Kyoto once\" can't use 以来: ×日本に来て以来、一度京都へ行った。 For a simple \"after that, I did something,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>日本に来てから、京都へ行った。</span><br>・The first half must be something that <b>has already happened</b>. You can't use it for future plans.<br>・The starting point is usually some time ago. \"Ever since I just finished lunch\" sounds odd in English, and it's just as odd in Japanese.<br>・It sounds fairly formal and bookish. In casual conversation, many people say <span class='gd-jp'>〜てから、ずっと</span> instead.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same event, moving house:<br><b>てから</b> = just the order of events, \"did A, then B\":<br><span class='gd-jp'>引っ越してから、ジョギングを始めた。</span> (After I moved, I started jogging.)<br><b>て以来</b> = it has been this way <b>from then until now</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>引っ越して以来、毎朝ジョギングしている。</span> (Ever since I moved, I've been jogging every morning.)<br><b>をきっかけに</b> = stresses that the event was the <b>trigger</b> that got things started:<br><span class='gd-jp'>引っ越しをきっかけに、ジョギングを始めた。</span> (Moving house was the trigger; I started jogging.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can add \"and it's still like that today\" to the second half, use て以来. If it's just \"and then,\" use てから. If you mean \"that event is why I started,\" use をきっかけに.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>以来</span> can also follow a time noun directly, or you can use <span class='gd-jp'>それ以来</span> (since then) to pick up the previous sentence:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>去年の春以来、彼から連絡がない。</span> (I haven't heard from him since last spring.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一度飲みすぎて倒れた。それ以来、お酒は飲んでいない。</span> (I once drank too much and collapsed. I haven't had a drop since then.)</div>","n2-9":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜からこそ」 means <b>\"precisely because ~, (that's why) ~\"</b>. The ordinary 「から」 just states a reason. Add 「こそ」 and it's like highlighting the reason with a marker: <b>this is the reason, and nothing else</b>. It's often used when going against the usual way of thinking: most people would feel A makes you not want to do something, but you do it precisely because of A.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>The \"plain form\" is the form without です・ます.<br><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>思う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>思うからこそ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>頑張った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>頑張ったからこそ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忙しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忙しいからこそ</span></span><br><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: add 「だ」 in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>家族</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>家族だからこそ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>大切</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>大切だからこそ</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Stressing the real reason</span>You're telling the listener: this is exactly why I act this way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あなたのことを思うからこそ、厳しく言うんです。</span> (It's precisely because I care about you that I'm being so harsh.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>毎日練習したからこそ、本番で落ち着いていられた。</span> (It's precisely because I practiced every day that I could stay calm on the day.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Against the usual expectation</span>Normally you'd expect the opposite reaction, but you say \"it's exactly because of this that I ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しいからこそ、休みの日を大切にしている。</span> (It's precisely because I'm busy that I treasure my days off.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>失敗したからこそ、わかったこともある。</span> (There are things I only understood because I failed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>だからこそ</span>Placed at the start of a sentence, it picks up what the previous sentence said and means \"and that's exactly why.\" This usage is very common.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この仕事は大変です。だからこそ、やりがいがあります。</span> (This job is tough. And that's exactly why it's rewarding.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half often ends in 「<span class='gd-jp'>のだ</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>んです</span>」. This adds an explanatory tone, as in \"the fact is…\", which goes well with からこそ.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't forget 「だ」 after nouns and な-adjectives: it's <span class='gd-jp'>家族だからこそ</span>, not 「家族からこそ」.<br>・Only 「から」 works here, not 「ので」: there's no 「ので＋こそ」 pattern.<br>・Don't use it for ordinary, negative, trivial reasons. For \"I overslept, so I was late,\" just say <span class='gd-jp'>寝坊したから遅刻した</span>. Saying 「寝坊したからこそ遅刻した」 would sound very odd, as if oversleeping were some grand reason.<br>・In English you'd say \"It's precisely because … <b>that</b> …\", but the second half of the Japanese sentence needs no separate word for that \"that.\" こそ already carries the emphasis.<br>・This phrase is emotionally heavy, so it shows up more in sincere talk, speeches and essays. Using it constantly for small everyday matters sounds dramatic.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>から</b> = simply giving a reason.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>心配だから、電話した。</span> (I was worried, so I called.)<br><b>からこそ</b> = stresses \"this is the reason,\" often used when the other person has misunderstood, or when you want to make a strong point.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>心配だからこそ、何度も電話したんです。</span> (It's precisely because I was worried that I called you again and again.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>からには</b> = \"now that ~, then ~,\" followed by a resolution or something you should do.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>留学するからには、しっかり勉強したい。</span> (Now that I'm going to study abroad, I want to study seriously.)<br><b>からこそ</b> = \"precisely because ~,\" followed by a result or action that came from it.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>留学したからこそ、台湾の良さに気づいた。</span> (It's precisely because I studied abroad that I realized how good Taiwan is.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if \"now that ~, I'll ~\" fits, use からには. If \"precisely because ~, ~\" fits, use からこそ. If it's just an ordinary \"because ~, so ~,\" plain から is enough.</div>","n2-10":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にもかかわらず」means <b>\"in spite of ~\" / \"even though ~, still ~\"</b>. You state a fact first, then follow it with a result that is <b>the opposite of what you'd expect</b>. It's very close to 「のに」, but it's more <b>formal and objective</b>. You'll see it a lot in news, reports, speeches and business settings, and rarely in casual chat with friends.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Unexpected result</span>Things should have gone one way, but they didn't.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何度も確認したにもかかわらず、同じミスをしてしまった。</span> (Even though I checked several times, I still made the same mistake.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨にもかかわらず、たくさんの人が集まった。</span> (Despite the rain, a lot of people gathered.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing the present situation</span>The second half doesn't have to be past tense. It can describe something that's still going on.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>反対が多いにもかかわらず、政府は計画を進めている。</span> (Despite strong opposition, the government is pushing ahead with the plan.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼はお金持ちであるにもかかわらず、質素な生活をしている。</span> (Although he's wealthy, he lives a simple life.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Thanks in formal settings</span>A set phrase you'll hear all the time in speeches and business emails.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本日はお忙しいにもかかわらず、お集まりいただきありがとうございます。</span> (Thank you all for coming today despite your busy schedules.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the dictionary form, or the た-form or ない-form).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>努力した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>努力したにもかかわらず</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寒い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寒いにもかかわらず</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: don't add 「だ」. Either attach directly, or add 「である」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>雨</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>雨にもかかわらず</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生であるにもかかわらず</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>不便</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>不便にもかかわらず</span></span> (you can also say <span class='gd-jp'>不便であるにもかかわらず</span>)<br>For the past, 「だった」 works fine: <span class='gd-jp'>学生だったにもかかわらず</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't add だ</b> after a noun: say <span class='gd-jp'>雨にもかかわらず</span>, not 「雨だにもかかわらず」.<br>・The second half states a <b>fact</b> (something that has happened or is still going on). You can't follow it with will, invitations, commands or requests, like \"let's ~\", \"please ~\" or \"I'll ~\". To say \"Even if it rains, let's go out,\" use ても: <span class='gd-jp'>雨でも出かけよう。</span><br>・The first half must also be a <b>fact</b>. English \"even if\" is often hypothetical (it hasn't happened yet). For that, use 「ても」, not にもかかわらず.<br>・It's very formal. If you're complaining to a friend, \"I told you already and you still won't listen,\" のに sounds much more natural. にもかかわらず would sound like you're reading out a press release.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>のに</b> = carries <b>your own feelings</b> (dissatisfaction, regret, surprise). It's very common in speech, and you can even leave it hanging at the end of a sentence.<br><b>にもかかわらず</b> = a more <b>calm, objective</b> statement. It's formal and leans toward written language.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何度も説明したのに、全然わかってくれない！</span> (I've explained it so many times, and you still don't get it!)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何度も説明したにもかかわらず、理解してもらえなかった。</span> (Despite explaining it several times, I couldn't get them to understand.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Trailing off mid-sentence, as in <span class='gd-jp'>せっかく作ったのに。</span> (And I went to the trouble of making it...), only works with のに. You can't do it with にもかかわらず.</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another look-alike that's very easy to misread is <b>にかかわらず</b>, without the 「も」. It means something completely different: <b>\"regardless of ~\"</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年齢にかかわらず、誰でも参加できます。</span> (Regardless of age, anyone can take part.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天気にかかわらず、試合は行います。</span> (The match will go ahead regardless of the weather.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to complain or show emotion, use のに. To say \"even though ~, still ~\" in a formal, objective way, use にもかかわらず. When you see 「にかかわらず」, first check for 「も」. With も it means \"even though\"; without も it means \"regardless of\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>At the start of a sentence it becomes 「<span class='gd-jp'>それにもかかわらず</span>」, meaning \"despite that\" or \"even so\", and it picks up the previous sentence: <span class='gd-jp'>何度も注意された。それにもかかわらず、彼は遅刻を続けた。</span> (He was warned many times. Even so, he kept showing up late.)<br>Other N2 patterns meaning \"although ~, but ~\" are <span class='gd-jp'>ものの</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>とはいえ</span>. Try studying them side by side.</div>","n2-12":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とはいえ」means <b>\"even though ~, still …\" / \"that may be true, but …\"</b>. You first admit a fact, then add: \"But it's not as simple as you might think.\"<br>Take \"Even though it's spring, the mornings are still cold.\" You admit it's spring, but the impression that \"spring = warm\" isn't entirely true.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>\"Even though it's ~\"</span>The first half is a fact that gives people a certain impression, and the second half says the reality is different from that impression.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>春とはいえ、朝晩はまだ寒い。</span> (Even though it's spring, mornings and evenings are still cold.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に5年住んでいるとはいえ、敬語はまだ苦手だ。</span> (Even though I've lived in Japan for five years, I'm still not good at keigo.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>安いとはいえ、毎日買うと結構な金額になる。</span> (Cheap as it is, buying it every day adds up to quite a lot.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At the start of a sentence</span>On its own at the start of a sentence, it means \"having said that\" and picks up the previous sentence.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>仕事は大変だ。とはいえ、辞めるつもりはない。</span> (The work is tough. Having said that, I don't plan to quit.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs and い-adjectives: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>慣れている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>慣れているとはいえ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>合格した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>合格したとはいえ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安いとはいえ</span></span><br>Nouns and な-adjectives: the 「だ」 is <b>usually dropped</b>, so attach とはいえ directly (adding だ is also possible).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>春</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>春とはいえ</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利とはいえ</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>便利とはいえ、スマホばかり見るのはよくない。</span> (Handy as it is, staring at your phone all the time isn't good.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The 「は」 is read as <b>わ</b>: とはいえ is pronounced 「とわいえ」.<br>・The first half must be a <b>fact that already holds</b>. You can't put a hypothetical that hasn't happened yet. \"Even if it rains tomorrow\" is <span class='gd-jp'>明日雨が降っても</span>, not とはいえ.<br>・The second half must say something that <b>runs against expectations</b>, and it often appears with <span class='gd-jp'>まだ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>やはり</span>. It doesn't mean \"so\", so you can't follow it with something that simply continues the first half.<br>・とはいえ feels somewhat written and formal. In casual talk with friends, people more often say <span class='gd-jp'>とは言っても</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>って言っても</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ものの</b> also means \"although ~, but\", but its most natural use is \"<b>you did something, but the result fell short of expectations</b>\":<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>チケットを買ったものの、結局行けなかった。</span> (I did buy the ticket, but in the end I couldn't go.)<br>If the first half is a noun, ものの has to become 「noun + である + ものの」, which sounds stiff. That's where とはいえ flows nicely:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>プロとはいえ、ミスすることもある。</span> (Even though they're pros, they sometimes make mistakes.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にもかかわらず</b> means \"in spite of ~, …\" and stresses that the result is <b>unexpected</b>, often with a tone of surprise or blame:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨にもかかわらず、たくさんの人が来た。</span> (Despite the rain, a lot of people came.)<br>とはいえ doesn't carry that strong sense of surprise. It's more like admitting the first fact and then calmly adding \"but…\".</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for \"even though it's ~ (an identity or state), that doesn't mean …\", use とはいえ. For \"I did ~, but the result wasn't …\", use ものの. For \"in spite of ~, it even …\", use にもかかわらず.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>とはいうものの</span> means almost the same thing, and it can also open a sentence as \"having said that\": <span class='gd-jp'>とはいうものの、やはり心配だ。</span> (Having said that, I'm still worried.)</div>","n2-13":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜くせに」 is the English <b>“even though you…” / “and you of all people…”</b>, said with some heat. When someone says 「<span class='gd-jp'>知ってるくせに</span>」, it doesn't just mean “even though you know.” Behind it is another line: “that's so unfair of you.” It isn't a neutral description. You're <b>scolding or calling someone out</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs and い-adjectives: attach directly (present or past both work)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知っている</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知っているくせに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>若い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>若いくせに</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言った</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言ったくせに</span></span><br>な-adjectives: keep the <b>な</b>, then attach　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>下手</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>下手なくせに</span></span><br>Nouns: add <b>の</b>, then attach　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子ども</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子どものくせに</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Calling someone out</span>The first half states the person's situation or ability, and the second half says what they did that's out of line.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>知っているくせに、教えてくれない。</span>(You know, and yet you won't tell me.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>行くって言ったくせに、来なかった。</span>(You said you'd go, and then you didn't show up.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>下手なくせに、いつも自慢する。</span>(You're bad at it, yet you're always bragging.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どものくせに、生意気なことを言うな。</span>(You're just a kid, so don't talk back like that.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Leaving it unfinished</span>You skip the second half and stop right at くせに. It often has a playful, teasing or sulky tone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>本当は好きなくせに。</span>(You actually like it, don't you.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Nouns and な-adjectives <b>can't take だ</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>子どものくせに</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>下手なくせに</span>, not 「子どもだくせに」or 「下手だくせに」.<br>・Both halves must be about <b>the same person</b>. “He knows, and <b>he</b> still won't say” works. If the subjects differ, you can't use くせに.<br>・The tone is harsh, basically scolding, so <b>don't use it with your boss, customers or elders</b>, and it doesn't belong in formal writing.<br>・It's mostly aimed at other people. Aimed at yourself, it becomes self-deprecating: <span class='gd-jp'>日本人のくせに、漢字が苦手なんです。</span>(I'm Japanese, yet I'm not great at kanji.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: he knows the answer but won't tell me.<br><b>くせに</b> = you're annoyed and blaming him: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は知っているくせに、教えてくれない。</span><br><b>のに</b> = you find it surprising or regrettable, with a milder tone: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は知っているのに、教えてくれない。</span><br><b>にもかかわらず</b> = formal, written, no emotion: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は事情を知っているにもかかわらず、説明しなかった。</span>(Although he understood the situation, he did not explain it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>のに has a wider range. It works in these two cases where くせに doesn't:<br>Different subjects in each half: <span class='gd-jp'>雨なのに、彼は出かけた。</span>(Even though it was raining, he went out.)<br>Your own regret, with no one to blame: <span class='gd-jp'>一生懸命勉強したのに、落ちた。</span>(I studied so hard, and I still failed.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use くせに when you want to <b>call someone out</b>; use のに when you just find it <b>regrettable or surprising</b>; use にもかかわらず for <b>formal situations</b>.</div>","n2-14":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜一方で」 is like the English <b>“while… , on the other hand…”</b>. You set two things side by side: on one side A, on the other side B. It's especially common in news, reports and articles, and the tone is fairly formal.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Contrasting two sides</span>Use it when one thing has both good and bad sides, or when two people or places are in opposite situations. You put them next to each other to compare.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この仕事は大変な一方で、やりがいもある。</span>(This job is tough, but on the other hand it's also rewarding.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>都市部の人口が増える一方で、地方の人口は減っている。</span>(While the population of urban areas is growing, that of rural areas is shrinking.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>兄はスポーツが得意な一方で、弟は本を読むのが好きだ。</span>(My older brother is good at sports, whereas my younger brother likes reading.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Doing two things at once</span>One person does A and also does B. Here there's no sense of “opposite”. It just says they're covering both sides.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は会社で働く一方で、週末は小説を書いている。</span>(He works at a company, and at the same time he writes novels on weekends.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中先生は研究を続ける一方で、学生の指導にも力を入れている。</span>(Professor Tanaka continues his research while also putting real effort into guiding his students.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put the plain form before it, meaning the form without ます or です. Verbs and い-adjectives attach directly; <b>な-adjectives and nouns need extra care</b>.<br>Verbs: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>増える</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>増える一方で</span></span>, and the past form also works: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>伸びた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>伸びた一方で</span></span><br>い-adjectives: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忙しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忙しい一方で</span></span><br>な-adjectives: keep 「な」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利な一方で</span></span> (<span class='gd-jp'>便利である一方で</span> is also possible)<br>Nouns: add 「である」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生である一方で</span></span><br>You can drop 「で」 and say just 「一方」, which feels more written.</div><div class='gd-sec'>It can also stand alone at the start of a sentence, meaning “on the other hand”: <span class='gd-jp'>その一方で</span>、<span class='gd-jp'>一方、</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>給料は上がった。その一方で、残業も増えた。</span>(My salary went up. On the other hand, the overtime increased too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・な-adjectives can't take だ: 「便利だ一方で」 is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>便利な一方で</span>. Nouns are the same: not 「学生だ一方で」 but <span class='gd-jp'>学生である一方で</span>.<br>・It's formal. Saying 「仕事は大変な一方で…」 to a friend would sound like you're reading a press release. In everyday talk, <span class='gd-jp'>けど</span> is enough: <span class='gd-jp'>仕事は大変だけど、楽しいよ。</span>(Work is hard, but it's fun, you know.)<br>・<b>When 「一方」 goes at the very end as 「一方だ」, the meaning changes completely.</b> See below.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same topic, population:<br><b>一方で (mid-sentence)</b> = contrasting two sides</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>都市の人口は増える一方で、地方は減っている。</span>(City populations are growing, while on the other hand rural areas are shrinking.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>一方だ (at the end of the sentence)</b> = something keeps changing in one direction only</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>都市の人口は増える一方だ。</span>(City populations just keep growing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Before 一方だ you can only put verbs that express change, like <span class='gd-jp'>増える</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>減る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>悪くなる</span>, and it's mostly used for changes in a bad direction.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ばかりだ (change)</b> means almost the same as 一方だ, but leans more toward “getting worse and worse”, often with a sense of worry or helplessness.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>父の病気は悪くなるばかりだ。</span>(My father's illness just keeps getting worse.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if 「一方」 is followed by more words and you're comparing two things, it's 一方で. If 「一方」 sits at the end and the sentence stops there, meaning “keeps on ~ing”, it's 一方だ.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>一方で simply sets two things side by side. If you want to say “although A, B happened”, with a twist of “not what you'd expect”, learn <span class='gd-jp'>〜ものの</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜にもかかわらず</span> next.</div>","n2-16":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜つつも」 means <b>\"even though I know ~, I still ~\"</b> or <b>\"I kept meaning to ~, but ended up ~\"</b>. Think of \"I know staying up late is bad for me, yet I scroll on my phone until 3 a.m.\" It captures the feeling of <b>knowing it in your head while your actions don't follow</b>. It leans <b>written</b>, so you'll see it more in articles, news, and formal settings.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the verb's ます form, drop ます, and add つつ（も）.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>思う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>思います</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>思いつつ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知ります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知りつつ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言います</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言いつつ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べつつ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しつつ</span></span><br>It attaches to <b>verbs</b> only. Adjectives and nouns can't take it directly.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>although ~, yet ~</span>The first half is what's in your mind, and the second half is an action that goes against it. The most common forms are 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と知りつつ</span>」, 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と思いつつ</span>」, and 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と言いつつ</span>」. The second half often uses <span class='gd-jp'>てしまう</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>られない</span>, which adds a hint of regret or inability.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>体に悪いと知りつつも、夜更かしがやめられない。</span>（I know it's bad for my health, yet I can't stop staying up late.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>早く返事をしなきゃと思いつつ、一週間たってしまった。</span>（I kept thinking I had to reply soon, and a week went by.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ダイエット中だと言いつつ、ケーキを二つも食べた。</span>（She says she's on a diet, yet she ate two slices of cake.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>while ~ing</span>「つつ」 without 「も」 has another meaning: two things happening at the same time. It's the written-style version of 「ながら」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>景色を楽しみつつ、山道を歩いた。</span>（We walked the mountain path while enjoying the scenery.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>皆さんの意見を聞きつつ、計画を進めていきます。</span>（We'll move the plan forward while listening to everyone's opinions.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches to the ます form, not the dictionary form: <span class='gd-jp'>知りつつ</span> is correct, and 「知るつつ」 is wrong.<br>・The <b>same person</b> must be the subject on both sides. \"My mother objected, but I went abroad to study anyway\" involves two people, so you can't use つつ. Say <span class='gd-jp'>母は反対したが、私は留学した。</span><br>・With 「も」 it is always \"although ~, yet ~\". With just 「つつ」, you have to use context to decide whether it means \"although\" or \"while\".<br>・It leans written. When chatting with friends, 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜けど</span>」 or 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜のに</span>」 is usually enough. That said, set phrases like 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と思いつつ</span>」 and 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜と知りつつ</span>」 do show up in speech too.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>つつも vs ながらも</b>: The meaning is almost the same, and in one sentence you can often swap them.<br><span class='gd-jp'>悪いと知りつつも、やってしまった。</span><br><span class='gd-jp'>悪いと知りながらも、やってしまった。</span>（I knew it was wrong, but I did it anyway.）<br>The difference: つつも <b>attaches to verbs only</b> and sounds more like writing. ながらも can also attach to adjectives and nouns, as in <span class='gd-jp'>狭いながらも楽しい我が家</span> (small, but a happy home), and there you can't swap in つつも.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>つつも vs つつある</b>: They differ by one character but mean completely different things. つつある means \"is gradually becoming ~\" and has no \"although\" meaning.<br><span class='gd-jp'>景気は回復しつつある。</span>（The economy is gradually recovering.）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: for \"knowing ~, yet ~\", use つつも. If what comes before is an adjective or noun, switch to ながらも. When you see つつある, think \"gradually becoming\".</div>","n2-17":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>This 「〜ながら（も）」 means <b>\"although ~, yet ~\"</b> or <b>\"even though ~, still ~\"</b>. You state one situation first, then follow it with a result you wouldn't expect.<br>You learned 「<span class='gd-jp'>音楽を聞きながら勉強する</span>」 (study while listening to music) at N4, and that one means <b>doing two things at once</b>. This one looks exactly the same but means something completely different.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: Use the ます form, drop ます, and add ながら. The most common shape is 「〜ている」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>住んでいます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>住んでいながら</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>知ります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>知りながら</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: Attach directly, and keep the い as is. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>狭い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>狭いながら</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: Drop the な and attach. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>残念な</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>残念ながら</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: Attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子ども</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子どもながら</span></span><br>Adding 「も」 to make 「ながらも」 means the same thing, with a slightly stronger \"although\" feel.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>近くに住んでいながら、一度も行ったことがない。</span>（Even though I live nearby, I've never once been there.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>悪いと知りながら、うそをついてしまった。</span>（I knew it was wrong, yet I told a lie.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は学生ながら、自分で会社を経営している。</span>（Although he's still a student, he runs his own company.）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>狭いながらも、住み心地のいい部屋だ。</span>（It's small, but it's a very comfortable room to live in.）</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'>A few of these have become set phrases that you'll hear a lot in daily life and at work. Memorizing them as whole sentences is the fastest way.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>残念ながら</span>\"Unfortunately.\" It's often used when announcing bad news.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>残念ながら、明日の試合は中止になりました。</span>（Unfortunately, tomorrow's game has been cancelled.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>勝手ながら</span>Common on shop notices. It means \"we apologize for taking the liberty of ~\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>勝手ながら、本日は休業させていただきます。</span>（We apologize, but we will be closed today.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>陰ながら</span>\"Quietly, behind the scenes.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>陰ながら応援しています。</span>（I'm quietly cheering you on.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>我ながら</span>\"Even I have to admit.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>我ながらよくできたと思う。</span>（Even I think I did a good job.）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't add extra pieces when connecting: it's <span class='gd-jp'>狭いながら</span>, not 狭くながら; <span class='gd-jp'>残念ながら</span>, not 残念なながら; and <span class='gd-jp'>子どもながら</span>, not 子どものながら.<br>・Apart from the set phrases above, this pattern is <b>written and formal</b>, the kind of thing you'd use in articles, news, and interviews. With friends, 「<span class='gd-jp'>狭いけど、いい部屋だよ</span>」 sounds more natural.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>昔ながらの</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>生まれながらの</span> don't mean \"although\". <span class='gd-jp'>昔ながらの町並み</span> means \"a street that keeps its old-time look\", and <span class='gd-jp'>生まれながらの才能</span> means \"an innate talent\". Learn them separately.<br>・Both halves usually talk about <b>the same person or the same thing</b>, as in \"although he's a student, (he) runs a company\".</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Simultaneous ながら</b> = while ~ing: <span class='gd-jp'>テレビを見ながらご飯を食べる。</span>（I eat while watching TV.）<br><b>Contrastive ながら</b> = although ~, yet ~: <span class='gd-jp'>毎日テレビを見ていながら、そのニュースを知らなかった。</span>（Even though I watch TV every day, I didn't know about that news.）</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>つつ（も）</b>: It means almost the same as contrastive ながら（も）, but it's more written and <b>attaches to verbs only</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>悪いと知りつつも、うそをついてしまった。</span>（I knew it was wrong, yet I told a lie.）<br>You can't use it with adjectives or nouns: say <span class='gd-jp'>狭いながらも</span>, not 狭いつつも.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>くせに</b>: It also means \"even though ~\", but it carries <b>blame or dissatisfaction</b>, like a spoken complaint.<br><span class='gd-jp'>知っているくせに、教えてくれない。</span>（You know perfectly well, and you still won't tell me!）<br><span class='gd-jp'>知っていながら、教えてくれなかった。</span>（He knew, yet he didn't tell me. This one sounds more like a calm statement.）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: if what comes before is an adjective or noun, or if 「も」 follows, it's \"although\". If it follows a verb, check whether the second half goes against expectation. If it does, it's \"although\"; if not, it's \"while ~ing\". Use くせに to complain to someone, and ながら or つつ when writing.</div>","n2-20":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に限らず」means <b>“not only ~”</b> or <b>“not limited to ~”</b>. You first name the thing everyone would naturally think of, then say the range is actually wider and other things apply too.<br>It's like saying “Not just weekends, weekdays are crowded too.” In Japanese: <span class='gd-jp'>週末に限らず、平日も混んでいる。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a <b>noun</b> in front and add 「に限らず」directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生に限らず</span></span> (not only students)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日本</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本に限らず</span></span> (not only Japan)<br>「限らず」is read <b>かぎらず</b> and means “not limited to.” The second half often contains words like <span class='gd-jp'>も</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>誰でも</span>, or <span class='gd-jp'>何でも</span>, which stretch the range wider.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>The range is bigger than you'd think</span>People assume only A is like this, but B and C are too.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は週末に限らず、平日もいつも混んでいる。</span> (This shop is crowded not only on weekends but always on weekdays too.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この公園は子どもに限らず、大人にも人気がある。</span> (This park is popular not only with children but with adults too.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に限らず、アジア全体で少子化が進んでいる。</span> (Not only in Japan: the birth rate is falling across all of Asia.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No restrictions, open to anyone</span>Very common in notices and event descriptions.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>学生に限らず、誰でも参加できます。</span> (Not limited to students. Anyone can join.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>It's the opposite of 「に限り」and 「に限って」</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>本日に限り半額</span> means “half price today only,” while <span class='gd-jp'>本日に限らず</span> means “not only today.” That one extra 「ず」flips the meaning completely, so watch for it when reading notices.<br>・It basically follows a noun. A verb can't go directly before it, so you can't say 「食べるに限らず」. To talk about actions, 「〜ばかりでなく」sounds more natural.<br>・The tone is formal and written. You'll see it in news, notices, and essays. With friends, just say <span class='gd-jp'>〜だけじゃなくて</span>.<br>・What comes first should be “the thing everyone would already think of,” and what follows is “what's also included.” If you reverse the order, the sentence sounds odd.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に限らず</b> = what comes before is <b>one specific thing</b>, and the meaning is “not only that”:<br><span class='gd-jp'>学生に限らず、社会人も参加できる。</span> (Not only students; working adults can join too.)<br><b>を問わず</b> = what comes before is <b>a category that splits into several types</b>, and the meaning is “no matter which type”:<br><span class='gd-jp'>このイベントは年齢を問わず、誰でも参加できる。</span> (This event is open to anyone, regardless of age.)<br>「年齢」splits into many ages, so you use を問わず. 「学生」is one specific group, so you use に限らず.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can replace the word with “whatever kind of ○○” (年齢, 性別, 国籍, 経験, 天候), use を問わず. If it's one specific person, place, or thing (学生, 日本, 週末), use に限らず.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>とは限らない</b>: it also contains 「限」, but it goes at the end of the sentence and means “<b>not necessarily</b>”: <span class='gd-jp'>高い物がいい物とは限らない。</span> (Expensive things aren't necessarily good.) This is a completely different thing from “not only ~.”</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>のみならず</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ばかりでなく</span> mean “not only ~, but also ~,” and with a noun in front they're often interchangeable with に限らず. The difference is that に限らず only takes nouns, and its focus is “the range isn't limited to this.”</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, study <span class='gd-jp'>〜を問わず</span> (regardless of ~), plus <span class='gd-jp'>〜のみならず</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜ばかりか</span>, which also mean “not only ~.” Then you'll have this whole “range” group covered.</div>","n2-21":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜を問わず」means <b>“regardless of ~”</b> or <b>“no matter ~.”</b> <span class='gd-jp'>問う</span> means “to ask, to question,” so <span class='gd-jp'>問わず</span> is “without asking,” that is, “regardless.” A job ad that says “all ages welcome, no experience required” would say <span class='gd-jp'>年齢を問わず</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>経験を問わず</span> in Japanese. It means the result is the same whichever type of that condition applies.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Recruiting and notices</span>You'll see it most in job ads, event sign-ups, and shop notices, meaning “this condition has no restrictions.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年齢を問わず、どなたでもご参加いただけます。</span> (Anyone can take part, regardless of age.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このアルバイトは経験を問わず、誰でも応募できます。</span> (This part-time job doesn't require experience. Anyone can apply.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Describing a situation</span>Say that something isn't affected by a condition and holds true in every case.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この歌は世代を問わず愛されている。</span> (This song is loved by every generation.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昼夜を問わず、工事の音がうるさい。</span> (Day or night, the construction noise is loud.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add <span class='gd-jp'>を問わず</span> directly to a noun. The noun in front is one of two kinds:<br><b>Words that naturally have many types</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>年齢</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>性別</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>国籍</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>経験</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>天候</span><br><b>A pair of opposites</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>男女</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>昼夜</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>経験の有無</span> (with or without experience)<br>You can also attach it to 「〜かどうか」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>経験があるかどうか</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>経験があるかどうかを問わず</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>は問いません</span>Goes at the end of a sentence. Very common in job ads.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年齢は問いません。</span> (Age doesn't matter.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Dropping を</span>On posters and recruitment notices, to keep things short, it's often written as <span class='gd-jp'>年齢問わず</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>経験問わず</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What comes before must be a word that “has different types.” <span class='gd-jp'>学生を問わず</span> doesn't work, because “student” is just one type and there's nothing to be “regardless” about. Say <span class='gd-jp'>学生かどうかを問わず</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>学生・社会人を問わず</span> instead.<br>・「問わず」is read <b>とわず</b>. It's the negative form of <span class='gd-jp'>問う</span>, so don't write it as 問えず.<br>・This is a very formal, written expression. With friends, <span class='gd-jp'>関係なく</span> sounds more natural: <span class='gd-jp'>年とか関係なく、誰でも楽しめるよ。</span> (Age doesn't matter, anyone can enjoy it!)<br>・The particle is always 「を」. Don't swap it for に or が.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に限らず</b> = <b>not only A, but B too ~</b> (you give one representative example, then widen the range):<br><span class='gd-jp'>このアプリは若者に限らず、お年寄りにも人気だ。</span> (This app is popular not only with young people but with older people too.)<br><b>を問わず</b> = <b>whichever type A is, it's the same</b> (the condition isn't taken into account at all):<br><span class='gd-jp'>このアプリは年齢を問わず人気だ。</span> (This app is popular regardless of age.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>にかかわらず</span> means almost the same as を問わず: <span class='gd-jp'>天候にかかわらず実施します。</span> (It will be held regardless of the weather.)<br>If you add 「も」in the middle, it becomes <b>にもかかわらず</b>, which means something completely different: “in spite of ~.” <span class='gd-jp'>雨にもかかわらず、たくさんの人が来た。</span> (Despite the rain, many people came.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: “not only A, but B too” uses に限らず; “no matter what A is” uses を問わず; “in spite of A” uses にもかかわらず.</div>","n2-22":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にわたって」is like the English news phrases <b>\"spanning ~\"</b>, <b>\"across ~\"</b>, <b>\"over a period of ~\"</b> or <b>\"throughout ~\"</b>. Think \"heavy rain for three days straight\" or \"power outages across the whole country.\" The point is to stress <b>a long time or a wide area</b>, covered from start to finish, the whole stretch included.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + にわたって. The noun before it needs to be <b>a length</b> or <b>a whole area</b>. The three most common types are:</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A long time</span>3日間、10年、長年、長期間</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>会議は5時間にわたって続いた。</span>(The meeting went on for a full five hours.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A wide area</span>全国、広範囲、関東全域、多方面</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>台風の影響で、関東全域にわたって大雨が降った。</span>(Because of the typhoon, heavy rain fell across the entire Kanto region.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Number of times / amount</span>3回、数ページ</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この特集は3回にわたって放送されます。</span>(This special will be broadcast in three parts.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にわたり</b>: Same meaning, but more formal and written. Press releases and public notices love it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>調査は全国にわたり実施された。</span>(The survey was carried out nationwide.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にわたる＋noun</b>: Use this form when a noun follows directly.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>長年にわたる研究の成果が発表された。</span>(The results of many years of research were announced.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にわたった＋noun</b>: For something that has already finished.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>2時間にわたった話し合いは、結論が出ないまま終わった。</span>(The two-hour discussion ended without reaching a conclusion.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・You can't put a <b>point in time</b> or a <b>single place</b> before it: you can't say 「3時にわたって」or 「月曜日にわたって」. It needs a \"stretch\" or a \"whole area,\" like <span class='gd-jp'>3時間</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>1週間</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>全国</span>.<br>・It carries a feeling of \"so long, so wide,\" so using it for small things sounds odd. <span class='gd-jp'>10分にわたってシャワーを浴びた</span> sounds like a news report on your shower.<br>・The tone is fairly formal and shows up in news, reports and notices. When chatting with friends, <span class='gd-jp'>3日間ずっと雨だった</span> (it rained for three days straight) sounds much more natural.<br>・When a noun follows directly, use <b>にわたる</b>: say <span class='gd-jp'>3時間にわたる会議</span>, not 「3時間にわたって会議」.<br>・This grammar comes from the verb <span class='gd-jp'>渡る</span>. You'll occasionally see it written 「に渡って」, but it's mostly written in hiragana.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>The one most easily confused with it is <b>〜にかけて</b>. Both talk about range, but from different angles.<br><b>にかけて</b>: Tells you <b>from where to where</b>. It gives a start and an end point, and the range is rough.<br><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>3月から5月にかけて工事が行われる。</span>(Construction will take place from around March to May.)</div><b>にわたって</b>: Tells you <b>how long in total, or how big the whole area is</b>, stressing length and breadth.<br><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>3か月にわたって工事が行われる。</span>(Construction will go on for three months.)</div>It works the same way with places: <span class='gd-jp'>九州から関西にかけて雨が降る</span> (rain will fall from Kyushu to the Kansai area) lays out the range, while <span class='gd-jp'>西日本全域にわたって雨が降る</span> (rain will fall across all of western Japan) stresses how large the range is.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if the sentence has two endpoints, \"A から B,\" you'll usually use にかけて. If there's only one \"total length\" or \"whole area\" before it, like <span class='gd-jp'>3か月</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>全国</span>, use にわたって.</div>","n2-23":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜をもとに」is the English <b>\"based on ~\"</b> or <b>\"using ~ as a basis.\"</b> What comes before it is the <b>material or starting point</b>, and what follows is the <b>new thing you made from it</b>. Think \"a movie based on a true story\" or \"curry made from Grandma's recipe.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>A <b>noun</b> always comes before it, followed by <span class='gd-jp'>をもとに</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>をもとにして</span>. The meaning is the same, but the version with して is a bit more complete.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>データ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>データをもとに</span></span> (based on the data)<br>When a noun follows directly, change it to <b>をもとにした＋noun</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>実話</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>実話をもとにした映画</span></span> (a movie based on a true story)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>をもとに＋action</span>It's often followed by verbs that produce something, like make, write, think up, decide or predict.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>アンケートの結果をもとに、新しいメニューを考えた。</span>(Based on the survey results, we came up with a new menu.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>祖母のレシピをもとにして、カレーを作ってみた。</span>(I tried making curry based on my grandmother's recipe.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>をもとに作られた</span>Very common when introducing the source of a work.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このドラマは人気漫画をもとに作られた。</span>(This drama was made based on a popular manga.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・In kanji it's <span class='gd-jp'>基に</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>元に</span>, read <b>もとに</b>, not 「きに」. Writing it in hiragana is perfectly fine too.<br>・Only a noun can come before it. If you want to put a whole clause there, first turn it into a noun with 「こと」: <span class='gd-jp'>調べたことをもとに、レポートを書いた。</span> (I wrote the report based on what I found out.)<br>・When English \"according to the weather report, it will rain tomorrow\" cites an <b>information source</b>, Japanese doesn't use をもとに. Use <span class='gd-jp'>によると</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうだ。</span><br>・What follows needs an action or result like \"make / come up with / decide.\" Plain statements like \"is there\" or \"like\" don't fit.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>をもとに</b> = using something as <b>raw material or a starting point</b>, which you can then process, extend or adapt:<br><span class='gd-jp'>このデータをもとに、新しい企画を考えた。</span> (Using this data as a starting point, I came up with a new plan.)<br><b>に基づいて</b> = <b>following</b> some basis without departing from it:<br><span class='gd-jp'>このデータに基づいて、結論を出した。</span> (I drew a conclusion based on this data.)<br>When judging according to laws or rules, <span class='gd-jp'>法律に基づいて判断する</span> is the more natural and formal choice. をもとに leans toward \"take it as material and make something,\" so it feels weaker with laws and rules. In formal settings, we recommend に基づいて.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if the result is \"using it as a base to make something new,\" use をもとに. If the result is \"following its rules or evidence,\" use に基づいて.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another pattern with the same shape, \"noun + を + 〜に,\" is <span class='gd-jp'>をきっかけに</span>, meaning \"triggered by ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>留学をきっかけに、料理を始めた。</span> (Studying abroad was what got me started on cooking.) What comes before it is \"the start of events,\" not \"material,\" so try remembering the two side by side.</div>","n2-24":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に応じて」means <b>\"depending on ~\" or \"in line with ~\"</b>. Think of a bento shop owner who \"decides how many lunches to order based on the headcount\": when the first thing changes, what follows <b>adjusts to match</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It attaches directly to a noun: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>収入</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>収入に応じて</span></span> (depending on income)<br>The noun is usually something that <b>varies by person or situation</b>, like income, age, ability, level, budget, circumstances, or needs.<br>It's read <b>おうじて</b>. In writing you'll also often see <span class='gd-jp'>に応じ</span>, with the 「て」 dropped. The meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>収入に応じて、税金が決まる。</span> (Taxes are determined according to income.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>年齢に応じて、料金が変わります。</span> (The fee changes depending on age.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>必要に応じて</span> means \"as needed.\" You'll see it all the time in company documents and notices.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>必要に応じて、資料を追加してください。</span> (Please add materials as needed.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>に応じた＋noun</span> This is the form you use most when a noun follows.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>レベルに応じたクラスを選べます。</span> (You can choose a class that matches your level.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>There is also a <span class='gd-jp'>に応じての</span>＋noun form (e.g. <span class='gd-jp'>能力に応じての配置</span>). It means the same thing but is less common, so as a beginner just remember <span class='gd-jp'>に応じた</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What follows must be something that <b>adjusts or changes accordingly</b>. You can't use it to simply say \"according to the news\": <span class='gd-jp'>ニュースによると、明日は雨だ。</span> (According to the news, it will rain tomorrow.) Use によると here.<br>・The tone is fairly formal, common in notices, explanations, and the workplace. With friends, <span class='gd-jp'>に合わせて</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>によって</span> is more common.<br>・The verb <span class='gd-jp'>応じる</span> on its own often means \"to respond to, to accept\": <span class='gd-jp'>インタビューに応じる</span> (to agree to an interview). Don't mix it up with the \"adjust according to ~\" meaning.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same topic, income and taxes:<br><b>に応じて</b> = <b>adjusting to match</b> what comes before: <span class='gd-jp'>収入に応じて、税金が決まる。</span> (Taxes are set according to income.)<br><b>によって</b> = <b>differs</b> <b>depending on</b> what comes before; the focus is on \"it's different\": <span class='gd-jp'>収入によって、税金が違う。</span> (Taxes differ depending on income.)<br><b>につれて</b> = as the first thing keeps changing, the second <b>changes gradually along with it</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>収入が増えるにつれて、税金も増える。</span> (As income rises, taxes rise too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>When no one is actively \"adjusting to match,\" によって sounds more natural: <span class='gd-jp'>国によって、習慣が違う。</span> (Customs differ from country to country.) Swapping in に応じて here would sound odd.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use に応じて for \"adjust to match it,\" によって for \"differs depending on it,\" and につれて for \"the more ~, the more ~.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Other patterns for \"changing along with something\" are <span class='gd-jp'>〜に伴って</span> (along with ~) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜につれて</span>. Try studying them side by side.</div>","n2-27":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜のみならず」is the formal version of <b>\"not only ~, but also…\"</b>. It means the same as the familiar 「<span class='gd-jp'>だけでなく</span>」, but it's used in <b>reports, news, and speeches</b>, not in chats with friends.<br>Break it apart and it makes sense: <span class='gd-jp'>のみ</span> = <span class='gd-jp'>だけ</span> (only), <span class='gd-jp'>ならず</span> = \"is not,\" so together: \"not just A.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Adding more</span> You state A, then add B, and B is usually bigger or broader in scope. The second half often has <b>も, でも, or にも</b> to mean \"B too.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>国内のみならず、海外でも高い評価を得ている。</span> (It is highly regarded not only in Japan but also overseas.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は学生のみならず、社会人にも人気がある。</span> (This shop is popular not only with students but also with working adults.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>日本</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>日本のみならず</span></span><br><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly to the plain form (the dictionary form, or the た-form or ない-form).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話せる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>話せるのみならず</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>狭い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>狭いのみならず</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: add <b>である</b>; you can't use 「だ」. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静かだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かであるのみならず</span></span><br>You can also add である to a noun when you want to stress status or nature: <span class='gd-jp'>医者であるのみならず</span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は英語が話せるのみならず、中国語も読める。</span> (He can not only speak English but also read Chinese.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は狭いのみならず、駅からも遠い。</span> (This room is not only small but also far from the station.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この町は静かであるのみならず、交通も便利だ。</span> (This town is not only quiet but also has convenient transportation.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ただ〜のみならず</span> Adding 「ただ」 in front makes it \"not merely ~,\" with a stronger tone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これはただ個人の問題のみならず、社会全体の問題でもある。</span> (This is not merely an individual problem, but a problem for society as a whole.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At the start of a sentence</span> Used on its own as a conjunction, it means \"not only that.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は仕事が速い。のみならず、ミスもほとんどない。</span> (He works fast. Not only that, he hardly ever makes mistakes.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・With な-adjectives, don't say <span class='gd-jp'>静かだのみならず</span>; say <span class='gd-jp'>静かであるのみならず</span>.<br>・It's stiff and very formal. With friends, use <span class='gd-jp'>だけじゃなく</span> for \"not only ~\"; のみならず would sound like you're reading an official document.<br>・It <b>adds on</b>; it doesn't mean \"not A but B.\" The two parts need to match: if A is a place, B should be a place too; if A is a problem, B should be a problem. Remember to add 「も」 in the second half.<br>・The direction must stay consistent: good with good, bad with bad. You can't use it for contrasting things.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: both adults and kids love this game.<br><b>だけでなく</b> = the most general form, usable in both speech and writing: <span class='gd-jp'>このゲームは子どもだけでなく、大人にも人気がある。</span><br><b>のみならず</b> = same meaning, in a formal, written tone: <span class='gd-jp'>このゲームは子どものみならず、大人にも人気がある。</span><br><b>ばかりか</b> = carries a sense of surprise, \"who would have thought even ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>このゲームは子どもばかりか、大人まで夢中になっている。</span> (Not just kids; even adults are hooked.)<br><b>に限らず</b> = the focus is \"not limited to the range of A,\" and the second half often pairs with <span class='gd-jp'>誰でも</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>どこでも</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>このゲームは子どもに限らず、誰でも楽しめる。</span> (This game isn't limited to kids; anyone can enjoy it.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use だけじゃなく when chatting and のみならず when writing; use ばかりか for the surprise of \"even ~\"; use に限らず for \"no limits, anyone can.\"</div>","n2-28":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「AはもちろんBも」means <b>\"A goes without saying, and even B ~\"</b>, much like \"not to mention B\" in English. A is the obvious one everyone would think of. B is the extra, less expected one you add on top.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は味はもちろん、サービスもいい。</span>(The food is great, of course, and the service is good too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Basically you attach 「はもちろん」directly to a noun. The second half usually picks it up with <b>も</b> or <b>まで</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子供</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子供はもちろん、大人も</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供はもちろん、大人も楽しめる映画です。</span>(A movie kids will love, not to mention adults.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>家具はもちろん、家電までついている部屋だ。</span>(A room that comes with furniture, and even appliances.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>If the noun is followed by a small particle like 「で」or「に」, keep it and add 「はもちろん」after it:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本ではもちろん、海外でも人気がある。</span>(It's popular in Japan, of course, and abroad too.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>To talk about an action, first add 「の」after the verb to turn it into a noun:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>話すのはもちろん、書くのも得意だ。</span>(He's good at speaking, of course, and at writing too.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Don't flip the order</b>: put the \"naturally expected\" item first. At a theme park, people think of kids first, so <span class='gd-jp'>子供はもちろん、大人も</span> sounds natural.<br>・<b>When the sentence ends in a negative, put the \"hard\" item first</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>漢字はもちろん、ひらがなも書けない。</span>(He can't write kanji, of course, and can't even write hiragana.) Not being able to write kanji is normal. The point is that he can't even write easy hiragana.<br>・Remember to follow up with <b>も</b> or <b>まで</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>英語はもちろん、スペイン語が話せる</span> sounds awkward without the も.<br>・This is different from a standalone 「もちろん」(of course): <span class='gd-jp'>もちろん行きます</span> means \"Of course I'll go,\" with no \"A goes without saying, and even B\" meaning.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three can be translated as \"not only A, but B too ~\". Here they are with the same sentence:<br><b>はもちろん</b> = A is obvious to begin with: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は英語はもちろん、中国語も話せる。</span>(He speaks English, of course, and Chinese too.)<br><b>ばかりか</b> = the focus is on B being a bit surprising: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は英語ばかりか、中国語も話せる。</span>(He doesn't just speak English; he even speaks Chinese.)<br><b>のみならず</b> = about the same as ばかりか, but very formal, mostly used in writing or speeches: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は英語のみならず、中国語も話せる。</span></div><div class='gd-sec'>One more practical difference: after <b>はもちろん</b> you can use a request or a decision, like \"please ~\" or \"I'll ~\". <b>ばかりか</b> isn't really used that way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>準備はもちろん、片付けも手伝ってください。</span>(Please help with the cleanup as well as the preparation.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to say \"A goes without saying\", use はもちろん. To stress \"even B, how surprising!\", use ばかりか. Save のみならず for formal writing.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜はもちろんのこと」means the same thing, with slightly more emphasis. In writing you'll often see the more formal <span class='gd-jp'>〜はもとより</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>国内はもとより、海外からも注文が来る。</span>(Orders come in from within the country, of course, and from overseas too.)</div>","n2-30":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜だけのことはある」means <b>\"No wonder ~\", \"As you'd expect from ~\", \"It really lives up to ~\"</b>. You see an impressive result, then look back and say: yes, this fits perfectly with who they are, what was spent, or the effort put in. It <b>lives up to its reputation</b>.</div><div class='gd-sec'>The <span class='gd-jp'>だけ</span> here doesn't mean \"only\". The feeling of the whole phrase is: \"Being ~, of course they have that ability. It figures.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Status that lives up to its name</span>An expert, a famous restaurant, or a famous person performs as well as their reputation suggests.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>さすがプロだけのことはある。</span>(As you'd expect from a pro.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>英語がペラペラだ。5年もアメリカに住んでいただけのことはある。</span>(His English is fluent. No wonder, after living in America for five years.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Money well spent</span>Like the English \"you get what you pay for\".</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このいす、全然疲れない。高かっただけのことはある。</span>(This chair never gets tiring, no matter how long you sit. It's expensive for a reason.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No wonder it's popular</span>After experiencing it yourself, you feel everyone's praise was justified.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このパン、本当においしい。行列ができるだけのことはあるね。</span>(This bread is really good. No wonder there's a line.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: plain form directly　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>練習した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>練習しただけのことはある</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: directly　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いだけのことはある</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: keep the 「な」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>有名</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>有名なだけのことはある</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: directly, without だ　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>プロ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>プロだけのことはある</span></span><br>For a politer tone, say <span class='gd-jp'>だけのことはありますね</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's often paired with <span class='gd-jp'>さすが</span>, which strengthens the \"as you'd expect\" feel. A very common order is to <b>state the result you saw first, then add だけのことはある</b> as the conclusion.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>景色が最高だ。さすが有名なだけのことはある。</span>(The view is amazing. No wonder it's so famous.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>For something whose result has already come out, you can also use the past 「あった」. It's often used to say effort paid off.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>合格できた。毎日勉強しただけのことはあった。</span>(I passed. Studying every day really paid off.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・If you read <span class='gd-jp'>だけ</span> as \"only\", the whole sentence becomes completely confusing. Here it means \"as you'd expect, living up to the name\".<br>・Don't add だ after a noun: say <span class='gd-jp'>プロだけのことはある</span>, not プロだだけのことはある.<br>・Keep 「な」on な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>有名なだけのことはある</span>, not 有名だけのことはある.<br>・You need an <b>actual result you've seen</b> as evidence. If something hasn't happened yet and you're only guessing, you can't use it.<br>・As a beginner, remember this is for <b>praise and agreement</b>. Don't use it to complain that something is bad.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>だけあって</b>: it also means \"as you'd expect from\", but it goes in the <b>middle of the sentence</b>, followed directly by the result, so everything fits in one sentence.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>さすがプロだけあって、写真がきれいだ。</span>(As you'd expect from a pro, the photos are beautiful.)<br><b>だけのことはある</b>: it goes at the <b>end of the sentence</b> as the final conclusion.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>写真がきれいだ。さすがプロだけのことはある。</span>(The photos are beautiful. As you'd expect from a pro.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ことだから</b>: it's also tied to a person's character or ability, but it's a <b>guess made before the result is out</b>. The form is noun + の + ことだから.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さんのことだから、きっと今回も合格するよ。</span>(Knowing Tanaka, he'll surely pass this time too.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>一回で合格した。さすが田中さんだけのことはある。</span>(He passed on the first try. That's Tanaka for you.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if the result isn't out yet and you're guessing, use ことだから. If the result is out and you're impressed, use だけのことはある. If you want to continue straight into the result within the same sentence, switch to だけあって.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Another pattern that gives a positive evaluation is <span class='gd-jp'>〜に値する</span> (worth ~), for example <span class='gd-jp'>この映画は見るに値する。</span>(This movie is worth watching.)</div>","n2-33":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜上に」means <b>\"not only ~, but also ~\"</b> or <b>\"~, and on top of that ~\"</b>. You state one point, then pile a second one on top in the <b>same direction</b>: good plus good, or bad plus bad.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は駅から近い上に、家賃も安い。</span>(This room is close to the station, and the rent is cheap too.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>道に迷った上に、雨にも降られた。</span>(I got lost, and on top of that, I got caught in the rain.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: attach directly, in either present or past.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>寝坊した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>寝坊した上に</span></span><br><b>い-adjectives</b>: attach directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>安い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>安い上に</span></span><br><b>な-adjectives</b>: change 「だ」 to 「な」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>親切だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>親切な上に</span></span><br><b>Nouns</b>: add 「である」 or 「の」. In writing, 「である」 is more common.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>医者</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>医者である上に</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は親切な上に、仕事も早い。</span>(She's kind, and she works fast too.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は寝坊した上に、財布まで忘れてしまった。</span>(Today I overslept, and I even forgot my wallet.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は医者である上に、作家としても有名だ。</span>(He's a doctor, and he's also famous as a writer.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>You'll notice the second half often has <span class='gd-jp'>も</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>まで</span>, because the meaning is \"this <b>too</b> ~\" or \"even this ~\".</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Read it as <b>うえに</b>, not じょうに.<br>・Both halves must point in the same direction. <span class='gd-jp'>安い上に、まずい。</span> is wrong: cheap is a plus and tasting bad is a minus. For one good and one bad, use <span class='gd-jp'>安いけど、まずい。</span> (It's cheap, but it tastes awful.)<br>・Don't keep 「だ」 on な-adjectives: say <span class='gd-jp'>便利な上に</span>, not 便利だ上に.<br>・A 「上に」 sentence describes or evaluates things, so don't follow it directly with \"please do ~\" or \"let's ~\". If you want to persuade someone, state both facts first, then lead into it with <span class='gd-jp'>から</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>寒い上に風も強いから、今日は家にいよう。</span> (It's cold and windy too, so let's stay home today.)<br>・The 「上に」 in <span class='gd-jp'>机の上に本がある。</span> means \"on top of the desk\". That's about position and has nothing to do with this grammar point. Context makes it obvious.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three contain 「上」 but mean completely different things. Let's compare them in a work context:<br><b>上に</b> = and also (two facts stacked together): <span class='gd-jp'>この仕事は給料が安い上に、残業も多い。</span> (This job has low pay, and there's a lot of overtime too.)<br><b>上で</b> = do A first, then B: <span class='gd-jp'>条件をよく確認した上で、契約します。</span> (I'll sign the contract after checking the terms carefully.)<br><b>上は</b> = since ~, then I must: <span class='gd-jp'>引き受けた上は、最後までやります。</span> (Since I've taken it on, I'll see it through to the end.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you can say \"and also\" in English, it's 上に. If it's \"after doing ~, then\", it's 上で. If it's \"now that ~, then\", it's 上は.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>その上</span> at the start of a sentence also means \"and what's more\": <span class='gd-jp'>この店は安い。その上、おいしい。</span> (This restaurant is cheap. And it's delicious, too.)<br>For a stronger \"not only ~, but even ~\", go on to <span class='gd-jp'>〜ばかりか</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜のみならず</span>.</div>","n2-34":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜のことだから」means <b>\"knowing his personality, he'll surely ~\"</b> or <b>\"you know what he's like, he'll probably ~\"</b>. You know well how a certain person usually acts, and you use that knowledge as the basis for guessing what they'll do this time.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Guessing from personality</span>In the first half, mention a person you know well, ideally with a hint of their personality. In the second half, give your guess.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>時間に厳しい部長のことだから、もう会議室にいるはずだ。</span>(Our department head is so strict about time, so he should already be in the meeting room.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>優しい彼女のことだから、きっと手伝ってくれるよ。</span>(She's so kind, so she'll definitely help you.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>弟のことだから、どうせまだ寝ているよ。</span>(You know my little brother, he's probably still asleep anyway.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Works for companies and shops too</span>If everyone knows how it usually operates, you can use it for them as well.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの会社のことだから、また値上げするだろう。</span>(Knowing that company, they'll probably raise prices again.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add 「の」 after the noun, then 「ことだから」:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>彼</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>彼のことだから</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>あの会社</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>あの会社のことだから</span></span><br>Often a word describing their personality goes in front, to make the basis clearer:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>真面目な彼</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>真面目な彼のことだから</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half is usually your guess, so it often appears together with <span class='gd-jp'>だろう</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>きっと</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>はずだ</span>, and <span class='gd-jp'>に違いない</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't drop 「の」: it's <span class='gd-jp'>彼のことだから</span>, not 彼ことだから.<br>・The person in the first half must be someone whose habits you know well, and usually the listener knows their personality too. You can't use it for someone you've just met, because you don't really know them.<br>・The second half is \"I guess\" or \"I judge\", not a fact that has already happened. If you're completely certain, you don't need this pattern.<br>・Translating it as just \"because it's him\" can make it sound like an ordinary reason. The real point is <b>\"based on what I know about him\"</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation, guessing whether a friend will be on time:<br><b>Noun + だから</b> = because he's a certain kind of person or has a certain status (the reason is a fact or a category): <span class='gd-jp'>彼は日本人だから、時間を守るだろう。</span> (He's Japanese, so he'll probably be punctual.)<br><b>Noun + のことだから</b> = based on what I know about <b>this person</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>彼のことだから、時間を守るだろう。</span> (Knowing him, he'll probably be punctual.)<br>In the first sentence, the reason is the status of being \"Japanese\". In the second, you know him personally and know he's always on time.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if the reason is \"he is ~ (a status or fact)\", use だから. If the reason is \"I know he's just that kind of person\", use のことだから.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half is often used for a confident guess, so go on to <span class='gd-jp'>〜に違いない</span> (it must be ~) and <span class='gd-jp'>〜に決まっている</span> (it's definitely ~): <span class='gd-jp'>あの人のことだから、また忘れたに決まっている。</span> (You know what he's like, he's definitely forgotten again.)</div>","n2-31":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に値する」 means <b>\"worth ~ing\" or \"deserving of ~\"</b>, in a more formal, written register. A news report says \"this discovery deserves attention,\" and a book review says \"this book is worth reading.\" Japanese uses this pattern for both. It means that the person or thing <b>measures up and has real value</b>, and deserves that kind of assessment.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + に値する: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>尊敬</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>尊敬に値する</span></span> (worthy of respect)<br>Verb dictionary form (the base form you find in a dictionary) + に値する: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読むに値する</span></span> (worth reading)<br><span class='gd-jp'>値する</span> is read <b>あたいする</b>. It is a verb in its own right and conjugates like <span class='gd-jp'>する</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>値しない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>値します</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Positive evaluation</span>These are the words that most often come before it: <span class='gd-jp'>尊敬</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>称賛</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>信頼</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>注目</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>一読</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>検討</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>特筆</span>. For now, just memorize them as fixed combinations.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の努力は称賛に値する。</span> (His effort deserves praise.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この本は一読に値する。</span> (This book is worth reading.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このデータは注目に値する。</span> (This data deserves attention.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Negative: not worth it</span>Switch to <span class='gd-jp'>値しない</span> and the tone gets very blunt, basically \"not worth bothering with at all.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんな噂は信じるに値しない。</span> (A rumor like that isn't worth believing.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その意見は検討に値しない。</span> (That opinion isn't worth considering.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Before a noun</span>You can use it directly to describe a person or thing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は信頼に値する人物だ。</span> (He is a person worthy of trust.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The particle is always <b>に</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>尊敬に値する</span>, not 尊敬を値する.<br>・Read it <b>あたいする</b>. The 「値」 in <span class='gd-jp'>値段</span> is read ね, so don't mix them up.<br>・It can't attach to the ます-form: you can't say 「読みますに値する」. To be polite, change the ending instead: <span class='gd-jp'>読むに値します</span>.<br>・It's stiff and written. If you're chatting with a friend about a bowl of ramen, just say <span class='gd-jp'>このラーメン、食べる価値あるよ</span>. Saying 食べるに値する would sound like you're writing a food review.<br>・In English, \"totally worth it\" often means good value, as in \"totally worth it after the discount.\" Japanese uses <span class='gd-jp'>お得</span> for that, not に値する.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>価値がある</b>: It means almost the same as に値する, but it works in both everyday and written contexts, and it's the most common way to say it.<br><span class='gd-jp'>この本は読む価値がある。</span> (This book is worth reading: fine in casual talk.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>この本は一読に値する。</span> (This book is worth a read: book reviews, formal settings.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>だけのことはある</b>: This is also praise, but the focus is <b>\"no wonder, as expected of ~\"</b>. It says the result matches the person's background and effort. Take one situation, hearing a piano performance:<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼の演奏は称賛に値する。</span> (His performance deserves praise: it says the performance earns the praise.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>さすが十年ウィーンで学んだだけのことはある。</span> (As expected of someone who studied in Vienna for ten years: it says the good result matches his history.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: to say \"worth ~ing,\" use に値する in formal settings and 価値がある in everyday speech. To say \"no wonder, as expected of ~,\" use だけのことはある.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The N1 pattern 「〜に足る」 is very close in meaning, \"sufficient to ~, qualified to ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>信頼に足る人物</span> (a person sufficiently trustworthy).</div>","n2-32":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜上は」 means <b>\"since ~, then (should) ~\"</b> or \"now that ~, then ~.\" The first half states something already settled, such as your role, a decision you made, or the point things have reached. The second half says \"so I will / should do this.\" The feel is close to English \"since you promised, follow through,\" only it sounds more <b>formal and stiff</b>, and often carries a sense of resolve.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Responsibility that comes with a role</span>Since you hold this role, you should do what that role requires.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>教師である上は、生徒の手本にならなければならない。</span> (Since I'm a teacher, I must set an example for my students.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>If you did it, own it</span>Since you agreed or decided, you see it through to the end.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>引き受けた上は、最後まで責任を持ちます。</span> (Now that I've taken it on, I'll take responsibility to the end.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>やると決めた上は、もう迷わない。</span> (Now that I've decided to do it, I won't hesitate anymore.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Given how things have turned out</span>Things have already come to this, so you have to face it.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こうなった上は、正直に話すしかない。</span> (Now that it's come to this, all I can do is tell the truth.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs</b>: dictionary form or た-form, then add は directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>試合に出る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>試合に出る上は</span></span> (since I'm going to play in the match)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>約束した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>約束した上は</span></span> (since I promised)<br><b>Nouns</b>: you need to insert 「である」 in between.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>社会人</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>社会人である上は</span></span> (since I'm a working adult)</div><div class='gd-h'>What can follow</div><div class='gd-sec'>The second half has to express <b>your resolve, an obligation, a suggestion, or a judgment</b>. These are the endings you'll see most:<br><span class='gd-jp'>〜なければならない</span> (must), <span class='gd-jp'>〜べきだ</span> (should), <span class='gd-jp'>〜しかない</span> (have no choice but to), <span class='gd-jp'>〜つもりだ</span> (intend to), <span class='gd-jp'>〜たい</span> (want to).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試合に出る上は、絶対に勝ちたい。</span> (Since I'm going to play in the match, I definitely want to win.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Read it <b>うえは</b>, not じょうは.<br>・A noun can't attach directly: <span class='gd-jp'>学生である上は</span> is correct, and 「学生だ上は」 is wrong.<br>・The first half must be something <b>already settled</b>, not a hypothetical. You can't add 「もし」 to get 「もし約束した上は」.<br>・The second half can't just state a fact. A sentence like 「日本に来た上は、毎日雨だった」 doesn't work, because \"it rained every day\" is neither your resolve nor your judgment.<br>・It's formal and written. Using it with a friend sounds stiff, and in everyday speech people mostly use <span class='gd-jp'>からには</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>からには</b>: It means almost the same thing, \"since ~, then ~.\"<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>引き受けた上は、最後まで頑張ります。</span> (Since I've taken it on, I'll do my best to the end.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>引き受けたからには、最後まで頑張ります。</span> (Same as above.)<br>The difference is the setting: <span class='gd-jp'>上は</span> is stiffer and common in writing, speeches, and solemn declarations. <span class='gd-jp'>からには</span> also works in formal settings, and you hear it more in everyday conversation.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>上に</b>: It differs by just one particle, but the meaning is completely different: \"not only ~ but also ~.\"<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は安い上に、おいしい。</span> (This place is not only cheap, it's also delicious.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>店長である上は、遅刻はできない。</span> (Since I'm the store manager, I can't be late.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if the second half adds one more plus or minus, use 上に. If it says \"so I should, I must, I have no choice,\" use 上は. When talking with friends, think of からには first for \"since ~.\"</div>","n2-36":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ずにはいられない」 means <b>\"can't help ~ing\"</b> or <b>\"can't help but ~\"</b>. A feeling or urge wells up inside you, you can't hold it down, and you end up doing it. Literally it's \"if I don't do ~, I can't stay put,\" which is another way of saying \"I couldn't help doing it.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A feeling wells up</span>You hear or see something, and a certain feeling rises on its own.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この歌を聞くと、昔を思い出さずにはいられない。</span>(Every time I hear this song, I can't help remembering the old days.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の努力には、感心せずにはいられない。</span>(Seeing how hard he works, I can't help but admire him.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't stop yourself from speaking or acting</span>You could have held back, but you just can't stop yourself.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>困っている人を見ると、声をかけずにはいられない。</span>(When I see someone in trouble, I can't help going up to ask if they're okay.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あまりにひどい話で、一言言わずにはいられなかった。</span>(It was such an awful thing that I couldn't help saying something.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it: first check which verb group</div><div class='gd-sec'>First put the verb into its 「ない」 form, then choose which version to attach:<br>・<b>ず version</b>: drop 「ない」 and add <span class='gd-jp'>ずにはいられない</span><br>・<b>ないで version</b>: keep 「ない」 and add <span class='gd-jp'>ではいられない</span><br>The meaning is the same. The ず version is more common and more written in tone; the ないで version is a little more conversational.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 1 (godan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泣く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泣かない</span></span> → drop ない → <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>泣か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>泣かずにはいられない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言わずにはいられない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 2 (ichidan verbs)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べずにはいられない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べないではいられない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Group 3 (memorize these)</b>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>せずにはいられない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>心配する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>心配せずにはいられない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来ずにはいられない</span>（こずには）</span><br>The ないで version just uses the regular ない form: <span class='gd-jp'>心配しないではいられない</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The ず form of する is <b>せず</b>, not しず: <span class='gd-jp'>心配せずには</span> is correct. But the ないで version is <span class='gd-jp'>心配しないでは</span>, so don't mix the two.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>来ずには</span> is read <b>こずには</b>, not きずには.<br>・The ない form of <span class='gd-jp'>言う</span> is <span class='gd-jp'>言わない</span>, so it's <span class='gd-jp'>言わずには</span>, not 言あずには.<br>・With all those negatives, it's tempting to translate it as \"can't not ~,\" which sounds like someone is forcing you. In fact it's about <b>not being able to stop yourself</b>, so \"couldn't help doing it\" is the most accurate way to remember it.<br>・The subject is usually <b>I</b>. To talk about someone else, add <span class='gd-jp'>ようだ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>らしい</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>彼は文句を言わずにはいられないようだ。</span>(He seems unable to stop himself from complaining.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ざるを得ない</b> = you don't want to, but <b>circumstances force you</b>, so you have no choice:<br><span class='gd-jp'>部長の冗談は全然おもしろくないけど、笑わざるを得なかった。</span>(The department head's joke wasn't funny at all, but I had no choice but to laugh.)<br><b>ずにはいられない</b> = the feeling bubbles up and <b>you can't help yourself</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>友だちの冗談がおもしろすぎて、笑わずにはいられなかった。</span>(My friend's joke was so funny that I couldn't help laughing.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>てたまらない</b> = describes a very strong <b>inner feeling</b>, and you might still be holding back:<br><span class='gd-jp'>ケーキが食べたくてたまらない。</span>(I'm dying to eat cake.)<br><b>ずにはいられない</b> = describes an <b>action you can't stop</b>, and you've usually already done it:<br><span class='gd-jp'>ケーキを見ると、食べずにはいられない。</span>(The moment I see cake, I can't help eating it.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: \"couldn't help doing it\" is ずにはいられない, \"didn't want to but had to\" is ざるを得ない, and \"feeling it so much I can't stand it\" is てたまらない.</div>","n2-38":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜かねない」 means <b>\"if this keeps up, you might end up ~ing\"</b>, and what follows is always something <b>bad</b>. It's like saying in English, \"Keep pulling all-nighters like that and you'll end up in trouble.\" It carries a note of reminder or warning.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Warning about a bad outcome</span>You often state a cause or condition first (<span class='gd-jp'>〜と</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>〜ば</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>このままでは</span>), then use かねない to say what bad thing might happen.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>無理を続けると、体を壊しかねない。</span>(If you keep pushing yourself, you could end up ruining your health.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんな言い方をしたら、誤解されかねないよ。</span>(If you put it like that, you might get misunderstood, you know.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>That person is capable of it</span>You say that someone really is the type who would do something like that, with a negative judgment.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの人なら、約束を忘れかねない。</span>(Knowing him, he might actually forget the appointment.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First put the verb into the ます form, drop 「ます」, then add 「かねない」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>壊す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>壊します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>壊しかねない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れかねない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>誤解する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>誤解します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>誤解しかねない</span></span><br>To make it polite, use <b>かねません</b>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このままでは、計画が中止になりかねません。</span>(If things stay as they are, the plan could end up being canceled.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It ends in 「ない」, but it <b>doesn't mean \"won't\"</b>. It means \"<b>might</b>.\" Don't automatically translate ない as \"not.\"<br>・Use it only for bad things. For good things, use かもしれない: ×<span class='gd-jp'>合格しかねない</span> → ○<span class='gd-jp'>合格するかもしれない</span> (might pass).<br>・It attaches only to the ます form with ます dropped, not to the dictionary form or the ない form: ×<span class='gd-jp'>壊すかねない</span>, ×<span class='gd-jp'>壊さないかねない</span>.<br>・The tone is fairly formal and is common in news, company reports, and doctors' warnings. In casual chat with friends, people more often say <span class='gd-jp'>〜かもしれない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>〜ちゃうかも</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>かねる</b> = \"(because of one's position) can't ~, not in a position to ~.\" It's often a shop clerk or customer service rep politely declining.<br><b>かねない</b> = \"might end up ~ing (something bad).\"<br>Say someone has been asked a sensitive question:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その質問にはお答えしかねます。</span>(I'm afraid I can't answer that question.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>その質問に答えたら、誤解されかねない。</span>(If I answer that question, I might get misunderstood.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>かもしれない</b> = a plain \"maybe,\" usable for good or bad things, with a neutral tone. <b>かねない</b> is only for bad things and adds a \"watch out\" feeling of warning.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日は晴れるかもしれない。</span>(It might clear up tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>このまま遊んでいたら、試験に落ちかねない。</span>(If you keep goofing off like this, you could end up failing the exam.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: \"can't ~, I'm afraid I'm unable to ~\" is かねる (it has no ない, yet it means \"can't\"); \"if this goes on, something bad might happen\" is かねない; and a \"maybe\" that doesn't care about good or bad is かもしれない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜得る</b> also means \"it's possible,\" but it works for good and bad alike and sounds more objective: <span class='gd-jp'>これは誰にでも起こり得ることだ。</span>(This is something that could happen to anyone.)</div>","n2-39":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜得る」 means <b>\"it's possible that ~\"</b>, and 「〜得ない」 means <b>\"it's impossible that ~\"</b>. The tone is like \"it could happen\" or \"it can't happen\" in English news and reports, so it's fairly formal. The one you've probably heard most is the line from Japanese dramas, <span class='gd-jp'>ありえない！</span>, meaning \"No way!\" or \"You've got to be kidding!\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its ます form, drop ます, and add 「得る」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>起こります</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>起こり得る</span></span> (can happen)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>あります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>あり得る</span></span> (can exist / is possible)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>考えます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>考え得る</span></span> (conceivable)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>し得る</span></span> (can do / is possible to do)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to read it: only one place where you have a choice</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Only the base form 「得る」</b> (at the end of a sentence or before a noun) can be read either <b>うる</b> or <b>える</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>あり得る</span> can be ありうる or ありえる, and both are correct. In news and writing, ありうる is more common.<br><b>Every other form is always read with 「え」</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>得ない</span> (えない), <span class='gd-jp'>得ます</span> (えます), <span class='gd-jp'>得た</span> (えた).</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Possible</span>You state objectively that something could happen. It works for good things and bad things alike.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>誰にでも起こり得る問題だ。</span> (This is a problem that could happen to anyone.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>それは十分あり得る話だ。</span> (That's entirely possible.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Impossible</span>You declare that something cannot happen.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんなことは現実的にあり得ない。</span> (Something like that can't happen in reality.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Conceivable / doable</span>Often appears together with 「限り」 or 「すべて」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>考え得る方法は全部試した。</span> (I tried every method I could think of.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Casual</span><span class='gd-jp'>ありえない</span> is the exception. Friends use it all the time in conversation to show shock or disbelief.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>え、また遅刻？ありえない！</span> (What, late again? No way!)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It talks about <b>whether something can happen</b>, not whether a person has a skill. \"I can swim\" is <span class='gd-jp'>泳げる</span>, never 泳ぎ得る.<br>・The negative is only read えない: it's <span class='gd-jp'>ありえない</span>, and there is no such reading as 「ありうない」.<br>・Apart from <span class='gd-jp'>ありえない</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>ありえる</span>, forms like <span class='gd-jp'>起こり得る</span> lean toward written language. In everyday chat, <span class='gd-jp'>起こるかもしれない</span> sounds more natural.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>ざるを得ない</span> also contains 「得ない」, but the meaning is completely different: <span class='gd-jp'>行かざるを得ない</span> means \"have no choice but to go\", not \"it's impossible to go\".<br>・When 「得る」 stands alone as a verb, it's read える and means \"to obtain\", as in <span class='gd-jp'>賞を得る</span> (win a prize). That's a different usage from this one.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>得る vs かねない</b>: Both mean \"it could happen\", but かねない is used only when you're <b>worried something bad will happen</b>.<br><span class='gd-jp'>このままでは事故が起こり得る。</span> (If this goes on, an accident could happen: calm statement)<br><span class='gd-jp'>このままでは事故が起こりかねない。</span> (If this goes on, we might end up with an accident: said with worry)<br>For good things, only 得る works: <span class='gd-jp'>努力すれば誰でも成功し得る。</span> (With effort, anyone can succeed.) Swap in かねない here and it's wrong.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>得ない vs っこない</b>: Both mean \"impossible\", but っこない is a very casual, <b>subjective</b> \"as if that could ever happen\".<br><span class='gd-jp'>一日で終わることはあり得ない。</span> (It's impossible to finish in one day: formal, objective)<br><span class='gd-jp'>一日で終わりっこないよ。</span> (There's no way I can finish that in a day: complaining to a friend)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for an objective \"possible / impossible\", use 得る・得ない. For worry about something bad, use かねない. For \"as if!\" with a friend, use っこない.</div>","n2-40":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜っこない」 means <b>\"no way ~\" or \"as if ~ could ever happen\"</b>. The speaker has already decided \"this will absolutely never happen.\" The tone is strong and very casual, often carrying a hint of \"come on\" or \"get real.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんなこと、できっこないよ。</span> (There's no way anyone could do that!)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to its ます form, <b>drop ます</b>, and add 「っこない」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>できます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>できっこない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>間に合います</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>間に合いっこない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>分かります</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>分かりっこない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>勝てます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>勝てっこない</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Can't be done</span>It pairs most often with 「できる」, 「分かる」, 「間に合う」, or verbs about whether you can pull something off, like 「勝てる」, 「受かる」, 「取れる」. It means \"there's simply no way.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あんな強いチームに勝てっこない。</span> (There's no way we can beat a team that strong.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今から走っても間に合いっこないよ。</span> (Even if we run now, there's no way we'll make it.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>説明したって、あの人には分かりっこない。</span> (Even if I explain it, there's no way that person will get it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Someone wouldn't do that</span>When you talk about someone else's behavior, it means \"there's no way they'd do that.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの頑固な父が謝りっこない。</span> (My stubborn dad would never apologize, no way.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Attach it to the <b>form with ます dropped</b>, not the dictionary form: say <span class='gd-jp'>分かりっこない</span>, never 「分かるっこない」.<br>・「っこない」 is already negative, so don't put ない before it.<br>・It attaches only to verbs, not directly to adjectives or nouns.<br>・「っこ」 has a small っ, so pause for a beat before 「こ」. Don't read it as 「こない」, or it will sound like 「来ない」.<br>・This is how friends talk, and it's blunt. It isn't suitable for your boss or customers, or for written reports.<br>・Saying 「<span class='gd-jp'>君にはできっこない</span>」 (\"There's no way you can do it\") to someone is like looking down on them to their face. It really stings, so use it carefully.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to say the same thing: \"With this much work, there's no way it'll be done in a day.\"<br><span class='gd-jp'>一日で終わりっこない。</span> (<b>っこない</b>: casual and emotional, like complaining)<br><span class='gd-jp'>一日で終わるわけがない。</span> (<b>わけがない</b>: \"how could it be\", works in speech and writing)<br><span class='gd-jp'>一日で終わるはずがない。</span> (<b>はずがない</b>: \"impossible\" by logical deduction, calmer)<br>Note that the attachment differs: わけがない and はずがない attach to the dictionary form (<span class='gd-jp'>終わる</span>), while っこない attaches to the form with ます dropped (<span class='gd-jp'>終わり</span>).</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: when you're grumbling or venting to a friend, use 「っこない」. When you want to sound more formal, switch to 「わけがない」 or 「はずがない」.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The opposite meaning is 「〜に決まっている」 = \"it's bound to ~, no doubt about it\": <span class='gd-jp'>そんなの、失敗するに決まっている。</span> (Doing it that way is bound to fail.)</div>","n2-42":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に決まっている」 means <b>\"It's definitely ~, obviously\"</b>. The speaker has already made up their mind and sounds completely sure, with a hint of \"Isn't that obvious?\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: attach directly. Past and negative forms work too.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くに決まっている</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いに決まっている</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>忘れた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>忘れたに決まっている</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: <b>don't add だ</b>. Attach に directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>嘘</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>嘘に決まっている</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>無理</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>無理に決まっている</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>In casual conversation it's often shortened to <span class='gd-jp'>〜に決まってる</span>. The politer form is <span class='gd-jp'>〜に決まっています</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Obviously so</span>You don't need evidence. You're simply sure it must be this way.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こんな時間に電話なんて、急用に決まってる。</span>(A call at this hour? It's obviously something urgent.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんなに安いのは偽物に決まってるよ。</span>(Something that cheap? It's a fake, obviously.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Of course</span>Someone asks about something obvious, and you answer \"Well, duh.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>「一緒に行く？」「行くに決まってるじゃん！」</span>(\"Want to go together?\" \"Of course I'm going!\")</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>10時間も歩いたんだから、疲れたに決まってるでしょ。</span>(You walked for 10 hours. Of course you're tired.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Don't add だ after nouns or な-adjectives: <span class='gd-jp'>嘘だに決まっている</span> is wrong. Say <span class='gd-jp'>嘘に決まっている</span>.<br>・This is the <b>speaker's own subjective conviction</b>, and it sounds strong. Saying 「〜に決まっています」 to a boss or a customer can come across as presumptuous, even confrontational. It's also rarely used in formal writing or reports.<br>・In English, \"it has to be\" or \"must\" can also express obligation (\"you must come\"), but に決まっている <b>never means that</b>. To say \"Be sure to come tomorrow,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>明日は必ず来てください</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>決まっている</span> originally means \"has already been decided.\" So sometimes it's meant literally: <span class='gd-jp'>会議は月曜日に決まっている。</span> (The meeting has been set for Monday.) Check the context to tell which one it is.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: your friend still hasn't shown up.<br><b>に違いない</b> = an inference based on some grounds. It sounds calm and works in both speech and writing:<br><span class='gd-jp'>道が混んでいるに違いない。</span> (The roads must be jammed.)<br><b>に決まっている</b> = more subjective and more emotional, used almost only in conversation:<br><span class='gd-jp'>道が混んでいるに決まってるよ。</span> (The roads are jammed, obviously.)<br><b>に相違ない</b> = same meaning as に違いない, but stiff, mostly found in writing and novels:<br><span class='gd-jp'>道が混んでいるに相違ない。</span> (The roads are undoubtedly congested.)<br>The opposite direction is <b>っこない</b> = \"no way that ~,\" which is also casual and subjective:<br><span class='gd-jp'>あの人が時間通りに来っこないよ。</span> (There's no way he'll come on time.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: for calm reasoning use に違いない, for writing use に相違ない, and for a \"Obviously!\" or \"Of course!\" tone use に決まっている. To say \"no way,\" use っこない.</div>","n2-44":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ものだ」 means <b>\"How ~ it is!\" or \"Back in the day…\"</b>. It doesn't report facts. It speaks with <b>a reflective, life-experience feeling</b>, like an older person looking at a photo and saying, \"Time really flies, doesn't it?\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs in plain form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>変わる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>変わるものだ</span></span><br>い-adjectives: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>早い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>早いものだ</span></span><br>な-adjectives keep 「な」: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>静か</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>静かなものだ</span></span><br>For things in the past, use the た form: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遊んだ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遊んだものだ</span></span><br>In speech it's often shortened to <b>もんだ</b>, and <b>なあ</b> is often added at the end: <span class='gd-jp'>早いもんだなあ。</span> For a politer tone, use <span class='gd-jp'>ものです</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reflecting on life</span>A sigh about time, life, or change.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>月日がたつのは早いものだ。</span>(How quickly time passes!)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この町もずいぶん変わったものだ。</span>(This town has changed so much, hasn't it?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Nostalgia</span>た form + ものだ, often together with 「よく」, talks about things you <b>used to do often</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供の頃はよくこの川で遊んだものだ。</span>(As a kid, I used to play in this river all the time.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>学生時代は朝まで友達と話したものだ。</span>(In my student days, I'd often talk with friends until morning.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Admiration or disbelief</span>「よく～たものだ」 = \"how could you possibly ~\" or \"you actually managed to ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>よくそんなことが言えたものだ。</span>(I can't believe you had the nerve to say that.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The nostalgic use only works for things you <b>did repeatedly in the past</b>. It can't be used for something that happened once: <span class='gd-jp'>去年京都へ行ったものだ</span> is wrong. Just say <span class='gd-jp'>去年京都へ行った</span>.<br>・「よく」 has two meanings: in <span class='gd-jp'>よく遊んだものだ</span>, よく means \"often\"; in <span class='gd-jp'>よく言えたものだ</span>, よく means \"how could you / actually.\" Check the context.<br>・For small things right in front of you, like the weather being hot or the food being good, plain <span class='gd-jp'>暑いなあ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>おいしいね</span> is enough. Forcing ものだ in sounds like you're giving a speech on the meaning of life.<br>・You can't put 「だ」 between a な-adjective and ものだ: <span class='gd-jp'>静かだものだ</span> is wrong.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ものだ (naturally ~ / should ~)</b>: states general truths or rules, with little emotion.<br><span class='gd-jp'>年をとると、物忘れが多くなるものだ。</span> (As people get older, they naturally get more forgetful.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>人の話は最後まで聞くものだ。</span> (You should listen to what people say until the end.)<br><b>ものだ (exclamation)</b>: the same form, but with a feeling of reflection.<br><span class='gd-jp'>子供が大きくなるのは本当に早いものだ。</span> (Kids really do grow up fast.)<br><b>ものがある</b>: something makes you <b>feel a certain quality</b>. It's used to evaluate works, people, and performances.<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼の歌には人の心を打つものがある。</span> (There's something in his songs that moves people.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: if you can add 「本当に」 or 「なあ」 and it still reads as heartfelt, it's the exclamation. If it calmly states a principle or rule, it's \"naturally ~.\" If you're saying \"something has a certain quality,\" it's ものがある.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Next, look at <span class='gd-jp'>〜ものがある</span>, then go back and review the \"naturally ~\" <span class='gd-jp'>ものだ</span>. Comparing all three side by side is the best way to avoid mixing them up.</div>","n2-45":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ぶり」 has two <b>completely different</b> uses:<br>① After an action = <b>\"the way someone does it, how they go about it\"</b>, like English \"his work performance\" or \"the way she talks.\"<br>② After a length of time = <b>\"for the first time in ~, after a gap of ~\"</b>. It's the ぶり in <span class='gd-jp'>久しぶり</span>, which you already know.</div><div class='gd-h'>Use ①: the way someone does something</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>How to attach it</span>Add ぶり directly to a noun: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>仕事</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>仕事ぶり</span></span> (work performance)<br>For verbs, change to the ます form, drop ます, then add ぶり: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>話します</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>話しぶり</span></span> (way of speaking) <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>暮らします</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>暮らしぶり</span></span> (way of living)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の仕事ぶりはみんなに認められている。</span>(Everyone recognizes how well he works.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女の話しぶりから、自信を感じた。</span>(I could sense her confidence from the way she spoke.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>駅の混雑ぶりに驚いた。</span>(I was shocked at how crowded the station was.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's mostly used to describe or evaluate how <b>someone else or a certain situation</b> comes across. A few words are usually pronounced with 「っぷり」, such as <span class='gd-jp'>食べっぷり</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>飲みっぷり</span>:</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いい食べっぷりだね。</span>(You really dig in, don't you!)</div><div class='gd-h'>Use ②: again after a gap</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>How to attach it</span>Length of time + ぶり. It's often followed by 「に + verb」, 「の + noun」, or simply 「だ／です」.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>5年ぶりに母校を訪れた。</span>(I visited my old school for the first time in five years.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>10年ぶりの再会だった。</span>(It was a reunion after ten years.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>久しぶりの休みだ。</span>(Finally, a day off after a long time.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>田中さん、3年ぶりですね。</span>(Mr. Tanaka, it's been three years!)</div><div class='gd-sec'>The key point: during that stretch of time it <b>didn't happen</b>, and now it <b>has happened again</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Use ② can't be followed by a negative. To say \"I haven't seen them in three years,\" you can't say <span class='gd-jp'>3年ぶりに会わなかった</span>. Say <span class='gd-jp'>3年間会っていない</span>.<br>・Use ② always means \"it happened before, and now it's happening <b>again</b>.\" If you're doing something for the first time, there's no \"again,\" so you can't use ぶり.<br>・Use ① doesn't attach to just any word. The common ones are few: <span class='gd-jp'>仕事ぶり</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>話しぶり</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>暮らしぶり</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>生活ぶり</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>活躍ぶり</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>混雑ぶり</span>. The most practical approach is to memorize them as whole vocabulary items.<br>・Verbs attach in the ます-stem form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>話しぶり</span>, not 話すぶり.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the same \"after graduation\" situation:<br><b>て以来</b> = a state has continued <b>without a break</b> since then: <span class='gd-jp'>卒業して以来、彼には一度も会っていない。</span> (I haven't seen him even once since graduating.)<br><b>ぶり</b> = after all that time, it happened <b>again</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>卒業以来、5年ぶりに彼に会った。</span> (I saw him again for the first time in five years since graduating.)<br><b>後</b> = simply \"after ~,\" whether or not it had happened before: <span class='gd-jp'>卒業して5年後に結婚した。</span> (I got married five years after graduating.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: to say \"ever since then, it's been like this,\" use て以来. To say \"after a long gap, it happened again,\" use ぶり. To say just \"after ~,\" use 後.</div>","n2-46":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜がたい」 means <b>\"hard to ~\" or \"difficult to bring yourself to ~\"</b>. The point isn't that your ability falls short, but that <b>you can't get past it emotionally</b>: something is so outrageous that it's really hard to believe, accept, or forgive. The tone is fairly formal, and you see it a lot in news, writing, and formal settings.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to attach it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Change the verb to the ます form, drop ます, then add がたい.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>信じる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>信じます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>信じがたい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>許す</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>許します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>許しがたい</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>理解する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>理解します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>理解しがたい</span></span><br>Once attached, the whole word behaves like an い-adjective: you can put it before a noun or change it to the past tense.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>信じがたい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>信じがたかった</span></span> (it was hard to believe)</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>At the end of a sentence</span>Directly says \"I really find it hard to ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼のあの行動は今でも理解しがたい。</span>(I still find that behavior of his hard to understand.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この結果は受け入れがたい。</span>(This result is hard to accept.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Before a noun</span>Used as an adjective. This is the most common form, and many of these are already set phrases.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>それは信じがたい話だ。</span>(That's a hard-to-believe story.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>留学は忘れがたい経験になった。</span>(Studying abroad became an unforgettable experience.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>許しがたい行為だ。</span>(It's an unforgivable act.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Other common ones: <span class='gd-jp'>想像しがたい</span> (hard to imagine), <span class='gd-jp'>言いがたい</span> (hard to say), <span class='gd-jp'>得がたい</span> (rare and precious), <span class='gd-jp'>耐えがたい</span> (unbearable).</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches to the <b>ます form minus ます</b>, not the dictionary form: say <span class='gd-jp'>信じがたい</span>, not 信じるがたい.<br>・Don't use がたい when <b>the thing itself is hard to use or do</b>. A pen that's hard to write with is <span class='gd-jp'>このペンは書きにくい</span>, not 書きがたい.<br>・The tone is stiff and formal. Chatting with friends, <span class='gd-jp'>信じられない</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>許せない</span> sound more natural; 信じがたい would sound a bit like reading from a script.<br>・In writing it's often spelled with the kanji 「難い」, which is read <b>がたい</b> here.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling similar patterns apart</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にくい</b> = the thing itself is <b>hard to ~</b>, physically or technically difficult:<br><span class='gd-jp'>この説明は分かりにくい。</span> (This explanation is hard to follow: it's unclear.)<br><b>がたい</b> = <b>emotionally hard</b> to accept, believe, or forgive:<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼の考えは理解しがたい。</span> (I really can't understand his way of thinking: I can't get on board with it.)<br><b>かねる</b> = from your own position, you <b>can't comfortably do</b> something. Often used to decline politely:<br><span class='gd-jp'>その件にはお答えしかねます。</span> (I'm afraid I can't answer that matter.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: for something awkward to use or understand, use にくい. For something you can't get past emotionally, use がたい. To politely turn someone down, use かねる.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The <span class='gd-jp'>ありがたい</span> you say every day actually comes from 「有り難い」. It originally meant \"rare, hard to come by,\" and only later came to mean \"worth being thankful for.\"</div>","n2-47":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜かける」 means <b>'halfway through ~'</b>: halfway through a book, halfway through a meal, halfway through a sentence. The action has started but isn't finished, so it stops midway. After certain verbs it takes on the meaning <b>'almost ~' or 'about to ~'</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the verb's ます form, drop 「ます」, and add 「かける」 or 「かけの」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>読みます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>読みかける</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べかける</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>しかける</span></span><br>「かける」 itself conjugates as a Group 2 (ichidan) verb: <span class='gd-jp'>かけた</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>かけて</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>かけている</span>.<br>To modify a noun, use <b>かけの + noun</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>飲みかけのコーヒー</span> (a half-finished coffee).<br>To end a sentence, use <b>かけだ</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>この本はまだ読みかけだ。</span> (I haven't finished this book yet.)</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Halfway through</span>Attach it to actions you do deliberately, like reading, eating, writing, or speaking, to show you stopped partway.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>読みかけの小説が本棚に積まれている。</span> (Half-read novels are piled up on the bookshelf.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何か言いかけて、やめた。</span> (He started to say something, then stopped.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>飲みかけのコーヒー、捨ててもいい？</span> (Is it okay to throw out this half-drunk coffee?)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Almost, about to</span>Attach it to sudden changes like dying, drowning, going out, or forgetting. It means 'just about to happen', or 'it nearly happened, but didn't'.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供のころ、海で溺れかけたことがある。</span> (When I was a kid, I almost drowned in the sea.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>道で死にかけた猫を見つけた。</span> (I found a cat on the road that was close to death.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忘れかけていた夢を思い出した。</span> (I remembered a dream I had almost forgotten.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<span class='gd-jp'>話しかける</span> does not mean 'to stop halfway through speaking'. It means <b>'to speak to someone, to strike up a conversation'</b>. For 'stop halfway through saying something', use <span class='gd-jp'>言いかける</span>. <span class='gd-jp'>呼びかける</span> (to appeal to, call out to) and <span class='gd-jp'>出かける</span> (to go out) are also fixed words and have nothing to do with 'halfway'.<br>・To modify a noun, 「かけの」 is the most common form: <span class='gd-jp'>食べかけのパン</span> (a half-eaten piece of bread). Don't write 「食べかけパン」.<br>・Verbs of 'existence or state', like <span class='gd-jp'>ある</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>いる</span>, have no start or end, so they can't take かける.<br>・The same 「かける」 can mean 'halfway' or 'almost'. The verb before it tells you which: <span class='gd-jp'>死にかける</span> always means 'nearly die', never 'halfway dead'.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Compare them in the same situation:<br><b>かける</b> = already <b>close to that point</b>, or stopped midway: <span class='gd-jp'>火が消えかけている。</span> (The fire is about to go out, with just a little left.)<br><b>つつある</b> = <b>changing little by little</b>, with the focus on the process of change: <span class='gd-jp'>火が消えつつある。</span> (The fire is gradually dying down.)<br>Also, <span class='gd-jp'>つつある</span> is mostly used in writing and news reports, while <span class='gd-jp'>かける</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>かけの</span> are very common in everyday speech.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: to say 'stopped halfway' or 'almost ~', use かける. To say 'gradually changing', use つつある.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Its opposite is <span class='gd-jp'>〜きる</span> (to finish, to do all the way through): <span class='gd-jp'>一晩で読みきった。</span> (I read it all in one night.)</div>","n2-48":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ことなく」 is like the formal, written-style <b>'without ever ~'</b>, <b>'never ~'</b>, <b>'not even once ~'</b>. It means the same as 「〜ないで」 (without doing ~, ...) but is much more formal, and often appears in news, articles, and speeches.<br>What comes before it is <b>the thing that did not happen</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>休むことなく</span> = without resting, without stopping.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Add ことなく directly to the dictionary form of the verb (the base form you find in a dictionary).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>休む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>休むことなく</span></span> (without resting)<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>迷う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>迷うことなく</span></span> (without hesitation)<br>Noun + する verbs work the same way: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>遅刻する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>遅刻することなく</span></span> (without being late)<br>Passive forms can also be used: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>気づかれる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>気づかれることなく</span></span> (without being noticed)<br><b>ことなく itself has no past or present tense</b>. The tense comes from the end of the sentence.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll most often see it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>一度も・一日も</span>Put these words before it to stress 'not even once'.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は一日も休むことなく働き続けた。</span> (He kept working without taking a single day off.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Long-term persistence</span>Use it to say something stayed the same or went on uninterrupted for a long time.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この店は40年間、変わることなく同じ味を守っている。</span> (For 40 years, this restaurant has kept the same flavor without change.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Expressing resolve</span>Very popular in speeches and award acceptance remarks.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>これからも初心を忘れることなく、頑張ってまいります。</span> (From now on, too, I will keep working hard without forgetting my original resolve.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼女は迷うことなく、この道を選んだ。</span> (She chose this path without hesitation.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches to the <b>affirmative</b> dictionary form, not the ない form: ○<span class='gd-jp'>休むことなく</span>　×休まないことなく.<br>・When you see 「休む」, don't think 'did rest'. ことなく negates the whole preceding action.<br>・You can't drop する from noun + する verbs: ×連絡ことなく　○<span class='gd-jp'>連絡することなく</span>.<br>・This expression is stiff. To a friend, just say <span class='gd-jp'>食べないで出かけた</span> for 'went out without eating breakfast'. Using ことなく would sound like an official document.<br>・It's usually used for things that might have happened but didn't (didn't rest even though tired, didn't hesitate even though you might have). So it often carries a sense of 'determined' or 'admirable'.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'>Here are three ways to say 'He worked without resting':<br><span class='gd-jp'>休まないで働いた。</span> (Casual; the most common in conversation.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>休まずに働いた。</span> (Slightly formal; fine in both speech and writing.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>休むことなく働いた。</span> (The most literary, with a weight of 'consistently, throughout'.)<br>The meaning is the same; the difference is the <b>level of formality</b>.</div><div class='gd-sec'>But 「ないで」 has another use: <b>not doing A, doing B instead</b>. In that case, ことなく is usually not used.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>電車に乗らないで、歩いて帰った。</span> (I didn't take the train; I walked home instead.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Changing this sentence to 「電車に乗ることなく」 would sound very unnatural.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick tip: use ないで for casual talk and ずに for a bit more formality. Use ことなく when you want a news or speech tone, or to stress 'not even once'. For 'not A, but B instead', stick with ないで or ずに.</div>","n2-49":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ようがない」means <b>\"there's simply no way to ~\"</b> or <b>\"I can't even begin to ~\"</b>. The 「よう」 here means \"method\", so 「ようがない」 means <b>there's no method at all</b>. It's not that you don't want to, and it's not that you lack the ability. The situation just leaves you no way in.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Missing conditions or means</span>You want to do something, but a key thing (information, a tool, a clue) is missing, so you have no way to get started.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>住所も電話番号も知らないので、連絡しようがない。</span>(I don't know the address or the phone number, so there's no way to contact them.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この部品はもう売っていないから、直しようがないね。</span>(They don't sell this part anymore, so there's no way to fix it.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ようもない</span>With an extra 「も」 in the middle, it's stronger, with the feel of \"not even the slightest way.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>道が雪で埋まっていて、進みようもなかった。</span>(The road was buried in snow, so there was no way to move forward at all.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's common in both writing and speech. Saying 「<span class='gd-jp'>直しようがないよ</span>」 to a friend sounds perfectly natural.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the verb's ます form, drop ます, and add 「ようがない」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>書きます</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>書きようがない</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べようがない</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>連絡します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>連絡しようがない</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun + のしようがない</span>With nouns like 「連絡、説明、返事」, you'll often hear 「noun + の + しようがない」 too.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>説明しようがない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>説明のしようがない</span></span> (both are correct)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Before a noun</span>「が」 often becomes 「の」, giving 「ようのない + noun」. 「ようがない + noun」 is also fine.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>言いようのない寂しさを感じた。</span>(I felt a loneliness I couldn't put into words.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'>In speech, these set phrases come up most:<br><span class='gd-jp'>どうしようもない</span>: nothing can be done, whatever you try. You can also use it to call someone hopeless.<br><span class='gd-jp'>しょうがない</span>: can't be helped, oh well. It's really just 「<span class='gd-jp'>しようがない</span>」 said fast.<br><span class='gd-jp'>言いようがない</span>: (so good or so bad that) it's beyond words.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>雨じゃしょうがないね。</span>(If it's raining, there's nothing we can do.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It attaches to the <b>ます form</b>, not the volitional form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>書きようがない</span>, not 「書こうがない」.<br>・The 「しよう」 in <span class='gd-jp'>連絡しようがない</span> looks just like the しよう of \"let's do it\", but it's actually 「し + よう (method)」. The meaning is completely different.<br>・It talks about <b>having no method or means</b>, not about ability. \"I can't swim\" is <span class='gd-jp'>泳げない</span>; you can't say 「泳ぎようがない」.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: you're asked a question and can't answer.<br><b>Negative potential form</b> = a plain \"can't\", for any reason at all: <span class='gd-jp'>その質問には答えられない。</span> (I can't answer that question.)<br><b>ようがない</b> = you have no information or basis, so there's <b>no way</b> to answer: <span class='gd-jp'>何も聞いていないので、答えようがない。</span> (I haven't been told anything, so there's no way I can answer.)<br><b>かねる</b> = you actually could answer, but <b>your position makes it awkward</b>. Customer service staff and shop clerks use it to refuse politely: <span class='gd-jp'>その件についてはお答えしかねます。</span> (I'm afraid we're unable to answer regarding that matter.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use ようがない for \"no method, nowhere to start\"; use かねる for \"my position makes this awkward, so I'm politely declining\"; and use the negative potential form for a plain \"can't\" with any reason.</div>","n2-51":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜際（に）」is the <b>formal version</b> of \"when ~\". Think of the gap between English \"when you leave\" and \"upon leaving\". Japanese station announcements, shop notices and company emails use it a lot. It sounds more polite than 「時」 and more like an official notice.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Notices and announcements</span>Speaking to customers or the public, reminding them what to watch out for when they do something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>お降りの際は、足元にご注意ください。</span>(Please watch your step when getting off.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>ご利用の際は、こちらのカードをお見せください。</span>(Please show this card when you use the service.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Business and formal situations</span>Writing letters, or speaking to a superior or a client.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先日お会いした際に、いろいろ教えていただきました。</span>(When we met the other day, you taught me a great deal.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: add 「の」 and then 際.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>入場</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>入場の際</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>申し込み</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>申し込みの際</span></span><br><b>Verbs</b>: attach 際 directly to the dictionary form or the た form.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>来る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>来る際</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会った際</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'>The difference between the dictionary form and the た form is the same as with 「時」: the dictionary form is for the time <b>before you do it, when you're about to</b>; the た form is for <b>after it's done, that one occasion</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行く際に、パスポートを作りました。</span>(I got a passport when I was going to Japan: before departure.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本へ行った際に、友達に会いました。</span>(I met a friend when I went to Japan: after arriving.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Following it with 「に」 is the plain \"at the time of ~\". Switching to 「は」 adds a reminder feel, like \"in this situation, please...\", which is why notices so often write 「〜の際は」. Both are correct.</div><div class='gd-h'>Forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'>「お／ご + noun + の際」: the お or ご in front shows respect to the customer, so just memorize each phrase as a set.<br><span class='gd-jp'>お帰りの際</span> (when you head home), <span class='gd-jp'>お降りの際</span> (when you get off), <span class='gd-jp'>ご来店の際</span> (when you visit the store), <span class='gd-jp'>ご利用の際</span> (when you use it)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・A noun must be followed by 「の」: it's <span class='gd-jp'>入場の際</span>, not 入場際.<br>・際 is read <b>さい</b>.<br>・Don't use it in casual chat. For \"when I ate yesterday\", just say <span class='gd-jp'>昨日ご飯を食べた時</span>. Saying 「食べた際」 would sound like you're writing a report.<br>・際 refers to <b>the moment you do a certain action</b>. It isn't used for a period of time or a state: \"when I was a kid\" is <span class='gd-jp'>子どもの時</span>, not 子どもの際. Adjectives don't normally take 際 either; \"when I'm busy\" is <span class='gd-jp'>忙しい時</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling look-alikes apart</div><div class='gd-sec'>Same situation: logging in to a website.<br><b>時</b> = the general expression, usable anywhere: <span class='gd-jp'>ログインする時、パスワードを入れます。</span> (When you log in, you enter your password.)<br><b>際</b> = the formal expression, common in notices and instructions: <span class='gd-jp'>ログインの際は、パスワードを入力してください。</span> (Please enter your password when logging in.)<br><b>場合</b> = \"in the case that ~\", for something not everyone will run into: <span class='gd-jp'>パスワードを忘れた場合は、こちらをクリックしてください。</span> (If you forget your password, please click here.)<br>Everyone has to enter a password to log in, so it's 際; only some people forget their password, so it's 場合.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: use 「時」 when chatting with friends; use 「際」 for notices and when speaking to customers; use 「場合」 for \"in case ~ happens\".</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に先立って」 is also formal and means \"before ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>開会に先立って、社長が挨拶をしました。</span> (Before the meeting began, the president gave a greeting.)</div>","n2-52":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜からすると」 means <b>“from the standpoint of ~”</b> or <b>“judging from ~”</b>. Put a person before it and you step into that person’s shoes. Put a clue before it (a facial expression, someone’s appearance, grades) and you’re drawing a conclusion from that clue.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + <span class='gd-jp'>からすると</span> / <span class='gd-jp'>からすれば</span> / <span class='gd-jp'>からしたら</span><br>All three mean the same thing. <span class='gd-jp'>からしたら</span> is more colloquial, and you’ll hear it a lot in casual conversation.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>親</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>親からすると</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>彼の表情</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>彼の表情からすれば</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Taking someone’s standpoint</span>Put a person or group before it, for example <span class='gd-jp'>親</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>子供</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>外国人</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>会社</span>. It means “this is how it looks from that person’s side,” which may not match how you feel yourself.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親からすると、子供の将来を心配するのは自然なことだ。</span>(From a parent’s point of view, worrying about their child’s future is only natural.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供からしたら、大人の話はつまらないだろう。</span>(From a child’s point of view, grown-ups’ conversations are probably boring.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>外国人からすれば、敬語はかなり難しい。</span>(From a foreigner’s point of view, honorific language is quite difficult.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Judging from a clue</span>Put a visible clue before it, for example <span class='gd-jp'>表情</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>様子</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>話し方</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>成績</span>. The sentence often ends with <span class='gd-jp'>ようだ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>らしい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>はずだ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>だろう</span>, showing “this is my inference.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の表情からすると、何か隠しているようだ。</span>(Judging from his expression, he seems to be hiding something.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この成績からすれば、合格できるはずだ。</span>(Judging from these grades, you should be able to pass.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>空の様子からすると、午後は雨になりそうだ。</span>(Judging from the look of the sky, it looks like rain this afternoon.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The 「から」 here is <b>not “because”</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>親からすると</span> doesn’t mean “because of the parents”; it means “from the parents’ side.” Also don’t mix it up with <span class='gd-jp'>からには</span> (since ~, then ~) or <span class='gd-jp'>からこそ</span> (precisely because ~).<br>・Only a <b>noun</b> can come before it. If you want to use an action as the clue, turn it into a noun first, as in <span class='gd-jp'>彼の話し方からすると</span>. You can’t say 「話すからすると」 directly.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>すれば</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>したら</span> look like “if ~,” but here they <b>carry no hypothetical meaning</b>. All three simply mean “judging from ~.”<br>・The second half of the sentence gives a <b>view or judgment</b>. Don’t follow it with a decision or a request, such as “I’m going to ~” or “Please ~.”</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b><span class='gd-jp'>にとって</span></b> = <b>what something is to a person</b>. It is often followed by an evaluative word (<span class='gd-jp'>大切</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>ありがたい</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>難しい</span>).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供にとって、親は一番大切な存在だ。</span>(For a child, parents are the most important people.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b><span class='gd-jp'>からすると</span></b> = you step into that person’s position and say <b>how they would see or think about it</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子供からすると、親の注意はうるさいだけだろう。</span>(From a child’s point of view, a parent’s warnings are probably just nagging.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>Also, the “judging from a clue” use can’t be replaced with <span class='gd-jp'>にとって</span>. You wouldn’t say 「<span class='gd-jp'>表情にとって</span>」 (use <span class='gd-jp'>からすると</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>から見ると</span> instead).<br><br><b><span class='gd-jp'>から見ると</span></b> = mostly interchangeable with <span class='gd-jp'>からすると</span>, and it works for both uses: “from someone’s standpoint” and “judging from a clue.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>あの様子から見ると、何かあったらしい。</span>(Judging from how he looks, something seems to have happened.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to say what value or effect something has for a person, use <span class='gd-jp'>にとって</span>. To say “how it would look to that person” or to infer something from a clue, use <span class='gd-jp'>からすると</span>; <span class='gd-jp'>から見ると</span> usually works there too.</div>","n2-53":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にかけては」 means <b>“when it comes to ~, (someone) is really good.”</b> You name a field first, such as cooking, math, or sales, and then follow with praise like “no one can match them” or “they’re second to none.” The point of the whole sentence is that <b>this person is especially strong in this area</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Noun + にかけては<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>料理</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>料理にかけては</span></span> (when it comes to cooking)<br>If you want to talk about an action, first add 「こと」 to make it a noun:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>走る</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>走ることにかけては</span></span> (when it comes to running)</div><div class='gd-h'>The patterns you’ll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Praising someone else</span>It’s most often followed by 「<span class='gd-jp'>右に出る者はいない</span>」 (no one surpasses them) or 「<span class='gd-jp'>かなう人はいない</span>」 (no one can match them).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>和食にかけては、彼女の右に出る者はいない。</span>(When it comes to Japanese cooking, no one surpasses her.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>営業にかけては、彼が社内一だ。</span>(When it comes to sales, he’s number one in the company.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Talking about yourself</span>It’s followed by 「<span class='gd-jp'>誰にも負けない</span>」 (I won’t lose to anyone) or 「<span class='gd-jp'>自信がある</span>」 (I’m confident).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>記憶力にかけては、誰にも負けない。</span>(When it comes to memory, I won’t lose to anyone.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>人の名前を覚えることにかけては、自信がある。</span>(When it comes to remembering people’s names, I’m confident.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・What comes before it must be <b>a skill or a field someone is good at</b>, and what follows must be <b>an evaluation of strong ability</b>. A topic unrelated to ability, like 「<span class='gd-jp'>日本の天気にかけては</span>」, doesn’t work.<br>・If you use it with a weakness, as in “when it comes to making excuses, he’s a genius,” it becomes sarcastic or joking, not real praise.<br>・Don’t drop the 「は」. Without it you get 「にかけて」, which is read as a different grammar point that expresses a range of time or place.<br>・This expression is on the formal side, like when introducing someone or writing an essay. With friends, it’s more common to say 「<span class='gd-jp'>料理なら誰にも負けない</span>」 (if it’s cooking, I won’t lose to anyone).</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar forms</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にかけて</b> (without は) = a <b>range</b> from one point to another, with a time or place before it:<br><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今夜から明日の朝にかけて、雨が降るでしょう。</span>(It will probably rain from tonight through tomorrow morning.)</div><b>にかけては</b> = when it comes to a particular <b>skill</b>:<br><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>数学にかけては、クラスで彼に勝てる人はいない。</span>(When it comes to math, no one in the class can beat him.)</div></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>に関しては</b> = simply “regarding ~.” It just names the topic, and what follows can be anything:<br><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>料理に関しては、母に聞いてください。</span>(About cooking, please ask my mother.)</div><b>にかけては</b> = what follows is always praise of ability in that area:<br><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>料理にかけては、母にかなう人はいない。</span>(When it comes to cooking, no one can match my mother.)</div></div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if a time or place comes before it, use にかけて. If you’re just introducing a topic, use に関しては. Use にかけては only when you want to say “he’s the best in this area.”</div>","n2-58":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜とは限らない」means <b>\"not necessarily ~\"</b> or <b>\"doesn't always ~\"</b>. Everyone assumes \"it's always like this,\" and you step in with \"not always, there are exceptions.\" In English you'd say \"expensive doesn't always mean good.\" In Japanese that's <span class='gd-jp'>高いものがいいとは限らない。</span></div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Challenging a stereotype</span>You hit the brakes on an idea that \"everyone takes for granted.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本人がみんな寿司が好きだとは限らない。</span>(Not all Japanese people necessarily like sushi.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>先生の言うことがいつも正しいとは限らない。</span>(What the teacher says isn't always right.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Warning of a risk</span>Things aren't guaranteed to go as expected, so you prepare ahead.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>天気予報が当たるとは限らないから、傘を持っていこう。</span>(The weather forecast isn't always right, so let's take an umbrella.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a complete sentence in plain form (the form without です・ます) before it, then attach とは限らない directly.<br>Verb: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>成功する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>成功するとは限らない</span></span><br>い-adjective: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いとは限らない</span></span><br>な-adjective: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>便利だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>便利だとは限らない</span></span> (だ is often dropped: <span class='gd-jp'>便利とは限らない</span>)<br>Noun: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>本物だ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>本物だとは限らない</span></span> (you can also say <span class='gd-jp'>本物とは限らない</span>)<br>With seniors or in formal situations, use <span class='gd-jp'>とは限りません</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>必ずしも〜</span>Adding <span class='gd-jp'>必ずしも</span> (not necessarily) in front makes the \"not necessarily\" feel clearer. It's very common in written text.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>有名な大学を出た人が、必ずしも仕事ができるとは限らない。</span>(People who graduated from famous universities aren't necessarily good at their jobs.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>からといって〜</span>It means \"you can't say it's definitely ~ just because ~.\" It's most useful to memorize the whole phrase together.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>安いからといって、お得だとは限らない。</span>(Just because something is cheap doesn't mean it's a good deal.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>みんな・いつも・全て</span>Words meaning \"all\" or \"every time\" often appear before it, because とは限らない is made precisely to overturn a claim that \"everything is like this.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It means \"<b>not necessarily</b>,\" not \"<b>is not</b>.\" <span class='gd-jp'>高いものがいいとは限らない</span> only says that expensive things aren't guaranteed to be good. It doesn't say expensive things are bad.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>必ずしも</span> must be followed by a negative. You can't say 「必ずしもいい」. You have to say <span class='gd-jp'>必ずしもいいとは限らない</span>.<br>・Watch out for <span class='gd-jp'>〜ないとも限らない</span>, because the meaning flips: <span class='gd-jp'>事故が起きないとも限らない。</span> means \"we can't guarantee an accident won't happen,\" that is, <b>an accident could still happen</b>. It's used to urge caution.<br>・限る is read <b>かぎる</b>, so it's かぎらない. Don't read it as げんらない.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three talk about \"whether a place is crowded on weekends\":<br><b>に限らず</b> = not only ~ (a wider range): <span class='gd-jp'>この店は週末に限らず、いつも混んでいる。</span> (This shop is crowded not only on weekends but all the time.)<br><b>とは限らない</b> = not necessarily ~ (there are exceptions): <span class='gd-jp'>週末だからといって、混んでいるとは限らない。</span> (Even if it's the weekend, it's not necessarily crowded.)<br><b>に決まっている</b> = definitely ~ (you're very sure): <span class='gd-jp'>週末だから、混んでいるに決まっている。</span> (It's the weekend, so it's bound to be crowded.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>A quick rule of thumb: <b>noun + に限らず</b> = \"not only ~\"; <b>whole sentence + とは限らない</b> = \"not necessarily ~.\" To say \"definitely,\" use に決まっている. To say \"not necessarily,\" use とは限らない.</div>","n2-59":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「<span class='gd-jp'>〜をきっかけに</span>」means <b>\"prompted by ~, I started…\"</b> or <b>\"taking ~ as an opportunity, I…\"</b>. Before it you put the event that triggered you, and after it comes the <b>new action or change that began</b> as a result. For example: \"I started cooking for myself after coming to Japan,\" or \"I started paying attention to my health after getting sick.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本に来たのをきっかけに、料理を始めた。</span>(After coming to Japan, I started cooking for myself.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>入院をきっかけに、健康に気をつけるようになった。</span>(After being hospitalized, I started taking care of my health.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Noun</span>Attach it directly: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>留学</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>留学をきっかけに</span></span><br><span class='gd-tag'>Verb</span>First add「の」or「こと」to turn it into a noun-like event, then attach: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>子どもが生まれた</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>子どもが生まれたのをきっかけに</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>子どもが生まれたのをきっかけに、タバコをやめた。</span>(After my child was born, I quit smoking.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>It's often paired with expressions of \"starting\" or \"changing,\" like 「<span class='gd-jp'>始めた</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>ようになった</span>」「<span class='gd-jp'>やめた</span>」.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll usually hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>がきっかけで</span>This makes the triggering event the subject. It's very common in spoken Japanese.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>友達の一言がきっかけで、転職を考えるようになった。</span>(A single remark from a friend got me thinking about changing jobs.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>きっかけは〜</span>This is a question you'll be asked all the time in interviews and self-introductions.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本語を始めたきっかけは何ですか。</span>(What made you start learning Japanese?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>アニメがきっかけです。</span>(It was anime.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>をきっかけとして</span>The meaning is the same but it's more formal, and you see it more often in news and written text.</div><div class='gd-h'>Common pitfalls</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Before a verb you must add「の」or「こと」: say <span class='gd-jp'>日本に来たのをきっかけに</span>, and never just 「日本に来たをきっかけに」.<br>・「を」goes with「に」and「が」goes with「で」: <span class='gd-jp'>留学をきっかけに</span> / <span class='gd-jp'>留学がきっかけで</span>. Don't split these pairs and mix them up.<br>・It describes something that <i>triggers you to start something new</i>, not a plain cause. For a direct result like \"the trains stopped because of heavy rain,\" <span class='gd-jp'>大雨で電車が止まった</span> is enough, and using きっかけ would sound odd.<br>・Both good and bad events can go before it. Illness, a breakup, or being scolded can all be a きっかけ.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from similar patterns</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three are about the same event, \"moving house\":<br><b>をきっかけに</b> = moving <b>triggers</b> you to start something new:<br><span class='gd-jp'>引っ越しをきっかけに、ジョギングを始めた。</span> (Taking the move as a chance, I started jogging.)<br><b>て以来</b> = <b>ever since</b> then, a state has continued:<br><span class='gd-jp'>引っ越して以来、毎朝ジョギングしている。</span> (Ever since I moved, I've been jogging every morning.)<br><b>に伴って</b> = things that <b>naturally go along with</b> moving, not something you were inspired to do:<br><span class='gd-jp'>引っ越しに伴って、住所変更の手続きをした。</span> (Along with the move, I did the paperwork for the change of address.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>A quick rule of thumb: \"Because of that, I started ~\" is をきっかけに. \"Ever since then, I've kept ~\" is て以来. \"When that happens, ~ naturally follows\" is に伴って.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>More formal versions are 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜を機に</span>」and「<span class='gd-jp'>〜を契機に</span>」, which are very common in company announcements and the news: <span class='gd-jp'>結婚を機に、仕事を辞めた。</span> (Taking marriage as an opportunity, I quit my job.)</div>","n2-55":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にしろ／〜にせよ」means <b>\"whether A or B, ...\"</b> or <b>\"even if it's ..., still ...\"</b>. You first throw out a few possibilities, then state a conclusion or opinion that <b>doesn't change</b> because of them. The two forms mean the same thing, but <span class='gd-jp'>にせよ</span> is more formal and shows up more in writing and formal settings.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Whether A or B</span>You put two (or more) options side by side, and the conclusion is the same whichever one it is. This is the most common pattern.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>行くにしろ行かないにしろ、早めに連絡してください。</span>(Whether you go or not, please let me know early.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>肉にせよ魚にせよ、新鮮なものを選びたい。</span>(Whether it's meat or fish, I want to pick something fresh.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>No matter what</span>Put a question word like 「何」「どちら」「誰」 in front, and it means \"it's the same whichever one it is.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何をするにしろ、健康が一番大事だ。</span>(Whatever you do, health matters most.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>いずれにせよ、一度会って話しましょう。</span>(Either way, let's meet and talk once.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-jp'>いずれにせよ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>どっちにしろ</span> are almost set phrases meaning \"anyway\" or \"in any case.\" You'll hear them a lot in meetings and in everyday conversation.</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Even if ～</span>With just one situation, it means \"even if that's really the case, still ...\" It often carries a slightly disapproving or cautionary tone.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冗談にしろ、そんなことを言うべきではない。</span>(Even as a joke, you shouldn't say something like that.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>忙しいにせよ、電話ぐらいできたはずだ。</span>(Even if you were busy, you could at least have made a phone call.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Verbs and い-adjectives</b>: use the plain form (the form without ます; affirmative, negative and past are all fine) and attach it directly.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行く</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行くにしろ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>行かない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>行かないにしろ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>高い</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>高いにせよ</span></span></div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns and な-adjectives</b>: attach directly, or add 「である」 first (more formal). <b>You can't put だ in between</b>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>学生</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>学生にしろ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>不便</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>不便にせよ</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>不便</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>不便であるにせよ</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・After nouns and な-adjectives, <b>don't add だ</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>学生だにしろ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>不便だにせよ</span> are both wrong.<br>・With 「AにしろBにしろ」, A and B should be the same kind of thing, on equal footing: <span class='gd-jp'>賛成にしろ反対にしろ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>行くにしろ行かないにしろ</span>.<br>・The second half is usually your <b>opinion, request or advice</b>, with things like <span class='gd-jp'>べきだ</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>てください</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>ほうがいい</span>.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>しろ</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>せよ</span> are originally the imperative forms of <span class='gd-jp'>する</span>, but here they have <b>no commanding meaning at all</b>. <span class='gd-jp'>勉強しろ</span> means \"Go study!\", while <span class='gd-jp'>勉強にしろ遊びにしろ</span> means \"whether it's studying or playing.\" The difference is whether 「に」 is there.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にしても</b>: almost the same meaning as にしろ and にせよ; the difference is the level of formality.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>行くにしても行かないにしても、連絡してね。</span>(Whether you go or not, let me know, okay? — between friends)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>出席されるにせよされないにせよ、ご連絡ください。</span>(Whether you will attend or not, please contact us. — formal notice)<br>From casual to formal, the order is roughly: <span class='gd-jp'>にしても</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>にしろ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>にせよ</span>.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>を問わず</b>: \"regardless of, without distinction.\" It attaches only to nouns, often to a whole range like 「年齢」 or 「男女」, and is used in rules and job ads.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>年齢を問わず、誰でも参加できます。</span>(Regardless of age, anyone can take part.)<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>大人にしろ子どもにしろ、参加費は同じだ。</span>(Whether adult or child, the participation fee is the same.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: for a condition covering a whole range (\"regardless of ...\"), use を問わず. To list specific options one by one and then give a conclusion, use にしろ・にせよ. Chatting with friends, にしても sounds most natural; in writing and formal settings, use にせよ.</div>","n2-56":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜にほかならない」means <b>\"it is precisely ...\"</b>, <b>\"it is nothing other than ...\"</b>, or <b>\"when you get down to it, it's ...\"</b>. The speaker concludes with total confidence: this is the answer, <b>and nothing else</b>.<br>「ほか」means \"other,\" so 「ほかならない」 literally says \"it can't be anything other than this,\" which is how it comes to mean \"it is exactly this.\" The tone is stiff, and it mostly appears in writing, speeches and news commentary.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Declaring the essence</span>\"A is, at bottom, B\": you reveal what something really is.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この結果は、皆さんの協力の成果にほかならない。</span>(This result is nothing other than the fruit of everyone's cooperation.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の説明は、言い訳にほかならない。</span>(His explanation is, plainly speaking, just an excuse.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Declaring the cause</span>Use 「〜のは、〜からにほかならない」, meaning \"the reason that ... is nothing other than that ...\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼が合格できたのは、毎日努力したからにほかならない。</span>(The reason he passed is simply that he worked hard every day.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>事故が起きたのは、安全確認を怠ったからにほかならない。</span>(The accident happened for no other reason than that safety checks were neglected.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to connect it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Nouns</b>: attach directly. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>努力の結果</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>努力の結果にほかならない</span></span><br><b>Giving a cause</b>: plain form, add 「から」 first, then attach. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>努力した</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>努力したからにほかならない</span></span><br><b>Stating the essence of an action</b>: dictionary form of the verb plus 「こと」, which turns it into a noun, then attach. <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>逃げる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>逃げることにほかならない</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>何もしないのは、問題から逃げることにほかならない。</span>(Doing nothing is, in essence, running away from the problem.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>In speeches and formal settings, to be more polite, say <b>にほかなりません</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>Easy mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・When you see 「ならない」, don't assume it's a negative or \"must not.\" Here it's a <b>very strong affirmation</b>: \"it is exactly this.\"<br>・A verb can't attach directly: say <span class='gd-jp'>遅れたからにほかならない</span>, not 遅れたにほかならない.<br>・When the first half uses 「〜のは」 to ask \"why did this happen?\", the natural ending is 「〜からにほかならない」 or 「〜のおかげにほかならない」. Putting in just a noun easily makes the two halves not match up.<br>・Using it with friends sounds like you're reading out a thesis. In casual speech, 「〜からだよ」 or 「まさに〜だ」 is enough.<br>・It's a firm assertion, not a guess. Don't use it for things you're not sure about.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>にすぎない</b> = \"it's just ...\", with a feeling of <b>looking down on</b> something.<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼の成功は運がよかったからにすぎない。</span>(His success is only because he was lucky.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼の成功は努力の結果にほかならない。</span>(His success is nothing other than the result of hard work.)<br>The two look alike, but one belittles and the other draws a conclusion.</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>からこそ</b> = stresses \"<b>precisely because of</b> this reason, this result came about.\" It can go mid-sentence, or at the end as 「〜のは〜からこそだ」. It's also common in speech and often carries emotion.<br><span class='gd-jp'>努力したからこそ、合格できた。</span>(Precisely because I worked hard, I passed.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>合格できたのは、努力したからこそだ。</span>(The reason I could pass is precisely that I worked hard.)<br><span class='gd-jp'>合格できたのは、努力したからにほかならない。</span>(The reason I could pass is nothing other than that I worked hard.)<br>The last two mean nearly the same thing; the difference is in tone. からこそ <b>emphasizes that reason</b>, while にほかならない <b>declares \"it's nothing else\"</b>, which is stiffer and more written, like drawing a conclusion in a report.</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to say \"it's just ...\" with a bit of disdain, use にすぎない. To stress \"precisely because ... , ...\", use からこそ. To confidently conclude \"it's nothing else, it's ...\", use にほかならない.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>Since this is also a very confident tone, try comparing <span class='gd-jp'>〜に違いない</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>〜に相違ない</span>. They express a strong inference, \"it must be ...\", whereas にほかならない directly asserts \"it is ...\".</div>","n2-60":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜あげく」 works like English <b>“after all that fuss, in the end…” or “after going through all that, it turned out…”</b>. What comes before is a long, tiring process, and what follows is usually a disappointing result nobody wanted. The speaker often sounds a little annoyed, or like it was all for nothing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>さんざん待たされたあげく、満席だと言われた。</span>（After being left waiting forever, I was told they were full）</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs take the <b>た form</b> (the past-tense form), followed by <span class='gd-jp'>あげく</span>:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>迷う</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>迷ったあげく</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>悩む</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>悩んだあげく</span></span><br>Nouns take 「の」 first:<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>議論</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>議論のあげく</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>けんか</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>けんかのあげく</span></span><br>You can add 「に」 after it or leave it off; the meaning is the same. It can also be written with kanji as <span class='gd-jp'>挙げ句</span>, so recognize that too.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll most often hear it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>All for nothing</span>Before it you'll often see <span class='gd-jp'>さんざん</span> (thoroughly, relentlessly) or <span class='gd-jp'>何度も</span> (many times), and after it often <span class='gd-jp'>結局</span> (in the end).</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>さんざん迷ったあげく、結局何も買わなかった。</span>（After agonizing for ages, I ended up buying nothing）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>長時間の議論のあげく、何も決まらなかった。</span>（After a long discussion, nothing got decided）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>A reluctant decision</span>You thought about it for a long time and finally had to settle on something.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>悩んだあげく、旅行には行かないことにした。</span>（After agonizing over it, I decided not to go on the trip）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>あげくの果てに</span>A fixed phrase meaning “and in the end, he even…”. It's used when things keep getting worse until they reach the most extreme point.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は遅刻を繰り返し、あげくの果てに無断で休んだ。</span>（He kept showing up late, and in the end he even stayed away without telling anyone）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Verbs must be in the た form: <span class='gd-jp'>迷うあげく</span> is wrong; say <span class='gd-jp'>迷ったあげく</span>.<br>・What comes before must be a process that <b>drags on or repeats</b>. Actions that are over in a moment don't work: <span class='gd-jp'>ドアを開けたあげく</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>一度電話したあげく</span> both sound odd. Something like <span class='gd-jp'>何度も電話したあげく、やっと通じたと思ったら留守電だった。</span> (I called again and again, and just when I thought I'd finally gotten through, it was voicemail) sounds natural.<br>・What follows is <b>an outcome that has already happened</b>, so the sentence usually ends in the past tense. You can't follow it with things that haven't happened yet, like “let's ~” or “please ~”.<br>・Don't use it when the result is good: <span class='gd-jp'>努力したあげく合格した</span> sounds odd. Switch to <span class='gd-jp'>末に</span>, covered below.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from a similar pattern</div><div class='gd-sec'><b><span class='gd-jp'>末に</span></b> also means “after going through ~, finally…”, and it's formed the same way (た form / noun + の + <span class='gd-jp'>末に</span>). The difference is that <span class='gd-jp'>末に</span> is <b>more neutral</b>. It works with good or bad results and is very common in news and formal writing. <span class='gd-jp'>あげく</span> carries a grumbling “after all that, it still ended up like this” feel and almost only takes bad results.<br>Here's the same long meeting both ways:<br><span class='gd-jp'>何度も話し合った末に、新しい計画が決まった。</span>（After discussing it many times, we finally settled on a new plan: a good result, so only <span class='gd-jp'>末に</span> works）<br><span class='gd-jp'>何度も話し合ったあげく、結局何も決まらなかった。</span>（We discussed it many times, and still nothing got decided: a grumbling tone, so <span class='gd-jp'>あげく</span> fits best）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: if the result is good or you're simply stating facts, use <span class='gd-jp'>末に</span>. If you want to grumble “after all that, this is what we got…”, use <span class='gd-jp'>あげく</span>.</div>","n2-61":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜てはじめて」 works like English <b>“only after ~ did I realize/understand…” or “not until ~ did…”</b>. Think of the saying “you don't know what you've got till it's gone”: the first half is something you <b>experienced firsthand</b>, and the second half is what you <b>only then understood, or felt for the first time</b>. The key point is that before that, you really didn't get it.</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the て form of a verb and add 「はじめて」 directly (it's also often written with kanji as 「初めて」).<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>なる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なって</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>なってはじめて</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>して</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>してはじめて</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>言われる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言われて</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>言われてはじめて</span></span><br>You usually put a comma after it, then the thing you only realized later.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Understanding only after experiencing</span>It's most often followed by words of understanding or discovery, such as <span class='gd-jp'>わかる</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>気づく</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>知る</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>実感する</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>親になってはじめて、親の気持ちがわかった。</span>（Only after becoming a parent myself did I understand how my parents felt）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>一人暮らしをしてはじめて、家事の大変さに気づいた。</span>（Only after living on my own did I realize how hard housework is）</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>言われてはじめて気づきました。</span>（I only noticed when someone pointed it out）</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>General truths</span>Use the present tense to say “you only really ~ once you've ~”.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>病気になってはじめて、健康のありがたさがわかる。</span>（You only learn how precious health is once you fall ill）</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・The second half <b>can't be your own intention, a command, or a request</b>.<br>×<span class='gd-jp'>日本に来てはじめて、日本語を勉強しよう。</span><br>What follows should be a realization or change that has already happened, not “what I'm going to do”.<br>・Don't confuse it with verb ます-stem + <span class='gd-jp'>はじめる</span> (start ~): <span class='gd-jp'>食べはじめる</span> means “start eating”, while <span class='gd-jp'>食べてはじめて</span> means “only after eating…”. The difference is whether there's a 「て」 in the middle.<br>・This pattern carries a feeling of “I didn't know until then”, so it sounds odd when used for things you already knew or that are taken for granted.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from a similar pattern</div><div class='gd-sec'>Both express “you only know by doing it”, but compare the two:<br><b>てはじめて</b> = looking back: <b>you understood only after doing it</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>実際に使ってみてはじめて、よさがわかった。</span>（Only after actually using it did I see how good it was）<br><b>ないことには</b> = a condition looking forward: <b>without doing it first, you can't</b>, and what follows is usually negative: <span class='gd-jp'>実際に使ってみないことには、よさはわからない。</span>（Unless you actually try it, you won't know how good it is）</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule of thumb: to say “it only clicked after I went through it”, use てはじめて. To say “if you don't do ~ first, you can't…”, use ないことには.</div>","n2-63":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜ものがある」 means <b>“there's something ~ about it” or “it really strikes you as ~.”</b> Say you hear a performance or look at an old photo. Something moves you, but you can't quite say what. Japanese uses this pattern for that feeling: <span class='gd-jp'>彼の演奏には心を打つものがある。</span> (There's something moving about his playing.)<br>The <span class='gd-jp'>もの</span> here isn't a physical object. It's <b>a quality or feeling that's hard to put into words</b>.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Evaluating people or things</span>Use it to praise works, performances and abilities. It sounds more thoughtful and measured than just saying “that's great.” You'll often hear it in film reviews, the news and feedback from a boss.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の演奏には人を引き込むものがある。</span>(There's something captivating about his playing.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新人だが、彼女の企画には光るものがある。</span>(She's new, but there's something that really stands out in her proposal.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Talking about your own feelings</span>Something gives you a certain feeling, good or bad.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この古い写真にはどこか懐かしいものがある。</span>(There's something nostalgic about this old photo.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>この年で徹夜するのは、つらいものがある。</span>(At my age, pulling an all-nighter is really rough.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Verbs: plain dictionary form　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>心を打つ</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>心を打つものがある</span></span><br>い-adjectives: attach directly　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>懐かしい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>懐かしいものがある</span></span><br>な-adjectives: add 「な」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>見事</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>見事なものがある</span></span><br>Negatives work too: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>納得できない</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>納得できないものがある</span></span> (it's a bit hard to accept)<br>The subject is often introduced with 「<span class='gd-jp'>〜には</span>」: <span class='gd-jp'>この映画には</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>彼の言葉には</span>……</div><div class='gd-h'>The phrases you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'>These are nearly fixed collocations, so memorizing them as chunks is fastest:<br><span class='gd-jp'>心を打つものがある</span> (it's moving)<br><span class='gd-jp'>胸に迫るものがある</span> (it deeply touches you)<br><span class='gd-jp'>目を見張るものがある</span> (it's remarkable)<br><span class='gd-jp'>光るものがある</span> (it has something that shines)<br><span class='gd-jp'>見るべきものがある</span> (it's worth seeing)<br><span class='gd-jp'>感慨深いものがある</span> (it stirs deep emotion)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・<b>Not every 「ものがある」 is this grammar point</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>この店にはおいしいものがある。</span> simply means “this restaurant has some tasty things,” meaning actual food. In the grammar version, the subject is usually something abstract, like a work, a performance or an experience.<br>・<b>Put the past tense on ある</b>: to say “it moved me at the time,” use <span class='gd-jp'>心を打つものがあった</span>. Don't write 心を打ったものがある.<br>・<b>Don't forget the な after な-adjectives</b>: it's <span class='gd-jp'>見事なものがある</span>, not 見事ものがある, and you can't attach it directly to a noun either.<br>・<b>Don't use it for objective facts</b>: you can measure how big a room is, so just say <span class='gd-jp'>この部屋は広い</span>. Saying 「この部屋には広いものがある」 sounds odd. This pattern is for qualities that make you feel something.<br>・It's fairly formal, but it shows up in casual speech too, like <span class='gd-jp'>それはちょっとつらいものがあるね</span> (that's honestly kind of rough, isn't it).</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>ものだ (exclamation)</b> = “isn't it ~!”, a remark about <b>life or things in general</b>:<br><span class='gd-jp'>子どもの成長は早いものだ。</span> (Children grow up so fast.)<br><b>ものがある</b> = pointing at <b>one specific person or thing</b> and saying it has a certain quality:<br><span class='gd-jp'>彼の成長ぶりには目を見張るものがある。</span> (His progress is truly remarkable.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if you're sighing “that's just how life and the world are,” use ものだ. If you're pointing at this person or this work in front of you and saying “there's something ~ about it,” use ものがある.</div>","n2-64":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜抜き」 is like ordering a drink “no ice” or noodles “hold the cilantro”: you <b>take out something that would normally be there</b>. At a sushi restaurant in Japan, <span class='gd-jp'>わさび抜きでお願いします</span> means “no wasabi, please.” It always follows a <b>noun</b> and is usually written with the kanji <span class='gd-jp'>抜き</span>, read ぬき.</div><div class='gd-h'>The forms you'll hear most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>抜きで＋action</span>Doing something without a certain thing.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>わさび抜きでお願いします。</span>(No wasabi, please.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今朝は朝ごはん抜きで会社に行った。</span>(I went to work this morning without breakfast.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>抜きの＋noun</span>Followed by a noun, it means “the ~ with that thing left out.”</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>玉ねぎ抜きのハンバーガーをください。</span>(I'd like a hamburger without onions, please.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>冗談抜きで</span>A set phrase meaning “seriously” or “joking aside.” It's very common in casual speech.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>冗談抜きで、このラーメンは日本一だと思う。</span>(Seriously, I think this ramen is the best in Japan.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>抜きには〜ない</span>“Without ~, you can't ~.” It stresses how important that thing is.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼の協力抜きには、この仕事は終わらなかった。</span>(Without his help, this job never would have been finished.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>日本の夏は花火抜きには語れない。</span>(You can't talk about Japanese summer without mentioning fireworks.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>は抜きにして</span>“Let's skip ~” or “setting ~ aside.” You hear it a lot at the start of dinners and meetings.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>堅苦しい挨拶は抜きにして、乾杯しましょう。</span>(Let's skip the formal greetings and have a toast.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・It only attaches to <b>nouns</b>. You can't say 食べ抜きで for “without eating breakfast.” Say <span class='gd-jp'>朝ごはん抜きで</span>, or switch to a verb: <span class='gd-jp'>朝ごはんを食べないで</span>.<br>・抜き carries the feeling of “it would normally be there, but it was deliberately removed.” If the thing wouldn't necessarily be there anyway, such as going out without an umbrella, <span class='gd-jp'>傘なしで出かけた</span> sounds more natural.<br>・「抜きには」 is almost always followed by a negative or “can't do” meaning, like <span class='gd-jp'>できない</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>語れない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>考えられない</span>. Don't follow it with an ordinary affirmative sentence.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>冗談抜きで</span> is mostly used by the speaker to stress “I'm being serious now.” It isn't a way to tell the other person “don't joke.” You can also say <span class='gd-jp'>冗談抜きに</span>, with the same meaning.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>抜きには〜ない</b> and <b>ないことには</b> (n2-19) both mean “without ~, you can't ~.” The difference is what comes before them:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長の許可抜きには決められない。</span> (We can't decide without the president's permission.): a noun before it<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>社長が許可しないことには決められない。</span> (We can't decide unless the president approves.): a verb before it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>抜きで</b> and <b>ことなく</b> (n2-48) can both be translated as “without doing ~”:<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>休憩抜きで働いた。</span> (I worked with no breaks.): the noun “break time” is taken out<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>休むことなく働いた。</span> (I worked without resting.): a verb comes before it, and the tone is more formal</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if a <b>noun</b> comes before it, use 抜き. If a <b>verb</b> comes before it, switch to ないことには or ことなく.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>抜き comes from the verb <span class='gd-jp'>抜く</span> (to take out, to skip), and in daily life people often use the verb directly: <span class='gd-jp'>忙しくて昼ごはんを抜いた。</span> (I was so busy I skipped lunch.)</div>","n2-65":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜きる」means <b>\"all the way through,\" \"completely,\" \"to the very end\"</b>: run the whole course, spend every last yen, be totally worn out.<br>「〜きれる」means <b>can finish / can do it all</b>, and 「〜きれない」means <b>can't finish / can't do it all</b>. The point is always \"all of it, thoroughly, nothing left over.\"</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Take the verb's ます form, drop ます, and add <span class='gd-jp'>きる</span>.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>走ります</span> → drop ます → <span class='gd-jp'>走りきる</span></span><br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>食べます</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>食べきる</span></span><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>使います</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>使いきる</span></span><br><span class='gd-jp'>する</span> shows up in verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>説明する</span>: <span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>説明します</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>説明しきる</span></span><br>Once attached, <span class='gd-jp'>きる</span> itself conjugates as a Group 1 (godan) verb: <span class='gd-jp'>きった</span> (finished ~ing), <span class='gd-jp'>きれる</span> (can finish), <span class='gd-jp'>きれない</span> (can't finish).</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Doing it to the end, finishing it all</span>You didn't give up halfway, or nothing is left.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>フルマラソンを走りきった。</span>(I ran the whole marathon.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>給料を三日で使いきってしまった。</span>(I spent my entire paycheck in three days.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Reaching the extreme</span>After state verbs like <span class='gd-jp'>疲れる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>困る</span>, it means \"completely ~, ~ to the limit.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>残業続きで、疲れきっている。</span>(Overtime non-stop, and I'm completely exhausted.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>そんな分かりきったこと、聞かないでよ。</span>(Don't ask something that obvious, come on.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>Speaking with total certainty</span><span class='gd-jp'>言いきる</span> = to declare flatly, saying it so firmly there's no way back.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼は「絶対に勝つ」と言いきった。</span>(He declared flatly, \"We'll definitely win.\")</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>きれる／きれない</span>Asking whether you can finish it, or saying there's too much to finish.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こんなにたくさん、一人で食べきれる？</span>(That much food. Can you eat it all by yourself?)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>空に数えきれないほど星が出ている。</span>(There are more stars in the sky than you could count.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>明日からの旅行が待ちきれない。</span>(I can't wait for the trip starting tomorrow.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Attach it to the ます stem with ます dropped, not the dictionary form: it's <span class='gd-jp'>食べきる</span>, not 「食べるきる」.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>食べきれない</span> specifically means \"there's too much to eat it all.\" <span class='gd-jp'>食べられない</span> is broader: it can mean \"can't eat it\" (allergy, can't stomach it) or \"can't eat any more\" (<span class='gd-jp'>もうお腹いっぱいで食べられない</span>). To say clearly \"too much, I can't finish it,\" <span class='gd-jp'>食べきれない</span> is the safest choice.<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>待ちきれない</span> means \"can't wait (too eager),\" not \"can't finish waiting.\"<br>・<span class='gd-jp'>疲れきる</span> does not mean \"finished being tired\" or \"no longer tired.\" It means \"tired to the extreme,\" so don't read きる as \"finished\" here.<br>・Writing it with the kanji <span class='gd-jp'>切る</span> (<span class='gd-jp'>食べ切る</span>) is also fine, but it's not the <span class='gd-jp'>着る</span> of wearing clothes.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜終わる</b> = the action <b>has ended</b>; whether you did all of it isn't the point:<br></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>もうご飯食べ終わった？</span>(Have you finished eating yet? This asks whether the meal is over.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>〜きる</b> = <b>all of it, nothing left</b>, often with a sense of \"there was a lot\" or \"it wasn't easy\":</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>こんなに多いのに、全部食べきったの？</span>(There was this much, and you ate it all?)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: to say \"I'm done, it's over,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>終わる</span>. To stress \"ate it all, stuck it out to the end, nothing left,\" use <span class='gd-jp'>きる</span>.</div><div class='gd-h'>What to learn next</div><div class='gd-sec'>The opposite is <span class='gd-jp'>〜かける</span> (halfway done): <span class='gd-jp'>食べかけのパン</span> is \"half-eaten bread,\" while <span class='gd-jp'>食べきる</span> is \"eat it all up.\" Learn them as a pair and they're easy to tell apart.</div>","n2-66":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>「〜に先立って」is the formal way to say <b>\"before ~, first ~\"</b> (\"prior to,\" \"ahead of\"). Think of an emcee saying \"Before the ceremony begins, we'll first hear from the organizers,\" or a news report saying \"Ahead of the product launch, a press conference was held.\" It's that formal kind of \"before.\" You wouldn't use it for everyday things like washing your hands before a meal.</div><div class='gd-h'>When to use it</div><div class='gd-sec'>Put a <b>formal, planned major event</b> first (a meeting, construction, a launch, a departure, a ceremony), then say <b>what was done beforehand: a preparation, an announcement, or a ritual</b>. It's most common in news, notices, speeches, and business documents.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>新商品の発売に先立って、記者会見が開かれた。</span>(Ahead of the new product's launch, a press conference was held.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>出発に先立ち、参加者に注意事項が説明された。</span>(Before departure, the participants were briefed on the precautions.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>Noun + に先立って／に先立ち</b>: the most common pattern. The noun is usually one you can add する to, like 「開会、工事、発売」.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>工事</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>工事に先立って</span></span><br><b>Verb dictionary form + に先立って</b>: also possible, but less common.<br><span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>留学する</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>留学するに先立って</span></span></div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>留学するに先立って、ビザの手続きを済ませた。</span>(Before going to study abroad, I took care of the visa procedures.)</div><div class='gd-sec'>「に先立ち」is more written and a bit stiffer than「に先立って」, but the meaning is the same.</div><div class='gd-h'>How you'll hear it most</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>に先立ちまして</span>A set phrase for opening speeches and ceremonies; it's more polite.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>開会に先立ちまして、一言ご挨拶申し上げます。</span>(Before we open the meeting, allow me to say a few words.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>に先立つ + noun</span>It modifies the following noun directly, meaning \"the ~ held before ~.\"</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>試合に先立つ開会式で、選手代表が宣誓した。</span>(At the opening ceremony before the match, the players' representative took the oath.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・Attach に directly to the noun, with no の: it's <span class='gd-jp'>開会に先立って</span>, not 開会の先立って.<br>・The verb must be in the dictionary form, never the た form: you can't say 「決めたに先立って」.<br>・The reading is <b>さきだって</b>, not せんだって.<br>・Don't use it for small everyday things; it sounds absurdly grand. \"Brushing your teeth before bed\" is <span class='gd-jp'>寝る前に歯を磨く</span>; saying 「寝るに先立って」 sounds like you're issuing a press release.</div><div class='gd-h'>Telling it apart from look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'><b>前に</b> = the general \"before,\" usable in any situation: <span class='gd-jp'>会議の前に、資料を確認しておこう。</span> (Let's look over the materials before the meeting.)<br><b>に先立って</b> = formal \"in preparation for that big event, beforehand ~\": <span class='gd-jp'>会議に先立ち、資料が配布された。</span> (Before the meeting began, materials were distributed.)<br><b>際に</b> = \"when ~,\" referring to the moment itself, not before: <span class='gd-jp'>ご使用の際は、十分ご注意ください。</span> (Please be careful when using it.) / <span class='gd-jp'>ご使用に先立ち、説明書をお読みください。</span> (Before use, please read the instructions.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick rule: for everyday \"before,\" use 前に. For the formal \"before a major event begins, prepare first,\" use に先立って. For \"at the moment of doing it,\" use 際に.</div>","n2-67":"<div class='gd-h'>The idea in one line</div><div class='gd-sec'>All three of these mean something like <b>\"so ... I can't stand it\"</b> or <b>\"I'm dying to ...\"</b>. Use them when a feeling, emotional or physical, is too strong to hold down. The meanings are almost the same. The main difference is <b>how formal they sound</b>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>合格の知らせを聞いて、嬉しくてたまらない。</span>(Hearing that I passed, I'm unbelievably happy.)</div><div class='gd-h'>How to form it</div><div class='gd-sec'>First put the word into its「て」form, then add <span class='gd-jp'>たまらない</span> / <span class='gd-jp'>ならない</span> / <span class='gd-jp'>しょうがない</span>.<br>Verbs: use the て-form　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>気になる</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>気になってしょうがない</span></span><br>い-adjectives: replace the final「い」with「くて」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>嬉しい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>嬉しくてたまらない</span></span><br>な-adjectives: add「で」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>心配</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>心配でたまらない</span></span><br>Wanting to do something: <span class='gd-jp'>たい</span> also changes「い」to「くて」　<span class='gd-pair'><span class='gd-jp'>会いたい</span> → <span class='gd-jp'>会いたくてたまらない</span></span></div><div class='gd-h'>Choosing among the three</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>たまらない</span>The most standard one. It works with emotions, physical sensations and wants, and you can use it in everyday speech.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>国の家族に会いたくてたまらない。</span>(I miss my family back home so much.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>今日は暑くてたまらない。</span>(It's unbearably hot today.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>しょうがない</span>The most colloquial one, and the one you'll hear most when chatting with friends. A slightly more formal version is <span class='gd-jp'>しかたがない</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>昨日あまり寝てないから、眠くてしょうがない。</span>(I barely slept last night, so I'm dead tired.)</div><div class='gd-sec'><span class='gd-tag'>ならない</span>More written and formal. It carries the sense of a feeling that keeps welling up in you on its own. It's most often paired with expressions where a feeling \"arises by itself\", such as <span class='gd-jp'>気がする</span>, <span class='gd-jp'>思われる</span> and <span class='gd-jp'>思い出される</span>.</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>彼が何か隠している気がしてならない。</span>(I can't shake the feeling that he's hiding something.)</div><div class='gd-ex-line'>・<span class='gd-jp'>故郷のことが思い出されてならない。</span>(I can't stop thinking about my hometown.)</div><div class='gd-h'>Common mistakes</div><div class='gd-warn'>・This describes <b>your own</b> feelings. To talk about someone else, add <span class='gd-jp'>ようだ</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>らしい</span>: <span class='gd-jp'>妹は寂しくてたまらないようだ。</span>(My little sister seems to be terribly lonely.)<br>・Only feelings and emotions can go before it, not an action you decide to do. You can't say \"I'm studying like crazy\" with <span class='gd-jp'>勉強してたまらない</span>. To say you want to do something, use <span class='gd-jp'>〜たくて</span>.<br>・Don't confuse <span class='gd-jp'>てならない</span> with <span class='gd-jp'>てはならない</span>. With an extra「は」it means \"must not\": <span class='gd-jp'>嘘をついてはならない。</span>(You must not lie.) <span class='gd-jp'>なくてはならない</span>, on the other hand, means \"must / have to\".<br>・Don't confuse <span class='gd-jp'>てしょうがない</span> with <span class='gd-jp'>てもしょうがない</span>. With an extra「も」it means \"it's no use even if ...\": <span class='gd-jp'>今さら後悔してもしょうがない。</span>(It's no use regretting it now.)<br>・In formal writing or speeches, <span class='gd-jp'>しょうがない</span> is too casual. Use <span class='gd-jp'>ならない</span> or <span class='gd-jp'>しかたがない</span> instead.</div><div class='gd-h'>Don't confuse it with look-alikes</div><div class='gd-sec'>English often renders these as \"can't help ...\", so it's easy to mix them up with <b>ずにはいられない</b>.<br><b>てたまらない</b> = a feeling so strong you can't stand it. The focus is on <b>the feeling itself</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>母の病気が心配でたまらない。</span>(I'm worried sick about my mother's illness.)<br><b>ずにはいられない</b> = you can't help <b>doing an action</b>: <span class='gd-jp'>心配で、母に電話せずにはいられなかった。</span>(I was so worried that I couldn't help calling my mother.)</div><div class='gd-warn'>Quick test: if what you can't hold back is a feeling, use てたまらない. If it's doing something, use ずにはいられない.</div>"});
